-- Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab) textbook MCQs, chapter 1 (Biodiversity and Classification) BIO-0679 to BIO-0759, chapter 2 (Bacteria and Viruses) BIO-0760 to BIO-0823, chapter 3 (Cells and Subcellular Organelles) BIO-0824 to BIO-0915, chapter 4 (Biomolecules) BIO-0916 to BIO-0998, chapter 5 (Enzymes) BIO-0999 to BIO-1046, chapter 6 (Bioenergetics) BIO-1047 to BIO-1154, chapter 7 (Structural and Computational Biology) BIO-1155 to BIO-1185, chapter 8 (Plant Physiology) BIO-1186 to BIO-1280, chapter 9 (Human Digestive System) BIO-1281 to BIO-1341, chapter 10 (Human Respiratory System) BIO-1342 to BIO-1399, chapter 11 (Human Circulatory System) BIO-1400 to BIO-1468, chapter 12 (Human Skeletal and Muscular Systems) BIO-1469 to BIO-1534.
-- Original questions in the bank's template (stem, four options, key, why the key is right, why each other option is
-- wrong) with the source page in the textbook. Approved for the bank by the site owner, so they go straight to live.
-- Not in the free set. INSERT OR IGNORE skips rows already there; if the site owner's admin account doesn't exist
-- (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted.

-- Syllabus check, question by question (the topic decides, not just the chapter):
-- * PMDC MDCAT 2025 uniform curriculum (26-05-2025). On it: viruses (Acellular life), cell structure, biological
--   molecules, enzymes, respiration and ATP (Bioenergetics), and the human digestive, respiratory, circulatory and
--   support/movement units (Human physiology). Off it, so questions.mdcat = 0: classification and biodiversity, most of
--   bacteriology, cell theory/microscopy/signalling/membrane transport/stem cells, photosynthesis, photorespiration,
--   structural and computational biology, plant physiology, ECG, blood pressure regulation, and the disorders that
--   PMDC does not list (arthritis and smoking-related lung disease stay on).
-- * NUMS MDCAT 2026 syllabus: its own 13 units, so NUMS rows are listed separately and use the NUMS chapter names.
--   NUMS adds photosynthesis, bacteria (Prokaryotes), animal phyla (Diversity among animals), carnivorous plants and
--   water/food transport in plants (Life processes in animals and plants); it has no photorespiration, plant
--   hormones, ECG, blood pressure or disorders other than arthritis.
-- * AKU-EB HSSC I Biology: covers the whole book, so every question goes in the AKU bank. New AKU chapters:
--   Biodiversity and classification, Bacteria, Plant physiology.
-- The questions table chapter is the MDCAT chapter, or the AKU chapter for questions with mdcat = 0.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0679', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 1 (§1.1)', 'Who proposed the five-kingdom system of classification in 1969?', '["Robert Whittaker", "Carl Woese", "Ernst Mayr", "Carolus Linnaeus"]', 0, 'The book credits the American ecologist Robert Whittaker with the five-kingdom system in 1969.', '{"1": "Carl Woese proposed the three-domain system in 1990.", "2": "Ernst Mayr is linked with the biological species concept.", "3": "Linnaeus is not the source of the five-kingdom system in this chapter."}'),
  ('BIO-0680', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 1 (§1.1)', 'In 1990, Carl Woese divided living organisms into three domains because he recognised that', '["fungi and plants are one group", "viruses form a separate domain", "protists and animals cannot be separated", "prokaryotes form two separate groups, Archaea and Bacteria"]', 3, 'Woese suggested that prokaryotes are of two separate kinds, Archaea and Bacteria; with Eukarya this gives three domains.', '{"0": "Fungi and plants remain separate kingdoms of Eukarya.", "1": "Viruses are not placed in any domain.", "2": "Protists and animals are separate kingdoms within Eukarya."}'),
  ('BIO-0681', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 1 (§1.1)', 'In the five-kingdom system, all prokaryotes were placed in the kingdom', '["Protista", "Fungi", "Monera", "Archaea"]', 2, 'Whittaker''s kingdom Monera contained the prokaryotes; the other four kingdoms contained eukaryotes.', '{"0": "Protista contains eukaryotes.", "1": "Fungi are eukaryotes.", "3": "Archaea is a domain in the later three-domain system, not a Whittaker kingdom."}'),
  ('BIO-0682', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 1 (§1.1)', 'A diagram that shows the evolutionary relationships among organisms is called a', '["taxonomic key", "phylogenetic tree", "food web", "karyotype"]', 1, 'Evolutionary relationship among organisms is phylogeny, and the diagram showing it is a phylogenetic (evolutionary) tree.', '{"0": "A taxonomic key is used for identification, not to show ancestry.", "2": "A food web shows feeding links.", "3": "A karyotype shows an organism''s chromosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0683', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'According to molecular evidence described in the book, archaea are', '["more closely related to bacteria than to eukaryotes", "the direct ancestors of bacteria", "a group of eukaryotes that lost their nucleus", "more closely related to eukaryotes than to bacteria"]', 3, 'Molecular evidence suggests Eukarya evolved from Archaea after archaea split off from Bacteria, so archaea are closer to eukaryotes.', '{"0": "This is the reverse of what the evidence shows.", "1": "Archaea and Bacteria evolved independently from a common ancestor.", "2": "Archaea are prokaryotes, not reduced eukaryotes."}'),
  ('BIO-0684', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'The name Archaea comes from a Greek word meaning', '["ancient", "extreme", "small", "hidden"]', 0, 'Archaea comes from the Greek archaios, "ancient"; they diverged from bacteria in very ancient times.', '{"1": "\"Extreme\" describes some habitats of archaea, not the meaning of the name.", "2": "Size is not the meaning of the name.", "3": "\"Hidden\" is not the meaning of archaios."}'),
  ('BIO-0685', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'Which of these processes does NOT occur in archaea?', '["Binary fission", "Meiosis", "Fragmentation", "Budding"]', 1, 'Archaea reproduce asexually by binary or multiple fission, fragmentation or budding; mitosis and meiosis do not occur in them.', '{"0": "Binary fission is one of their methods of reproduction.", "2": "Fragmentation is listed as a method of reproduction.", "3": "Budding is listed as a method of reproduction."}'),
  ('BIO-0686', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'The membrane lipids of archaea differ from those of bacteria and eukaryotes in having', '["unbranched chains joined to glycerol by ester linkages", "no glycerol at all", "branched chains joined to glycerol by ether linkages", "cholesterol in place of fatty acids"]', 2, 'Archaeal membrane lipids have ether linkages to glycerol and branched chains, which makes the membranes more resistant to extreme conditions.', '{"0": "Unbranched chains with ester linkages describe bacteria and eukaryotes.", "1": "Glycerol is present in archaeal membrane lipids.", "3": "The book does not describe cholesterol replacing fatty acids."}'),
  ('BIO-0687', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'Some archaea have a cell wall made of', '["peptidoglycan", "cellulose", "pseudopeptidoglycan", "chitin"]', 2, 'Archaeal walls lack cellulose and peptidoglycan; they contain distinct polysaccharides and proteins, and some have pseudopeptidoglycan.', '{"0": "Peptidoglycan is the wall polymer of bacteria.", "1": "Cellulose is found in plant cell walls.", "3": "Chitin is found in fungal cell walls."}'),
  ('BIO-0688', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 3 (§1.1)', 'Which metabolic process is found in archaea but not in bacteria or eukaryotes?', '["Photosynthesis", "Nitrogen fixation", "Fermentation", "Methanogenesis"]', 3, 'Archaea have unique pathways such as methanogenesis (production of methane), not found in Bacteria or Eukarya.', '{"0": "Photosynthesis is carried out by some bacteria and by plants and algae.", "1": "Nitrogen fixation is carried out by bacteria.", "2": "Fermentation occurs in bacteria and eukaryotes too."}'),
  ('BIO-0689', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 3 (§1.1)', 'Enzymes extracted from some archaeans are useful in the laboratory because they', '["work only at low temperatures", "work best at high temperatures and allow rapid copying of DNA", "digest cellulose rapidly", "destroy bacterial plasmids"]', 1, 'Archaeal DNA-replication enzymes work best at high temperatures and allow rapid cloning of DNA in the laboratory.', '{"0": "Their value is that they withstand harsh conditions such as heat.", "2": "Cellulose digestion is not the use described.", "3": "Destroying plasmids is not the use described."}'),
  ('BIO-0690', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 3 (§1.1)', 'The group of archaea that live in extremely saline environments is', '["Halobacteria", "Methanogens", "Thermococci", "Thaumarchaeota"]', 0, 'Halobacteria live in extremely saline environments.', '{"1": "Methanogens produce methane as a by-product.", "2": "Thermococci are found in hot environments.", "3": "Thaumarchaeota are involved in nitrogen cycling."}'),
  ('BIO-0691', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 3 (§1.1)', 'Acidophilic archaea are used in mineral processing to', '["produce methane from sewage", "extract metals such as gold, cobalt and copper from ores", "make penicillin", "fix nitrogen in soil"]', 1, 'The book says acidophilic Archaea are used to extract metals such as gold, cobalt and copper from ores.', '{"0": "Biogas from sewage comes from methanogens.", "2": "Penicillin comes from the fungus Penicillium.", "3": "Nitrogen fixation is not the mineral-processing use."}'),
  ('BIO-0692', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 3 (§1.1)', 'In humans, intestinal gas is largely produced by the metabolism of', '["halobacteria", "cyanobacteria", "methanogens", "thermococci"]', 2, 'The book notes that intestinal gas in humans is largely the result of methanogen metabolism.', '{"0": "Halobacteria live in very salty places.", "1": "Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic bacteria.", "3": "Thermococci live in hot environments."}'),
  ('BIO-0693', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 4 (§1.1)', 'Comma-shaped bacteria are called', '["vibrios", "bacilli", "cocci", "spirilla"]', 0, 'Vibrios are comma-shaped; cocci are spherical, bacilli rod-shaped and spirilla spiral.', '{"1": "Bacilli are rod-shaped.", "2": "Cocci are spherical.", "3": "Spirilla are spiral-shaped."}'),
  ('BIO-0694', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 4 (§1.1)', 'Spherical bacteria that occur in clusters are named', '["streptococci", "diplococci", "spirilla", "staphylococci"]', 3, 'Cells in clusters are staphylococci; in chains, streptococci; in pairs, diplococci.', '{"0": "Streptococci are arranged in chains.", "1": "Diplococci occur in pairs.", "2": "Spirilla are spiral rods, not an arrangement of cocci."}'),
  ('BIO-0695', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 4 (§1.1)', 'Which pair is correctly matched with its major group of bacteria?', '["Spirulina: Cyanobacteria", "Helicobacter pylori: Firmicutes", "Treponema pallidum: Actinobacteria", "Mycobacterium tuberculosis: Proteobacteria"]', 0, 'The book lists Anabaena and Spirulina under Cyanobacteria.', '{"1": "H. pylori is listed under Proteobacteria.", "2": "Treponema pallidum is listed under Spirochaetes.", "3": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis is listed under Actinobacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-0696', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 5 (§1.1)', 'In eukaryotes, DNA is organised into', '["a single circular chromosome in a nucleoid", "plasmids only", "multiple linear chromosomes associated with histone proteins", "naked circular DNA in the cytoplasm"]', 2, 'Eukaryotic DNA is organised into multiple linear chromosomes in the nucleus, associated with histones.', '{"0": "A single circular chromosome in a nucleoid is the prokaryotic arrangement.", "1": "Plasmids are small extra DNA molecules of bacteria.", "3": "Eukaryotic DNA is enclosed in the nucleus."}'),
  ('BIO-0697', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Three-domain system', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 5 (§1.1)', 'Mitochondria and chloroplasts are thought to have entered eukaryotic cells through', '["binary fission", "endosymbiosis", "conjugation", "meiosis"]', 1, 'Eukaryotes are believed to have originated through endosymbiosis, in which prokaryotic cells such as mitochondria and chloroplasts were engulfed by a host cell.', '{"0": "Binary fission is a method of prokaryotic reproduction.", "2": "Conjugation is gene transfer between bacteria.", "3": "Meiosis is a type of cell division."}'),
  ('BIO-0698', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Taxonomic hierarchy', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 5 (§1.2)', 'The highest level of the taxonomic hierarchy is the', '["kingdom", "phylum", "species", "domain"]', 3, 'Domain is the highest level; kingdom comes just below it.', '{"0": "Kingdom is just below domain.", "1": "Phylum is below kingdom.", "2": "Species is the most specific level."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0699', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Taxonomic hierarchy', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 6 (§1.2)', 'Organisms placed in the same phylum share', '["the ability to interbreed", "a basic body plan and significant structural features", "the same genus name", "an identical diet"]', 1, 'Organisms within a phylum share a basic body plan and significant structural features.', '{"0": "Interbreeding defines a species.", "2": "Many genera belong to one phylum.", "3": "Diet is not the criterion for a phylum."}'),
  ('BIO-0700', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Taxonomic hierarchy', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 6 (§1.2)', 'Humans and great apes are grouped together in the family', '["Primates", "Mammalia", "Hominidae", "Homo"]', 2, 'Within the order Primates, the family Hominidae includes great apes and humans.', '{"0": "Primates is the order.", "1": "Mammalia is the class.", "3": "Homo is the genus."}'),
  ('BIO-0701', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Taxonomic hierarchy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 6 (§1.2)', 'Which sequence of taxa is in the correct order from broad to specific?', '["Class, order, family, genus", "Order, class, genus, family", "Family, order, class, genus", "Class, family, order, genus"]', 0, 'The hierarchy runs kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species.', '{"1": "Class is broader than order, and family broader than genus.", "2": "Family is narrower than order and class.", "3": "Order comes before family."}'),
  ('BIO-0702', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Taxonomic hierarchy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 7 (§1.2)', 'In the classification of the onion, Allium cepa, the family is', '["Asparagales", "Monocots", "Allium", "Amaryllidaceae"]', 3, 'The book''s table classifies onion as Plantae, Angiosperms, Monocots, Asparagales (order), Amaryllidaceae (family), Allium (genus).', '{"0": "Asparagales is its order.", "1": "Monocots is its class.", "2": "Allium is its genus."}'),
  ('BIO-0703', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Kingdoms of Eukarya', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 7 (§1.3)', 'The book calls some protists "simple multicellular". This means they', '["do not have multicellular sex organs", "have no nucleus", "are always parasitic", "lack chlorophyll"]', 0, 'Simple multicellular means the protist does not have multicellular sex organs.', '{"1": "All protists are eukaryotes with a nucleus.", "2": "Only certain protists are parasitic.", "3": "Algae have chlorophyll."}'),
  ('BIO-0704', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Kingdoms of Eukarya', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 7 (§1.3)', 'Amoebic dysentery is caused by the protist', '["Plasmodium", "Trypanosoma", "Entamoeba", "Paramecium"]', 2, 'The book lists amoebic dysentery as caused by Entamoeba.', '{"0": "Plasmodium causes malaria.", "1": "Trypanosoma causes sleeping sickness.", "3": "Paramecium is a free-living protozoan, not given as a disease agent."}'),
  ('BIO-0705', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Kingdoms of Eukarya', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 7 (§1.3)', 'Slime molds and water molds belong to the protist group that is', '["animal-like", "plant-like", "prokaryotic", "fungi-like"]', 3, 'Myxomycota and Oomycota are fungi-like protists with hypha-like structure; they are saprophytic.', '{"0": "Animal-like protists are protozoa such as Amoeba.", "1": "Plant-like protists are algae.", "2": "Protists are eukaryotes."}'),
  ('BIO-0706', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Kingdoms of Eukarya', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 8 (§1.3)', 'The cell walls of fungi are made of', '["cellulose", "chitin", "peptidoglycan", "silica"]', 1, 'Fungal cells are covered by a wall made of chitin, a polysaccharide.', '{"0": "Cellulose forms walls of plants and algae.", "2": "Peptidoglycan forms bacterial walls.", "3": "Silica is found in some sponge spicules, not fungal walls."}'),
  ('BIO-0707', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Kingdoms of Eukarya', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 8 (§1.3)', 'Rhizopus, the bread mould, belongs to Zygomycota, whose hyphae', '["have septa", "are absent", "contain chlorophyll", "lack septa"]', 3, 'Zygomycota includes fungi whose hyphae lack septa, e.g. Rhizopus.', '{"0": "Septate hyphae are found in Ascomycota and Basidiomycota.", "1": "Rhizopus does form hyphae.", "2": "Fungi are heterotrophs without chlorophyll."}'),
  ('BIO-0708', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Kingdoms of Eukarya', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 8 (§1.3)', 'Morels, truffles, Neurospora and yeasts are members of', '["Basidiomycota", "Zygomycota", "Ascomycota", "Oomycota"]', 2, 'Ascomycota, the largest group of fungi, includes morels, truffles, cup fungi, Neurospora and yeasts.', '{"0": "Basidiomycota includes mushrooms, puffballs, rusts and smuts.", "1": "Zygomycota includes Rhizopus.", "3": "Oomycota are fungi-like protists."}'),
  ('BIO-0709', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Kingdoms of Eukarya', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 8 (§1.3)', 'Plants that lack xylem and phloem, such as mosses and liverworts, are called', '["bryophytes", "gymnosperms", "angiosperms", "ferns"]', 0, 'Nonvascular plants or bryophytes lack conducting tissues; examples are liverworts, hornworts and mosses.', '{"1": "Gymnosperms are vascular seed plants.", "2": "Angiosperms are vascular flowering plants.", "3": "Ferns are seedless vascular plants."}'),
  ('BIO-0710', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 10 (§1.4)', 'In a sponge, water leaves the body through the', '["ostia", "osculum", "spongocoel lining", "gemmule"]', 1, 'Water enters through the pores (ostia) and leaves through the larger pore, the osculum.', '{"0": "Ostia are where water enters.", "2": "The spongocoel is lined by choanocytes; it is a cavity, not the exit.", "3": "A gemmule is a resistant reproductive capsule."}'),
  ('BIO-0711', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 10 (§1.4)', 'The cells that line the spongocoel of a sponge are', '["choanocytes (collar cells)", "pinacocytes", "cnidocytes", "flame cells"]', 0, 'The third layer, lining the spongocoel, is made of choanocytes or collar cells.', '{"1": "Pinacocytes form the thin outer layer.", "2": "Cnidocytes are stinging cells of cnidarians.", "3": "Flame cells are excretory cells of flatworms."}'),
  ('BIO-0712', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 10 (§1.4)', 'Gemmules in sponges are', '["stinging organelles", "resistant capsules of amoeboid cells that can form a new sponge", "skeletal needles of silica", "pores through which water enters"]', 1, 'Some sponges form gemmules, resistant capsules containing amoeboid cells, which form a new sponge in a favourable environment.', '{"0": "Stinging organelles are nematocysts of cnidarians.", "2": "Skeletal needles are spicules.", "3": "Pores for water entry are ostia."}'),
  ('BIO-0713', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 11 (§1.4)', 'In cnidarians, the jelly-like layer between epidermis and gastrodermis is the', '["mesohyl", "mesoderm", "mesoglea", "parenchyma"]', 2, 'A jelly-like mesoglea lies between the epidermis and gastrodermis of cnidarians.', '{"0": "Mesohyl is the jelly-like layer of sponges.", "1": "Cnidarians are diploblastic and lack mesoderm.", "3": "Parenchyma fills the body of flatworms."}'),
  ('BIO-0714', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 12 (§1.4)', 'In cnidarians, the polyp form', '["is umbrella-like, free-swimming and reproduces sexually", "has a tube-like digestive system", "has a central nervous system", "is cylindrical, attached at its aboral end and reproduces asexually"]', 3, 'Polyps are cylindrical, attached to a substrate at the aboral end, and reproduce asexually; medusae are umbrella-like, free-swimming and sexual.', '{"0": "This describes the medusa.", "1": "Cnidarians have a sac-like digestive system.", "2": "Cnidarians have a nerve net, not a CNS."}'),
  ('BIO-0715', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 12 (§1.4)', 'Flatworms are described as acoelomates because the space between body wall and organs is', '["filled with a loose connective tissue called parenchyma", "filled with fluid called pseudocoelom", "lined by mesoderm as a true coelom", "filled with mesoglea"]', 0, 'In flatworms a loose connective tissue, parenchyma, fills the space between body wall and organs, so there is no body cavity.', '{"1": "A fluid-filled pseudocoelom is found in nematodes.", "2": "A true coelom is found in molluscs, annelids and others.", "3": "Mesoglea is found in cnidarians."}'),
  ('BIO-0716', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 12 (§1.4)', 'In flatworms, the cilia of flame cells', '["capture prey", "pump blood through vessels", "beat to draw surrounding fluid into excretory tubules", "help in gaseous exchange"]', 2, 'Each branch of the protonephridia ends in a flame cell whose cilia beat to suck surrounding fluid into the tubules.', '{"0": "Prey capture is not their function.", "1": "Flatworms have no circulatory system.", "3": "Flatworms have no respiratory system; flame cells are excretory."}'),
  ('BIO-0717', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 13 (§1.4)', 'In nematodes, the nerve ring around the pharynx is attached to', '["a double ventral nerve cord", "four longitudinal nerve cords", "a dorsal tubular nerve cord", "five radial nerves"]', 1, 'Nematodes have a nerve ring around the pharynx attached to four longitudinal nerve cords.', '{"0": "A double ventral nerve cord is found in annelids and arthropods.", "2": "A dorsal tubular nerve cord is a chordate feature.", "3": "Radial nerves in fives are found in echinoderms."}'),
  ('BIO-0718', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 14 (§1.4)', 'The rasping, tongue-like organ found in most molluscs is the', '["mantle", "madreporite", "syrinx", "radula"]', 3, 'All molluscs except bivalves have a rasping tongue-like organ called the radula.', '{"0": "The mantle is an epithelial envelope around the visceral mass.", "1": "The madreporite is the sieve plate of echinoderms.", "2": "The syrinx is the voice organ of birds."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0719', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 14 (§1.4)', 'Which group of molluscs is an exception to the open circulatory system?', '["Cephalopods", "Gastropods", "Bivalves", "Land snails"]', 0, 'All molluscs except cephalopods have an open circulatory system.', '{"1": "Gastropods have an open system.", "2": "Bivalves lack a radula, but they still have an open system.", "3": "Land snails are gastropods with an open system."}'),
  ('BIO-0720', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 15 (§1.4)', 'Metameric segmentation in annelids means that', '["the body has three tagmata", "each segment has its own circulatory, excretory and neural elements", "body parts are arranged in fives", "the body is unsegmented and soft"]', 1, 'In annelids each segment has its own circulatory, excretory and neural elements; this is metameric segmentation.', '{"0": "Tagmata are fused segment regions in arthropods.", "2": "Fivefold symmetry is found in echinoderms.", "3": "Annelids are segmented worms."}'),
  ('BIO-0721', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 15 (§1.4)', 'Chitinous bristles called setae are found in most annelids but are absent in', '["earthworms", "Nereis", "leeches", "marine annelids"]', 2, 'Setae are chitinous bristles that are absent in leeches.', '{"0": "Earthworms have setae.", "1": "Nereis has setae.", "3": "Most marine annelids such as Nereis have setae."}'),
  ('BIO-0722', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 16 (§1.4)', 'The blood of most annelids contains haemoglobin', '["inside red blood cells", "only in the pseudohearts", "in a colourless haemolymph", "dissolved in the plasma"]', 3, 'Annelid blood has the respiratory pigment haemoglobin dissolved in plasma.', '{"0": "The book places it in plasma, not in cells.", "1": "Pseudohearts are pulsating vessels, not the location of the pigment.", "2": "Colourless haemolymph without haemoglobin is arthropod blood."}'),
  ('BIO-0723', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 16 (§1.4)', 'In arthropods, the process of shedding the exoskeleton is called', '["metamorphosis", "ecdysis", "regeneration", "fission"]', 1, 'Shedding the exoskeleton and secreting a new one is ecdysis or moulting.', '{"0": "Metamorphosis is change in body form during development.", "2": "Regeneration is regrowth of lost parts.", "3": "Fission is division of the body into pieces."}'),
  ('BIO-0724', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 16 (§1.4)', 'In insects, Malpighian tubules are attached to the alimentary canal at the junction of', '["foregut and midgut", "pharynx and oesophagus", "crop and gizzard", "midgut and hindgut"]', 3, 'Malpighian tubules project from the alimentary canal at the junction of midgut and hindgut.', '{"0": "The book places them further back, at the midgut-hindgut junction.", "1": "These are not the attachment point.", "2": "Crop and gizzard are not the attachment point given."}'),
  ('BIO-0725', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 17 (§1.4)', 'Water enters the water-vascular system of a sea star through a sieve-like plate called the', '["madreporite", "osculum", "nephridiopore", "spiracle"]', 0, 'The ring canal opens outside through a sieve-like plate, the madreporite.', '{"1": "The osculum is the water exit of sponges.", "2": "The nephridiopore is an excretory opening of flatworms.", "3": "Spiracles are openings of arthropod tracheae."}'),
  ('BIO-0726', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Animal phyla', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 17 (§1.4)', 'Echinoderm larvae and adults differ in that', '["the larvae are radial while the adults are bilateral", "both are asymmetrical", "the larvae are bilaterally symmetrical while the adults are radially symmetrical", "only the larvae have an endoskeleton"]', 2, 'Echinoderm larvae are bilateral, but adults show radial symmetry in fives around an oral-aboral axis.', '{"0": "This reverses the two stages.", "1": "Neither stage is asymmetrical.", "3": "The adults have the calcareous ossicles."}'),
  ('BIO-0727', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Chordates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 19 (§1.4)', 'In urochordates such as sea squirts, the notochord and nerve cord are', '["present throughout life", "present only in the free-swimming larva", "replaced by a vertebral column", "never formed"]', 1, 'In Urochordata the notochord and nerve cord are present only in the free-swimming larvae.', '{"0": "A lifelong notochord is found in cephalochordates such as Amphioxus.", "2": "Replacement by a vertebral column happens in vertebrates.", "3": "They are formed in the larva."}'),
  ('BIO-0728', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Chordates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 18 (§1.4)', 'Which feature is unique to chordates and appears at some stage of development?', '["Pharyngeal slits", "Bilateral symmetry", "Triploblastic body", "True coelom"]', 0, 'Notochord, pharyngeal slits, dorsal tubular nerve cord and post-anal tail are the four features unique to chordates.', '{"1": "Many non-chordates are bilaterally symmetrical.", "2": "Many invertebrates are triploblastic.", "3": "Molluscs, annelids and others have a true coelom."}'),
  ('BIO-0729', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 20 (§1.5)', 'The heart of a fish has', '["three chambers, two atria and one ventricle", "four chambers with complete separation", "two chambers, one auricle and one ventricle", "one chamber only"]', 2, 'Fishes have a two-chambered heart, one auricle and one ventricle, with single circulation.', '{"0": "Two atria and one ventricle is the amphibian heart.", "1": "Four chambers are found in birds and mammals.", "3": "The fish heart has two chambers."}'),
  ('BIO-0730', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 21 (§1.5)', 'Sharks, skates and ratfish (class Chondrichthyes) are characterised by', '["a bony skeleton and a swim bladder", "a sucking mouth with seven pairs of pharyngeal slits", "four pairs of tentacles around the mouth", "a cartilaginous skeleton and no swim bladder or lungs"]', 3, 'Chondrichthyes have a cartilaginous skeleton, a tail fin with a large upper lobe and lack a swim bladder or lungs.', '{"0": "This describes Osteichthyes.", "1": "This describes lampreys (Cephalaspidomorphi).", "2": "This describes hagfishes (Myxini)."}'),
  ('BIO-0731', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 21 (§1.5)', 'Caecilians are amphibians that are', '["legless", "tailed", "tailless", "egg-laying mammals"]', 0, 'Salamanders, newts and mudpuppies are tailed; frogs and toads are tailless; caecilians are legless amphibians.', '{"1": "Tailed amphibians are salamanders, newts and mudpuppies.", "2": "Tailless amphibians are frogs and toads.", "3": "Egg-laying mammals are monotremes."}'),
  ('BIO-0732', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 22 (§1.5)', 'The shell of the reptile amniotic egg is', '["permeable to water but not to gases", "leathery and partly permeable to gases but not to water", "absent", "made of chitin"]', 1, 'The amniotic egg is covered by a leathery calcareous shell that is partly permeable to gases but not to water.', '{"0": "This reverses the permeability.", "2": "Reptile eggs do have a shell.", "3": "The shell is calcareous, not chitinous."}'),
  ('BIO-0733', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 22 (§1.5)', 'In reptiles, greater movement of the head is allowed by the first two cervical vertebrae, the', '["incus and malleus", "keel and sternum", "atlas and axis", "ossicles"]', 2, 'In reptiles the first two cervical vertebrae, atlas and axis, allow more head movement.', '{"0": "Incus and malleus are mammalian ear bones.", "1": "The keel is part of the sternum in birds.", "3": "Ossicles are echinoderm skeletal plates in this chapter."}'),
  ('BIO-0734', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 23 (§1.5)', 'In birds, the organ of voice, the syrinx, is located', '["in the larynx", "in the crop", "in the air sacs", "at the lower end of the trachea"]', 3, 'The syrinx, the voice organ of birds, is at the lower end of the trachea.', '{"0": "The larynx with vocal cords is the mammalian voice apparatus.", "1": "The crop is an oesophageal storage pouch.", "2": "Air sacs connect to the lungs; they are not the voice organ."}'),
  ('BIO-0735', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 23 (§1.5)', 'In birds, the region of the stomach with muscular walls that crushes food is the', '["crop", "gizzard", "keel", "cloaca"]', 1, 'A region of the stomach called the gizzard has muscular walls to crush food.', '{"0": "The crop is a storage diverticulum of the oesophagus.", "2": "The keel is part of the sternum.", "3": "The cloaca is not described as crushing food."}'),
  ('BIO-0736', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 23 (§1.5)', 'Which of these is a flightless (running) bird?', '["Kiwi", "Pigeon", "Crow", "Robin"]', 0, 'Ostrich, kiwi, rhea, cassowary and emu have secondarily lost flight.', '{"1": "Pigeons fly.", "2": "Crows fly.", "3": "Robins fly."}'),
  ('BIO-0737', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 23 (§1.5)', 'Sudoriferous glands in mammals', '["secrete oil", "produce milk", "secrete digestive enzymes", "release watery secretions used in evaporative cooling"]', 3, 'Sudoriferous (sweat) glands release watery secretions used in evaporative cooling; sebaceous glands secrete oil.', '{"0": "Oil is secreted by sebaceous glands.", "1": "Milk is produced by mammary glands.", "2": "Skin glands do not secrete digestive enzymes."}'),
  ('BIO-0738', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 24 (§1.5)', 'The duckbill platypus and spiny anteater are', '["marsupials with a pouch", "placental mammals", "monotremes, egg-laying mammals", "reptiles with hair"]', 2, 'Monotremes are egg-laying (oviparous) mammals, e.g. duckbill platypus and echidna.', '{"0": "Marsupials include opossum and kangaroo.", "1": "Placental mammals give birth to young nourished by a placenta.", "3": "They are mammals."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0739', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Vertebrates', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 24 (§1.5)', 'Which animal completes its development in its mother''s pouch?', '["Bat", "Kangaroo", "Dolphin", "Echidna"]', 1, 'Marsupials give birth to immature young that finish developing in the pouch; opossum and kangaroo are examples.', '{"0": "The bat is a placental mammal.", "2": "The dolphin is a placental mammal.", "3": "The echidna is an egg-laying monotreme."}'),
  ('BIO-0740', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 25 (§1.6)', 'Prions are infectious particles composed of', '["circular RNA only", "DNA with a protein coat", "protein only", "RNA with a lipid envelope"]', 2, 'Prions are composed of protein only; viroids are composed of circular RNA only.', '{"0": "Circular RNA only describes viroids.", "1": "Nucleic acid in a protein coat describes a virus.", "3": "An enveloped RNA particle is a virus such as influenza."}'),
  ('BIO-0741', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 25 (§1.6)', 'The guidelines for classifying viruses are set by the', '["International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses (ICTV)", "World Health Organization", "International Union for Conservation of Nature", "Pakistan Medical and Dental Council"]', 0, 'Virus classification follows guidelines from the International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses (ICTV).', '{"1": "WHO is a health body and not named as the source.", "2": "IUCN deals with conservation.", "3": "PMDC is a medical regulator."}'),
  ('BIO-0742', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 25 (§1.6)', 'Sulfolobus spindle-shaped virus is an example of a virus that infects', '["bacteria", "plants", "animals", "archaea"]', 3, 'Archaea viruses infect archaea; the book''s example is Sulfolobus spindle-shaped virus.', '{"0": "Bacterial viruses (bacteriophages) include T4 and lambda.", "1": "Plant viruses include tobacco mosaic virus and potato virus X.", "2": "Animal viruses include influenza and rabies viruses."}'),
  ('BIO-0743', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 26 (§1.6)', 'Which virus is described as having a helical capsid?', '["Rabies virus", "Adenovirus", "Herpesvirus", "Poliovirus"]', 0, 'Helical viruses include tobacco mosaic virus and rabies virus.', '{"1": "Adenoviruses are icosahedral.", "2": "Herpesviruses are icosahedral.", "3": "Poliovirus is given as a non-enveloped virus."}'),
  ('BIO-0744', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 26 (§1.6)', 'An enveloped virus has an outer lipid envelope that is', '["made of peptidoglycan", "derived from the host cell membrane", "secreted by the viral nucleic acid", "made only of capsid protein"]', 1, 'Enveloped viruses such as influenza and HIV have an outer lipid envelope derived from the host cell membrane.', '{"0": "Peptidoglycan is a bacterial wall polymer.", "2": "Nucleic acid does not secrete lipid.", "3": "The capsid lies beneath the envelope."}'),
  ('BIO-0745', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 26 (§1.6)', 'Rotaviruses, which cause gastroenteritis, contain', '["single-stranded DNA", "double-stranded DNA", "double-stranded RNA", "single-stranded RNA"]', 2, 'The book gives rotaviruses as dsRNA viruses.', '{"0": "Single-stranded DNA viruses include parvoviruses.", "1": "Double-stranded DNA viruses include adenoviruses and herpesviruses.", "3": "Single-stranded RNA viruses include coronaviruses and influenza viruses."}'),
  ('BIO-0746', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 26 (§1.6)', 'Parvoviruses are classified as', '["double-stranded DNA viruses", "double-stranded RNA viruses", "reverse transcribing viruses", "single-stranded DNA viruses"]', 3, 'The book lists parvoviruses as ssDNA viruses.', '{"0": "dsDNA examples are adenoviruses and herpesviruses.", "1": "dsRNA examples are rotaviruses.", "2": "Reverse transcribing examples are HIV and hepatitis B virus."}'),
  ('BIO-0747', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 26 (§1.6)', 'In a negative-sense RNA virus, such as rabies virus, the RNA genome', '["is translated directly by host ribosomes", "is first transcribed into mRNA by a viral RNA polymerase", "is copied into DNA by reverse transcriptase", "is never expressed"]', 1, 'Negative-sense genomes are transcribed into mRNA by a viral RNA polymerase before translation.', '{"0": "Direct translation describes positive-sense viruses such as poliovirus.", "2": "Reverse transcription describes retroviruses and hepadnaviruses.", "3": "The genome is expressed after transcription."}'),
  ('BIO-0748', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 26 (§1.6)', 'Hepatitis B virus is a reverse transcribing virus whose genome is', '["DNA", "RNA", "protein", "circular RNA without a coat"]', 0, 'Hepadnaviruses like hepatitis B virus are reverse transcribing viruses with a DNA genome; retroviruses like HIV have an RNA genome.', '{"1": "An RNA genome with reverse transcription describes HIV.", "2": "Protein-only agents are prions.", "3": "Naked circular RNA describes viroids."}'),
  ('BIO-0749', 'Acellular life', 'Viruses', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 26 (§1.6)', 'Which is a positive-sense RNA virus whose genome is translated directly by host ribosomes?', '["Ebola virus", "Rabies virus", "Hepatitis B virus", "Hepatitis C virus"]', 3, 'Positive-sense RNA viruses include poliovirus and hepatitis C virus.', '{"0": "Ebola virus is negative-sense.", "1": "Rabies virus is negative-sense.", "2": "Hepatitis B virus is a DNA reverse transcribing virus."}'),
  ('BIO-0750', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Biodiversity', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 27 (§1.7)', 'The role or function of an organism in its ecosystem, including its interactions and its place in energy flow, is its', '["habitat", "population", "niche", "biome"]', 2, 'A niche is the role or function of an organism, including its interactions and its role in energy flow.', '{"0": "A habitat is where an organism lives.", "1": "A population is a group of one species.", "3": "A biome is a large ecological region."}'),
  ('BIO-0751', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Biodiversity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 27 (§1.7)', 'The variety of genetic information within all individuals of a species is assessed at the', '["species level", "genetic level", "ecosystem level", "community level"]', 1, 'At the genetic level biodiversity refers to the variety of genetic information within individuals of a species; it allows adaptation to change.', '{"0": "The species level counts species and their abundance.", "2": "The ecosystem level looks at the range of habitats.", "3": "The book''s three levels are species, genetic and ecosystem."}'),
  ('BIO-0752', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Biodiversity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 27 (§1.7)', 'Random sampling is used when assessing biodiversity mainly because it', '["gives every part of the area an equal chance of being sampled, minimising bias", "lets the researcher pick the richest spots", "avoids the need for statistics", "counts every organism in the area"]', 0, 'Random sampling minimises bias by giving every part of the study area an equal chance of being sampled.', '{"1": "Choosing the richest spots introduces bias.", "2": "Random samples are analysed statistically.", "3": "Sampling estimates rather than counts everything."}'),
  ('BIO-0753', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Biodiversity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 28 (§1.7)', 'Which method is most suitable for studying the distribution of plants or other sessile organisms in a field?', '["Point counts", "Mark-recapture", "Quadrat sampling", "Aerial surveys of moving herds"]', 2, 'Quadrat sampling, using randomly placed squares, is particularly useful for plant populations and sessile organisms.', '{"0": "Point counts are used for birds and other mobile animals.", "1": "Mark-recapture is for mobile animals that are hard to count.", "3": "Aerial surveys cover large areas and wide-ranging animals."}'),
  ('BIO-0754', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Biodiversity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 28 (§1.7)', 'To estimate the population size of animals that are difficult to count directly, ecologists use', '["quadrat sampling", "transect sampling", "point counts of plants", "mark-recapture"]', 3, 'Mark-recapture involves capturing, marking, releasing and later recapturing individuals to estimate population size.', '{"0": "Quadrats suit plants and sessile organisms.", "1": "Transects record species along a line.", "2": "Point counts observe animals from a fixed point and are not for plants."}'),
  ('BIO-0755', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Species and speciation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 29 (§1.8)', 'Organisms that look alike but are genetically distinct are called', '["hybrids", "cryptic species", "polyploids", "subspecies"]', 1, 'Cryptic species appear similar but are genetically distinct, which makes identification by physical traits difficult.', '{"0": "Hybrids are offspring of two different types.", "2": "Polyploids have extra sets of chromosomes.", "3": "Subspecies is not the term used in the book."}'),
  ('BIO-0756', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Species and speciation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 29 (§1.8)', 'Ernst Mayr''s species concept emphasises', '["physical similarity", "geographical location", "reproductive isolation", "body size"]', 2, 'Mayr emphasised reproductive isolation: species are groups of interbreeding natural populations.', '{"0": "Physical similarity can mislead because of cryptic species.", "1": "Location is not the criterion.", "3": "Body size is not the criterion."}'),
  ('BIO-0757', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Species and speciation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 29 (§1.8)', 'Speciation in a small isolated population at the edge of a larger population is called', '["allopatric speciation", "parapatric speciation", "sympatric speciation", "peripatric speciation"]', 3, 'Peripatric speciation involves a small, isolated population at the edge of a larger one, e.g. island species from a small founding population.', '{"0": "Allopatric speciation involves a population split into isolated groups.", "1": "Parapatric speciation involves adjacent populations along a gradient.", "2": "Sympatric speciation happens in the same area without barriers."}'),
  ('BIO-0758', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Species and speciation', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 30 (§1.8)', 'The grass Anthoxanthum odoratum, adapted to different soil conditions in adjacent areas, illustrates', '["parapatric speciation", "allopatric speciation", "peripatric speciation", "sympatric speciation by polyploidy"]', 0, 'Parapatric speciation occurs in adjacent populations along an environmental gradient with limited gene flow; the book''s example is Anthoxanthum odoratum.', '{"1": "Allopatric speciation needs geographical separation.", "2": "Peripatric speciation involves a small edge population.", "3": "Polyploidy is the book''s sympatric mechanism, not this example."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0759', 'Biodiversity and classification', 'Species and speciation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Biodiversity and Classification, p. 30 (§1.8)', 'Polyploidy in plants can lead to immediate reproductive isolation. This is an example of', '["allopatric speciation", "peripatric speciation", "parapatric speciation", "sympatric speciation"]', 3, 'Sympatric speciation, in the same area without barriers, often occurs through polyploidy or niche differentiation.', '{"0": "Allopatric speciation needs a geographical barrier.", "1": "Peripatric speciation needs a small isolated edge population.", "2": "Parapatric speciation involves adjacent environments along a gradient."}'),
  ('BIO-0760', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 34 (§2.1)', 'The major component of the bacterial cell wall is', '["cellulose", "chitin", "pseudopeptidoglycan", "peptidoglycan (murein)"]', 3, 'The major component of the bacterial wall is a unique macromolecule, peptidoglycan or murein.', '{"0": "Cellulose is the main wall material of plants.", "1": "Chitin forms fungal walls.", "2": "Pseudopeptidoglycan is found in some archaea."}'),
  ('BIO-0761', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 34 (§2.1)', 'Peptidoglycan is made of', '["long glycan chains cross-linked by short peptide fragments", "phospholipids joined by ether linkages", "chitin fibres embedded in protein", "lipopolysaccharides and porins"]', 0, 'Peptidoglycan is composed of long glycan (polysaccharide) chains cross-linked with short peptide fragments.', '{"1": "Ether-linked lipids are archaeal membrane lipids.", "2": "Chitin is a fungal wall material.", "3": "Lipopolysaccharides and porins belong to the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-0762', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 34 (§2.1)', 'The Gram staining technique was devised by', '["Robert Koch", "Carl Woese", "Hans Christian Gram", "Robert Whittaker"]', 2, 'Sir Hans Christian Gram devised Gram''s staining.', '{"0": "Koch is not credited with this stain in the book.", "1": "Woese proposed the three-domain system.", "3": "Whittaker proposed the five-kingdom system."}'),
  ('BIO-0763', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 34 (§2.1)', 'Gram-positive bacteria stain purple because they', '["lack a cell wall", "retain the violet dye in their thick peptidoglycan wall", "have an outer lipopolysaccharide membrane", "contain no peptidoglycan"]', 1, 'Gram-positive bacteria retain the violet dye and so stain purple; their walls have a thick peptidoglycan layer.', '{"0": "Bacteria without walls are mycoplasmas, not Gram-positive bacteria.", "2": "An outer lipopolysaccharide membrane is a Gram-negative feature.", "3": "Gram-positive walls are rich in peptidoglycan."}'),
  ('BIO-0764', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 34 (§2.1)', 'Compared with Gram-positive bacteria, the cell wall of Gram-negative bacteria has', '["a thick peptidoglycan layer and less lipid", "a thin peptidoglycan layer and an outer membrane", "no periplasmic space", "no lipids at all"]', 1, 'Gram-negative walls have a thin peptidoglycan layer and an outer membrane of lipopolysaccharides and lipoproteins.', '{"0": "A thick peptidoglycan layer with less lipid is the Gram-positive wall.", "2": "Gram-negative walls have more periplasmic space.", "3": "Their outer membrane is lipid-rich."}'),
  ('BIO-0765', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 34 (§2.1)', 'Porin in the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria', '["makes the cell wall rigid", "anchors the flagellum", "stores genes for antibiotic resistance", "acts as a pore for specific molecules"]', 3, 'The outer membrane contains porin, a protein that acts like a pore for specific molecules.', '{"0": "Rigidity comes from peptidoglycan.", "1": "Flagella are anchored by the rings of the basal body.", "2": "Resistance genes are carried on plasmids."}'),
  ('BIO-0766', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 34 (§2.1)', 'Gram-negative bacteria are resistant to many antibiotics mainly because of their', '["thick peptidoglycan layer", "capsule-free surface", "outer membrane", "70S ribosomes"]', 2, 'The book says the outer membrane makes Gram-negative bacteria resistant to many antibiotics.', '{"0": "The thick peptidoglycan layer is a Gram-positive feature.", "1": "The book does not give this as the reason.", "3": "All bacteria have 70S ribosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0767', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'The periplasmic space is the space between', '["the peptidoglycan layer and the cell membrane", "the capsule and the cell wall", "the two ribosomal subunits", "the nucleoid and the mesosome"]', 0, 'The periplasmic space is between the peptidoglycan layer and the cell membrane; Gram-negative bacteria have more of it.', '{"1": "The capsule lies outside the wall.", "2": "Ribosomal subunits are not separated by a space.", "3": "These are internal cytoplasmic structures."}'),
  ('BIO-0768', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'The gelatinous layer outside the cell wall of some bacteria, which gives colonies a sticky character, is the', '["periplasm", "capsule", "mesosome", "nucleoid"]', 1, 'Some bacteria produce a capsule, a gelatinous layer outside the wall that makes colonies sticky.', '{"0": "The periplasm lies inside the wall.", "2": "Mesosomes are infoldings of the cell membrane.", "3": "The nucleoid is the DNA region."}'),
  ('BIO-0769', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'In bacteria such as Mycoplasmas, which lack a cell wall, the outermost layer is the', '["capsule", "outer membrane", "cell membrane", "S-layer of protein"]', 2, 'The cell membrane lies outermost in bacteria that lack a cell wall, e.g. Mycoplasmas.', '{"0": "The book names the membrane, not a capsule.", "1": "An outer membrane is part of the Gram-negative wall.", "3": "No protein S-layer is described."}'),
  ('BIO-0770', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'Unlike eukaryotic membranes, the bacterial cell membrane lacks', '["sterols such as cholesterol", "phospholipids", "proteins", "a lipid bilayer"]', 0, 'The bacterial cell membrane does not have sterols (e.g. cholesterol).', '{"1": "Phospholipids are present in bacterial membranes.", "2": "Membrane proteins are present.", "3": "It is a lipid membrane."}'),
  ('BIO-0771', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'Mesosomes are infoldings of the bacterial cell membrane that', '["synthesise the capsule", "form the flagellum", "store plasmids", "take part in DNA replication and cell division and serve as respiratory centres"]', 3, 'Mesosomes are involved in DNA replication and cell division and also serve as respiratory centres.', '{"0": "Capsule synthesis is not given as their role.", "1": "The flagellum is built of flagellin protein from the basal body outward.", "2": "Plasmids lie free in the cytoplasm."}'),
  ('BIO-0772', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'Bacterial ribosomes sediment at', '["80S, with 60S and 40S subunits", "70S, with 60S and 40S subunits", "80S, with 50S and 30S subunits", "70S, with 50S and 30S subunits"]', 3, 'Each bacterial ribosome sediments at 70S; the larger subunit at 50S and the smaller at 30S.', '{"0": "80S ribosomes with 60S and 40S subunits are eukaryotic.", "1": "The subunits of the 70S ribosome are 50S and 30S.", "2": "Bacterial ribosomes are 70S."}'),
  ('BIO-0773', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'The bacterial chromosome in the nucleoid is', '["several linear DNA molecules wrapped on histones", "single-stranded RNA", "a single circular double-stranded DNA without histones", "enclosed in a nuclear membrane"]', 2, 'A bacterium has a single, circular, double-stranded DNA in the nucleoid, without attached histones.', '{"0": "Linear chromosomes with histones are eukaryotic.", "1": "Bacterial genetic material is DNA.", "3": "There is no nuclear membrane around the nucleoid."}'),
  ('BIO-0774', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'Plasmids are', '["self-replicating, circular, double-stranded DNA molecules outside the chromosome", "linear RNA molecules", "proteins that cross-link peptidoglycan", "membrane infoldings"]', 0, 'Plasmids are circular, double-stranded extrachromosomal DNA molecules that replicate on their own.', '{"1": "Plasmids are DNA, not RNA.", "2": "Cross-links are short peptides in peptidoglycan.", "3": "Membrane infoldings are mesosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-0775', 'Cell structure and function', 'Structure of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 35 (§2.1)', 'The cytoplasm of a bacterium lacks', '["ribosomes", "a cytoskeleton and membrane-bounded organelles", "a nucleoid", "dissolved substances"]', 1, 'Bacterial cytoplasm contains the nucleoid, ribosomes and mesosomes but lacks cytoskeleton and membrane-bounded organelles.', '{"0": "Ribosomes are present.", "2": "The nucleoid is present.", "3": "Dissolved substances are present."}'),
  ('BIO-0776', 'Bacteria', 'Endospores', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 36 (§2.2)', 'Bacterial endospores are', '["actively dividing cells", "thick-walled, dormant resting cells", "reproductive gametes", "flagellated swimming cells"]', 1, 'Endospores are thick-walled, metabolically inactive (dormant) resting cells.', '{"0": "Endospores do not divide.", "2": "They are not gametes.", "3": "They are dormant and do not swim."}'),
  ('BIO-0777', 'Bacteria', 'Endospores', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 36 (§2.2)', 'During sporulation, the new DNA is first isolated from the rest of the cell by a', '["septum formed by the cell membrane", "spore coat", "capsule", "new peptidoglycan layer"]', 0, 'After DNA replication, the cell membrane makes a septum to isolate the new DNA and a small portion of cytoplasm.', '{"1": "The spore coat forms later.", "2": "The capsule is not part of sporulation.", "3": "The new peptidoglycan layer forms later between the membranes."}'),
  ('BIO-0778', 'Bacteria', 'Endospores', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 36 (§2.2)', 'In endospore formation, the new peptidoglycan layer is laid down', '["outside the spore coat", "inside the nucleoid", "between the two membranes surrounding the new DNA", "outside the vegetative cell wall"]', 2, 'A new peptidoglycan layer forms between the two membranes around the separated DNA and cytoplasm; a spore coat then forms around it.', '{"0": "The spore coat is outermost.", "1": "Peptidoglycan is not formed inside the nucleoid.", "3": "The vegetative cell later breaks to release the spore."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0779', 'Bacteria', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 37 (§2.3)', 'When a bacterial population moves together by means of flagella, the movement is called', '["twitching", "gliding", "sliding", "swarming"]', 3, 'Movement of a population together by flagella is called swarming.', '{"0": "Twitching is mediated by pili.", "1": "Gliding uses a secreted slimy substance.", "2": "Sliding is due to pushing by dividing cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0780', 'Bacteria', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 37 (§2.3)', 'Twitching motility in bacteria is brought about by', '["counterclockwise rotation of flagella", "secretion of slime", "an axial filament", "pili that bind to a surface and retract"]', 3, 'Twitching or crawling is mediated by pili, which bind to the surface and retract, pulling the cell forward.', '{"0": "Flagellar rotation drives swimming.", "1": "Slime secretion is gliding.", "2": "The axial filament moves spirochaetes."}'),
  ('BIO-0781', 'Bacteria', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 37 (§2.3)', 'A bacterial flagellum pushes the cell forward when it rotates', '["counterclockwise", "clockwise", "back and forth like a whip", "only when attached to a surface"]', 0, 'Counterclockwise rotation of the flagellum pushes the cell forward with the flagellum trailing behind.', '{"1": "The book gives counterclockwise rotation for forward movement.", "2": "Bacterial flagella rotate; they do not whip like eukaryotic flagella.", "3": "Flagellar movement is for liquid media."}'),
  ('BIO-0782', 'Bacteria', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 37 (§2.3)', 'Sliding motility in bacteria results from', '["retraction of pili", "rotation of flagella", "the pushing force of dividing cells", "random movement of fluid particles"]', 2, 'Sliding is due to the expansion created by the pushing force of dividing cells.', '{"0": "Pilus retraction causes twitching.", "1": "Flagellar rotation causes swimming.", "3": "Random fluid movement is Brownian movement."}'),
  ('BIO-0783', 'Bacteria', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 37 (§2.3)', 'Streptococcus, which has neither flagella nor pili, moves by', '["gliding", "Brownian movement", "swarming", "an axial filament"]', 1, 'Bacteria such as Streptococcus that lack flagella or pili move by Brownian movement, the random movement of particles in fluid.', '{"0": "Gliding needs slime secretion.", "2": "Swarming needs flagella.", "3": "Axial filaments are found in spirochaetes."}'),
  ('BIO-0784', 'Bacteria', 'Motility', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 37 (§2.3)', 'The axial filament of spirochaetes lies', '["outside the outer membrane like a whip", "in the periplasmic space and is anchored at the poles", "inside the nucleoid", "in the capsule"]', 1, 'The axial filament runs lengthwise in the periplasmic space and consists of flagella-like fibrils anchored at the two poles.', '{"0": "It is internal, not an external whip.", "2": "It is not part of the nucleoid.", "3": "It is not in the capsule."}'),
  ('BIO-0785', 'Bacteria', 'Flagella', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 38 (§2.4)', 'Bacteria with a tuft of flagella at one pole are called', '["monotrichous", "amphitrichous", "peritrichous", "lophotrichous"]', 3, 'Lophotrichous bacteria have a tuft of flagella at one pole.', '{"0": "Monotrichous bacteria have a single polar flagellum.", "1": "Amphitrichous bacteria have flagella at both poles.", "2": "Peritrichous bacteria have flagella all around the cell."}'),
  ('BIO-0786', 'Bacteria', 'Flagella', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 38 (§2.4)', 'Bacteria without flagella are called', '["peritrichous", "monotrichous", "atrichous", "lophotrichous"]', 2, 'Bacteria that do not possess flagella are atrichous.', '{"0": "Peritrichous bacteria have flagella all over.", "1": "Monotrichous bacteria have one polar flagellum.", "3": "Lophotrichous bacteria have a polar tuft."}'),
  ('BIO-0787', 'Bacteria', 'Flagella', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 38 (§2.4)', 'Compared with flagella, bacterial pili are', '["smaller and thinner, and used mainly for attachment", "longer and thicker, and used for swimming", "built on a 9+2 pattern of microtubules", "found only in archaea"]', 0, 'Pili are non-helical filamentous appendages, smaller and thinner than flagella, used for attachment.', '{"1": "Flagella, not pili, are used for swimming.", "2": "Neither bacterial pili nor flagella have the 9+2 pattern.", "3": "Pili are described in bacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-0788', 'Bacteria', 'Flagella', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 38 (§2.4)', 'Bacterial flagella differ from eukaryotic flagella because they', '["have a 9+2 arrangement of microtubules", "are made of tubulin", "are made of flagellin and lack the 9+2 microtubule pattern", "are covered by the cell membrane"]', 2, 'Bacterial flagella are not built on the 9+2 pattern of microtubules; they are composed of flagellin protein.', '{"0": "The 9+2 pattern belongs to eukaryotic flagella.", "1": "Tubulin forms microtubules of eukaryotic flagella.", "3": "This is a feature of eukaryotic flagella."}'),
  ('BIO-0789', 'Bacteria', 'Flagella', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 38 (§2.4)', 'The basal body of the flagellum in Gram-negative bacteria has', '["two pairs of rings", "one pair of rings", "a 9+2 microtubule core", "no rings"]', 0, 'The basal body has rotating rings: one pair in Gram-positive and two pairs in Gram-negative bacteria.', '{"1": "One pair is found in Gram-positive bacteria.", "2": "Bacterial flagella lack microtubules.", "3": "The basal body is made of rings."}'),
  ('BIO-0790', 'Bacteria', 'Flagella', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 38 (§2.4)', 'In a bacterial flagellum, the curved structure that connects the basal body with the filament is the', '["pilus", "hook", "axial filament", "end plate"]', 1, 'The hook is a curved structure that connects the basal body with the filament.', '{"0": "Pili are separate appendages for attachment.", "2": "The axial filament is the internal flagellum of spirochaetes.", "3": "An end plate is part of the T4 phage tail."}'),
  ('BIO-0791', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 39 (§2.5)', 'Margulis and Schwartz classified all prokaryotes into', '["5 kingdoms", "3 domains", "8 classes", "16 phyla"]', 3, 'Margulis and Schwartz proposed a classification of all prokaryotes into 16 phyla.', '{"0": "Five kingdoms is Whittaker''s system.", "1": "Three domains is Woese''s system.", "2": "The book does not give 8 classes."}'),
  ('BIO-0792', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 39 (§2.5)', 'Escherichia coli belongs to the group', '["Pseudomonads", "Actinomycetes", "Omnibacteria", "Spirochaetes"]', 2, 'Omnibacteria are rigid, rod-shaped, heterotrophic Gram-negative bacteria; E. coli is an example.', '{"0": "Pseudomonads include Pseudomonas aeruginosa.", "1": "Actinomycetes include Mycobacterium.", "3": "Spirochaetes include Treponema."}'),
  ('BIO-0793', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 39 (§2.5)', 'In many cyanobacteria, atmospheric nitrogen is fixed in special cells called', '["endospores", "mesosomes", "gemmules", "heterocysts"]', 3, 'Many cyanobacteria fix nitrogen in special cells called heterocysts.', '{"0": "Endospores are dormant resting cells.", "1": "Mesosomes are membrane infoldings.", "2": "Gemmules are sponge reproductive capsules."}'),
  ('BIO-0794', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 39 (§2.5)', 'Besides chlorophyll a, cyanobacteria contain accessory pigments such as', '["carotenoids and phycobilins", "chlorophyll b only", "haemoglobin", "melanin"]', 0, 'Cyanobacteria contain chlorophyll a and accessory pigments such as carotenoids and blue and red phycobilins.', '{"1": "Chlorophyll b is not listed for cyanobacteria.", "2": "Haemoglobin is not a cyanobacterial pigment.", "3": "Melanin is not mentioned."}'),
  ('BIO-0795', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 39 (§2.5)', 'Mycoplasmas are naturally resistant to penicillin because they', '["have a thick capsule", "lack a cell wall", "form endospores", "have an outer membrane of porins"]', 1, 'Mycoplasmas and spiroplasmas lack cell walls, so antibiotics that inhibit wall growth do not affect them.', '{"0": "The book links resistance to the missing wall, not a capsule.", "2": "Endospores are not the reason given.", "3": "They have no wall or outer membrane."}'),
  ('BIO-0796', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 39 (§2.5)', 'Lethal yellowing disease of coconuts is caused by', '["spiroplasmas", "mycoplasmas", "pseudomonads", "actinomycetes"]', 0, 'Spiroplasmas cause significant plant diseases, e.g. lethal yellowing of coconuts.', '{"1": "Mycoplasmas are linked to pneumonia in humans.", "2": "Pseudomonads are plant pathogens but not named for this disease.", "3": "Actinomycetes are linked to TB, leprosy and dental plaque."}'),
  ('BIO-0797', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 39 (§2.5)', 'Syphilis is caused by Treponema, which belongs to the', '["omnibacteria", "spirochaetes", "actinomycetes", "cyanobacteria"]', 1, 'Treponema is an important spirochaete; spirochaetes are long spirilla with Gram-negative walls.', '{"0": "Omnibacteria include E. coli.", "2": "Actinomycetes include Mycobacterium.", "3": "Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic."}'),
  ('BIO-0798', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 40 (§2.5)', 'Leprosy and tuberculosis are caused by members of the', '["pseudomonads", "spirochaetes", "omnibacteria", "actinomycetes"]', 3, 'Mycobacterium leprae (leprosy) and Mycobacterium tuberculosis (TB) are actinomycetes.', '{"0": "Pseudomonads are soil and water rods.", "1": "Spirochaetes include Treponema.", "2": "Omnibacteria include E. coli."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0799', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 40 (§2.5)', 'Some actinomycetes are responsible for', '["lethal yellowing of coconuts", "syphilis", "dental plaque, in which tooth enamel is destroyed", "lethal pneumonia in all mammals"]', 2, 'The book says some actinomycetes are responsible for dental plaque, in which the enamel of teeth is destroyed.', '{"0": "Lethal yellowing is caused by spiroplasmas.", "1": "Syphilis is caused by Treponema, a spirochaete.", "3": "Mycoplasmas, not actinomycetes, are linked to pneumonia."}'),
  ('BIO-0800', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 40 (§2.5)', 'Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter obtain energy by', '["oxidising inorganic nitrogen compounds", "photosynthesis", "decomposing dead matter", "fixing atmospheric nitrogen in heterocysts"]', 0, 'They are chemosynthetic bacteria that oxidise nitrogen compounds and use the energy to make food.', '{"1": "They are not photosynthetic.", "2": "Decomposition is done by decomposer bacteria.", "3": "Heterocysts belong to cyanobacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-0801', 'Bacteria', 'Bacterial groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 40 (§2.5)', 'Azotobacter, found in soil and water, is a', '["chemosynthetic sulphur oxidiser", "nitrogen-fixing aerobic bacterium", "photosynthetic cyanobacterium", "wall-less mycoplasma"]', 1, 'Azotobacter belongs to the nitrogen-fixing aerobic bacteria and converts atmospheric nitrogen.', '{"0": "Chemosynthetic bacteria named are Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter.", "2": "It is not a cyanobacterium.", "3": "It has a cell wall."}'),
  ('BIO-0802', 'Bacteria', 'Importance of bacteria', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 41 (§2.6)', 'Lactobacillus, together with yeasts and moulds, is used to make', '["antibiotics such as tetracycline", "biopesticides", "fermented foods such as yogurt and cheese", "insulin from human genes"]', 2, 'Bacteria such as Lactobacillus with yeasts and moulds have long been used to prepare fermented foods like cheese, pickles, vinegar and yogurt.', '{"0": "Tetracycline comes from actinomycetes.", "1": "The biopesticide named is Bacillus thuringiensis.", "3": "Insulin is made by bacteria given human genes, not the use named for Lactobacillus."}'),
  ('BIO-0803', 'Bacteria', 'Importance of bacteria', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 41 (§2.6)', 'Use of bacteria to remove or degrade pollutants such as oil spills is called', '["biopesticide use", "fermentation", "sporulation", "bioremediation"]', 3, 'Removal or degradation of pollutants by bacteria is bioremediation.', '{"0": "Biopesticides control pests.", "1": "Fermentation makes foods like yogurt.", "2": "Sporulation is endospore formation."}'),
  ('BIO-0804', 'Bacteria', 'Importance of bacteria', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 41 (§2.6)', 'Which bacterium is commonly used as a biopesticide?', '["Escherichia coli", "Bacillus thuringiensis", "Treponema pallidum", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]', 1, 'Biological pest control commonly involves Bacillus thuringiensis, a Gram-positive soil bacterium.', '{"0": "E. coli is not named as a biopesticide.", "2": "Treponema causes syphilis.", "3": "P. aeruginosa can infect weak people."}'),
  ('BIO-0805', 'Bacteria', 'Importance of bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 42 (§2.6)', 'Bacteria are widely used in molecular biology and genetics research mainly because they', '["are eukaryotic", "have no DNA", "grow quickly and are easy to manipulate", "cannot mutate"]', 2, 'Bacteria grow quickly and are easily manipulated, so they are used in molecular biology, genetics and biochemistry.', '{"0": "Bacteria are prokaryotic.", "1": "Bacteria have DNA that can be mutated and studied.", "3": "Scientists deliberately make mutations in them."}'),
  ('BIO-0806', 'Bacteria', 'Normal flora', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 42 (§2.7)', 'The mixture of microorganisms regularly found at an anatomical site in a healthy person is called the', '["pathogen load", "biofilm", "plankton", "normal flora"]', 3, 'The mixture of organisms regularly found at any anatomical site is the normal flora.', '{"0": "Pathogens cause disease.", "1": "Biofilm is not the book''s term.", "2": "Plankton are drifting aquatic organisms."}'),
  ('BIO-0807', 'Bacteria', 'Normal flora', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 42 (§2.7)', 'In a healthy animal, which tissue is normally free of microorganisms?', '["Blood", "Skin", "Mucous membranes of the mouth", "Lining of the large intestine"]', 0, 'Internal tissues such as blood, brain and muscle are normally free of microorganisms; surface tissues are colonised.', '{"1": "Skin is a colonised surface tissue.", "2": "Mucous membranes are colonised.", "3": "The intestine is in contact with the environment and is colonised."}'),
  ('BIO-0808', 'Bacteria', 'Normal flora', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 42 (§2.7)', 'Enteric bacteria of the normal flora secrete', '["vitamin K and vitamin B12", "vitamin C and vitamin D", "insulin", "bile salts"]', 0, 'Enteric bacteria secrete vitamin K and vitamin B12, which are absorbed and distributed in the body.', '{"1": "These vitamins are not named.", "2": "Insulin can be made in engineered bacteria but is not a normal flora product.", "3": "Bile salts are made by the liver."}'),
  ('BIO-0809', 'Bacteria', 'Normal flora', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 42 (§2.7)', 'Normal flora protect us from pathogens partly by', '["destroying the body''s own antibodies", "competing with pathogens for attachment sites and nutrients", "producing toxins that damage the gut", "blocking vitamin absorption"]', 1, 'Normal flora compete with pathogens for attachment sites and nutrients, so pathogens have less chance to enter tissues.', '{"0": "They stimulate low levels of cross-reacting antibodies.", "2": "Harmful toxins are not a benefit.", "3": "They supply vitamins."}'),
  ('BIO-0810', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 43 (§2.8)', 'The size range of viruses given in the book is from', '["0.1 µm to 15 µm", "2 µm to 25 µm", "20 nm (parvovirus) to 250 nm (poxviruses)", "200 nm to 2,500 nm"]', 2, 'Viruses range in size from 20 nm (parvovirus) to 250 nm (pox viruses).', '{"0": "0.1 to over 15 µm is the size of archaea.", "1": "This is far larger than viruses.", "3": "This range is too large."}'),
  ('BIO-0811', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 43 (§2.8)', 'The protein subunits that make up a viral capsid are called', '["virions", "nucleocapsids", "spikes", "capsomeres"]', 3, 'The capsid is made of protein subunits called capsomeres.', '{"0": "A virion is a whole virus particle.", "1": "The nucleocapsid is the core plus capsid.", "2": "Spikes are glycoproteins on an envelope."}'),
  ('BIO-0812', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 43 (§2.8)', 'The nucleic acid core of a virus together with its capsid is called the', '["envelope", "capsomere", "nucleocapsid", "prion"]', 2, 'The central core and capsid are collectively called the nucleocapsid.', '{"0": "The envelope is an outer membrane in some animal viruses.", "1": "A capsomere is one capsid subunit.", "3": "A prion is a protein-only agent."}'),
  ('BIO-0813', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 43 (§2.8)', 'According to the book, the adenovirus capsid contains', '["6 capsomeres", "20 capsomeres", "162 capsomeres", "252 capsomeres"]', 3, 'Adenovirus, which causes common cold, contains 252 capsomeres in its capsid.', '{"0": "6 is the number of T4 tail fibres.", "1": "20 is the number of faces of an icosahedron.", "2": "162 is not the number given."}'),
  ('BIO-0814', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 43 (§2.8)', 'Viruses without an envelope are called', '["prions", "naked viruses", "bacteriophages", "retroviruses"]', 1, 'Non-enveloped viruses are known as naked viruses.', '{"0": "Prions are protein-only agents.", "2": "Bacteriophages are viruses of bacteria and may be naked or not.", "3": "Retroviruses such as HIV are enveloped."}'),
  ('BIO-0815', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 44 (§2.8)', 'In the T4 bacteriophage, attachment to the bacterial wall is helped by', '["six tail fibres attached to the end plate", "glycoprotein spikes on an envelope", "the hexagonal head", "reverse transcriptase"]', 0, 'Six tail fibres attached to the end plate help the phage attach to the bacterial wall.', '{"1": "Glycoprotein spikes are on HIV''s envelope.", "2": "The head holds the DNA.", "3": "Reverse transcriptase is a retrovirus enzyme."}'),
  ('BIO-0816', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 44 (§2.8)', 'The head of a T4 phage contains', '["single-stranded RNA", "reverse transcriptase and RNA", "a long double-stranded DNA", "protein only"]', 2, 'The T4 phage capsid is made of proteins and its core contains a long double-stranded DNA.', '{"0": "Single-stranded RNA is found in HIV.", "1": "RNA with reverse transcriptase is in HIV.", "3": "Protein only describes prions."}'),
  ('BIO-0817', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 44 (§2.8)', 'Bacteriophages are useful in genetic engineering because they', '["make bacteria resistant to all viruses", "digest bacterial DNA completely", "produce antibiotics", "can carry a gene of interest into a target bacterial cell"]', 3, 'The gene of interest is inserted into the phage DNA, which carries it into the target bacterium, producing transgenic bacteria.', '{"0": "The use described is gene transfer.", "1": "The phage DNA joins the bacterial chromosome rather than destroying all DNA.", "2": "Phages do not make antibiotics."}'),
  ('BIO-0818', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 44 (§2.8)', 'HIV belongs to the group of viruses called', '["retroviruses", "bacteriophages", "parvoviruses", "adenoviruses"]', 0, 'Human immunodeficiency virus belongs to the retroviruses, a special group of animal viruses.', '{"1": "Bacteriophages infect bacteria.", "2": "Parvoviruses are small ssDNA viruses.", "3": "Adenoviruses are icosahedral DNA viruses."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0819', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 44 (§2.8)', 'The glycoprotein spikes on the HIV envelope', '["copy RNA into DNA", "help the virus identify and bind to its target cell", "form the capsid", "store the viral genome"]', 1, 'Envelope glycoprotein spikes help the virus identify and bind to its target.', '{"0": "Copying RNA into DNA is done by reverse transcriptase.", "2": "The capsid is a protein coat inside the envelope.", "3": "The genome is RNA inside the capsid."}'),
  ('BIO-0820', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 44 (§2.8)', 'The approximate diameter of HIV given in the book is', '["1,000 nm", "20 nm", "100 nm", "250 nm"]', 2, 'HIV is spherical and about 100 nm in diameter.', '{"0": "1,000 nm (1 µm) is bacterial size.", "1": "20 nm is the size given for parvovirus.", "3": "250 nm is the size given for poxviruses."}'),
  ('BIO-0821', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 45 (§2.8)', 'Reverse transcriptase catalyses the formation of', '["RNA from a DNA template", "protein from mRNA", "a lipid envelope", "DNA from a single-stranded RNA template"]', 3, 'Reverse transcriptase reversely transcribes single-stranded RNA into DNA and then completes a DNA double helix.', '{"0": "RNA from DNA is ordinary transcription.", "1": "Protein from mRNA is translation.", "2": "The envelope is derived from the host membrane."}'),
  ('BIO-0822', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 45 (§2.8)', 'Which statement about the history of AIDS is given in the book?', '["AIDS was first reported in 1990", "The agent was shown to be a bacterium in 1984", "The causative virus was named HIV in 1986", "HIV causes AIDS in monkeys"]', 2, 'AIDS was first reported in 1981; in 1984 its cause was found to be a virus, which was named HIV in 1986.', '{"0": "AIDS was first reported in 1981.", "1": "In 1984 the agent was found to be a virus.", "3": "HIV can multiply in monkeys but does not cause AIDS in them."}'),
  ('BIO-0823', 'Acellular life', 'Virus structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Bacteria and Viruses, p. 45 (§2.8)', 'AIDS is fatal mainly because', '["the virus destroys red blood cells", "the virus blocks the airways", "the virus damages the kidneys directly", "the patient''s immune system is weakened, leaving the body open to other infections"]', 3, 'AIDS weakens the immune system, and no one can survive without it to defend against other viral and bacterial infections.', '{"0": "Red blood cell destruction is not the cause given.", "1": "Airway blockage is not the cause given.", "2": "Kidney damage is not the cause given."}'),
  ('BIO-0824', 'Cell structure and function', 'Properties of life', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 49 (§3.1)', 'According to the book, cells maintain a stable internal environment (homeostasis) mainly by', '["dividing by meiosis", "producing gametes", "moving with flagella", "regulating the movement of substances across their membranes"]', 3, 'Cells maintain homeostasis by regulating the movement of substances across their membranes.', '{"0": "Meiosis is a form of reproduction.", "1": "Gamete production is part of reproduction.", "2": "Flagella are for movement."}'),
  ('BIO-0825', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell theory', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 49 (§3.2)', 'Robert Hooke, who examined a thin slice of cork in 1665, called the little boxes he saw', '["animalcules", "nuclei", "cellulae", "protoplasts"]', 2, 'Hooke concluded plant parts were made of compartments and named them "cellulae".', '{"0": "\"Animalcules\" was Leeuwenhoek''s name for living cells in pond water.", "1": "The nucleus was discovered by Robert Brown in 1831.", "3": "Protoplast is a cell without its wall."}'),
  ('BIO-0826', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell theory', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 50 (§3.2)', 'The nucleus of the cell was discovered in 1831 by', '["Robert Hooke", "Robert Brown", "Theodor Schwann", "Anton van Leeuwenhoek"]', 1, 'In 1831 the British botanist Robert Brown discovered the nucleus.', '{"0": "Hooke described cork cells in 1665.", "2": "Schwann concluded that animals are made of cells.", "3": "Leeuwenhoek observed living cells in pond water."}'),
  ('BIO-0827', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 50 (§3.2)', 'Who concluded in 1838 that all plants are composed of cells?', '["Matthias Schleiden", "Theodor Schwann", "Rudolf Virchow", "Louis Pasteur"]', 0, 'The German botanist Matthias Schleiden concluded in 1838 that all plants are made of cells; Schwann said the same for animals the next year.', '{"1": "Schwann''s conclusion was about animals.", "2": "Virchow observed that all cells come from other cells.", "3": "Pasteur gave experimental proof that cells come from cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0828', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 50 (§3.2)', 'Experimental proof that cells arise only from pre-existing cells was provided in 1862 by', '["Rudolf Virchow", "Louis Pasteur", "Robert Brown", "Jean Baptiste de Lamarck"]', 1, 'Virchow observed that all cells come from other cells, and in 1862 Louis Pasteur provided the experimental proof.', '{"0": "Virchow made the observation but not the experimental proof.", "2": "Brown discovered the nucleus.", "3": "Lamarck observed cells in 1809."}'),
  ('BIO-0829', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 51 (§3.2)', 'Which of these is given in the book as an exception to the cell theory?', '["Red blood cells that carry oxygen", "Bacteria that divide by binary fission", "Vertebrate muscle cells that fuse to form multinucleated fibres", "Plant cells with a cellulose wall"]', 2, 'Myocytes that fuse into multinucleated fibres challenge the idea of a single cell as the basic unit; viruses, prions, organelle DNA and multinucleate fungi and algae are other exceptions.', '{"0": "Red blood cells are not given as an exception.", "1": "Bacteria dividing from pre-existing cells fit the theory.", "3": "A cellulose wall does not challenge the theory."}'),
  ('BIO-0830', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 51 (§3.2)', 'Mitochondria and chloroplasts are seen as exceptions to the cell theory because they', '["have no membranes", "are made only of protein", "are found outside cells", "have their own DNA and can replicate independently of the nucleus"]', 3, 'These organelles have their own DNA and replicate independently, suggesting they came from free-living prokaryotes.', '{"0": "Both are bounded by two membranes.", "1": "Protein-only agents are prions.", "2": "They are inside eukaryotic cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0831', 'Cell structure and function', 'Microscopy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 51 (§3.3)', 'The ability of a microscope to distinguish between two points that are close together is its', '["resolution", "magnification", "focal length", "illumination"]', 0, 'Resolution is the ability to distinguish between two close points; magnification is enlargement of the image.', '{"1": "Magnification is how much larger the image appears.", "2": "Focal length is not defined in the book.", "3": "Illumination is the light or electron source."}'),
  ('BIO-0832', 'Cell structure and function', 'Microscopy', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 51 (§3.3)', 'Total magnification of a light microscope is found by', '["adding the magnifications of all the lenses", "dividing the objective by the eyepiece magnification", "using only the objective lens value", "multiplying the magnifications of all the lenses"]', 3, 'Total magnification is determined by multiplying the magnification of all lenses.', '{"0": "Magnifications are multiplied, not added.", "1": "Division is not used.", "2": "Both lenses contribute."}'),
  ('BIO-0833', 'Cell structure and function', 'Microscopy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 52 (§3.3)', 'The resolving power of the light microscope given in the book is', '["0.2 nm", "1 mm", "0.2 µm", "2 µm"]', 2, 'The light microscope''s resolving power is 0.2 micrometre; it cannot distinguish objects smaller than this.', '{"0": "0.2 nm is the resolution of the electron microscope.", "1": "About 1 mm is the resolution of the naked eye.", "3": "2 µm is ten times coarser than the value given."}'),
  ('BIO-0834', 'Cell structure and function', 'Microscopy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 52 (§3.3)', 'Why can the electron microscope NOT be used to view living material?', '["Electrons cannot pass through cells", "The methods needed to prepare the specimen kill it", "Its resolution is too low", "It uses visible light"]', 1, 'The electron microscope cannot view living material because of the methods needed to prepare specimens.', '{"0": "TEM transmits electrons through thin specimens.", "2": "Its resolution is much higher than the light microscope.", "3": "It uses an electron beam, not light."}'),
  ('BIO-0835', 'Cell structure and function', 'Microscopy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 52 (§3.3)', 'The scanning electron microscope (SEM) is used mainly to study', '["the surfaces of cells and objects", "the internal structure of very thin sections", "living cells in motion", "colour of stained tissue"]', 0, 'SEM studies surface detail; surfaces are coated with metal that reflects the electron beam. TEM shows internal structure.', '{"1": "Internal structure in thin specimens is studied by TEM.", "2": "Electron microscopes cannot view living cells.", "3": "Electron images are not coloured by stains."}'),
  ('BIO-0836', 'Cell structure and function', 'Microscopy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 52 (§3.3)', 'According to the book, a transmission electron microscope can magnify objects up to about', '["1,500 times", "10,000,000 times", "100,000 times", "250,000 times"]', 3, 'TEM can magnify up to 250,000 times; SEM up to 100,000 times; the light microscope about 1500X.', '{"0": "1500X is the light microscope.", "1": "This is far above the value given.", "2": "100,000 times is the SEM figure."}'),
  ('BIO-0837', 'Cell structure and function', 'Microscopy', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 54 (§3.3)', 'In the electron microscope, the image is received on a', '["ocular lens", "glass slide", "condenser", "zinc sulphate fluorescent screen"]', 3, 'The book''s comparison table says the electron microscope image is received on a zinc sulphate fluorescent screen; in a light microscope it is seen through the ocular lens.', '{"0": "The ocular lens is used in the light microscope.", "1": "The slide holds the light-microscope specimen.", "2": "The condenser focuses light onto the specimen."}'),
  ('BIO-0838', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell wall', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 54 (§3.4)', 'When the cell wall is removed by wall-degrading enzymes, the remaining part of the cell is called a', '["protoplast", "middle lamella", "tonoplast", "plasmodesma"]', 0, 'The remaining components after removing the wall are called a protoplast.', '{"1": "The middle lamella is a layer between neighbouring walls.", "2": "The tonoplast is the vacuole membrane.", "3": "A plasmodesma is a channel through walls."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0839', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell wall', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 54 (§3.4)', 'The primary cell wall of plants is composed of', '["lignin, cellulose and hemicellulose", "cellulose, hemicellulose and pectin", "calcium and magnesium pectates only", "chitin and protein"]', 1, 'The primary wall is made of the polysaccharides cellulose, hemicellulose and pectin, with many proteins.', '{"0": "Lignin with cellulose and hemicellulose describes the secondary wall.", "2": "Ca and Mg pectates make the middle lamella.", "3": "Chitin is the fungal wall material."}'),
  ('BIO-0840', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell wall', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 55 (§3.4)', 'The middle lamella between plant cells is made mainly of', '["lignin", "cellulose microfibrils", "calcium and magnesium pectates", "peptidoglycan"]', 2, 'The middle lamella, formed from the cell plate in cytokinesis, contains magnesium and calcium pectates.', '{"0": "Lignin is in the secondary wall.", "1": "Cellulose microfibrils are in the primary wall.", "3": "Peptidoglycan is a bacterial wall polymer."}'),
  ('BIO-0841', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell wall', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 55 (§3.4)', 'The secondary wall of a plant cell is more rigid than the primary wall because it contains', '["lignin", "pectin", "chitin", "silica"]', 0, 'Due to lignin, the secondary wall is more rigid than the primary wall.', '{"1": "Pectin is in the primary wall and middle lamella.", "2": "Chitin is in fungal walls.", "3": "Silica walls are made by diatoms."}'),
  ('BIO-0842', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell wall', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 55 (§3.4)', 'Plasmodesmata are formed during cell division when', '["the nuclear envelope breaks", "parts of the endoplasmic reticulum are trapped in the new cell wall", "Golgi vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane", "lignin is deposited"]', 1, 'Plasmodesmata form when parts of the parent cell''s endoplasmic reticulum get trapped in the new wall.', '{"0": "Breakdown of the nuclear envelope is not the cause.", "2": "Vesicle fusion is exocytosis.", "3": "Lignin deposition makes the secondary wall."}'),
  ('BIO-0843', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell wall', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 55 (§3.4)', 'Diatoms make their cell walls from', '["cellulose only", "chitin", "silicic acid", "peptidoglycan"]', 2, 'Diatoms synthesise their walls from silicic acid; silica walls need less energy, so diatoms grow fast.', '{"0": "Algal walls generally contain cellulose, but diatoms use silica.", "1": "Chitin is the fungal wall material.", "3": "Peptidoglycan forms bacterial walls."}'),
  ('BIO-0844', 'Cell structure and function', 'Plasma membrane', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 56 (§3.4)', 'The fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane was proposed in 1972 by', '["Schleiden and Schwann", "Watson and Crick", "Margulis and Schwartz", "Singer and Nicolson"]', 3, 'S. J. Singer and Garth Nicolson proposed the fluid mosaic model in 1972.', '{"0": "Schleiden and Schwann contributed to the cell theory.", "1": "Watson and Crick are linked to DNA structure.", "2": "Margulis and Schwartz classified prokaryotes."}'),
  ('BIO-0845', 'Cell structure and function', 'Plasma membrane', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 57 (§3.4)', 'In the lipid bilayer of the plasma membrane, the phospholipids are arranged with', '["hydrophobic tails pointing inwards and hydrophilic phosphate heads facing outwards", "hydrophilic heads inwards and tails outwards", "all molecules lying flat in one layer", "tails facing the cytoplasm only"]', 0, 'The hydrophobic tails are oriented inwards and the hydrophilic phosphate groups face the cytoplasm or the outside.', '{"1": "This is the reverse of the arrangement.", "2": "It is a bilayer, not a single flat layer.", "3": "The tails face each other in the middle of the bilayer."}'),
  ('BIO-0846', 'Cell structure and function', 'Plasma membrane', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 57 (§3.4)', 'In eukaryotic plasma membranes, cholesterol molecules wedged in the bilayer', '["act as receptors for hormones", "keep the membrane fluid at low temperatures", "form channels for ions", "attach the membrane to the cell wall"]', 1, 'Cholesterol in eukaryotic membranes keeps the fluidity of the membrane at low temperatures.', '{"0": "Receptor proteins bind messenger molecules.", "2": "Channel proteins form passages for ions.", "3": "Attachment proteins link the membrane to the cytoskeleton and external fibres."}'),
  ('BIO-0847', 'Cell structure and function', 'Plasma membrane', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 58 (§3.4)', 'Glycoproteins and glycolipids on the outer surface of the membrane are collectively called the', '["cytoskeleton", "middle lamella", "glycocalyx", "nuclear lamina"]', 2, 'The glycolipids and glycoproteins, collectively the glycocalyx, act as cell identification marks.', '{"0": "The cytoskeleton is a network of protein fibres inside the cell.", "1": "The middle lamella joins plant cell walls.", "3": "The nuclear lamina lines the inner nuclear envelope."}'),
  ('BIO-0848', 'Cell structure and function', 'Plasma membrane', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 58 (§3.4)', 'Which molecules can pass freely across the plasma membrane, according to the book?', '["Amino acids and sugars", "Proteins and nucleic acids", "Starch and glycogen", "Oxygen, carbon dioxide and water"]', 3, 'Small molecules such as oxygen, carbon dioxide and water pass freely; the passage of larger molecules like amino acids and sugars is regulated.', '{"0": "Amino acids and sugars are carefully regulated.", "1": "These large molecules do not pass freely.", "2": "Polysaccharides are too large to pass freely."}'),
  ('BIO-0849', 'Cell structure and function', 'Plasma membrane', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 59 (§3.4)', 'The technique that bleaches a fluorescently labelled area of membrane with a laser and watches the fluorescence return is used to study', '["the mobility of membrane proteins and lipids", "the atomic structure of membrane proteins", "the surface topology of cells", "the total lipid composition by mass spectrometry"]', 0, 'Fluorescence Recovery After Photobleaching studies the mobility and dynamics of membrane proteins and lipids.', '{"1": "Atomic structure is found by X-ray crystallography.", "2": "Surface topology is studied by SEM.", "3": "Lipid analysis by mass spectrometry is lipidomics."}'),
  ('BIO-0850', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 59 (§3.4)', 'Which reaction is given in the book as an example of a metabolic reaction that takes place in the cytoplasm?', '["Krebs cycle", "Photosynthesis", "Glycolysis", "Replication of mitochondrial DNA"]', 2, 'The cytoplasm is the site of various metabolic reactions, for example glycolysis.', '{"0": "The Krebs cycle is catalysed by enzymes in the mitochondrial matrix.", "1": "Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts.", "3": "Mitochondrial DNA replicates in the mitochondrion."}'),
  ('BIO-0851', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 60 (§3.4)', 'The nucleus typically occupies about what fraction of a eukaryotic cell''s volume?', '["1 percent", "10 percent", "50 percent", "80 percent"]', 1, 'The spherical nucleus typically occupies about 10 percent of a eukaryotic cell''s volume.', '{"0": "1 percent is too small.", "2": "50 percent is too large.", "3": "80 percent or more is the plant central vacuole."}'),
  ('BIO-0852', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 61 (§3.4)', 'The protein lining on the inner side of the nuclear envelope that binds chromatin is the', '["nucleolus", "perinuclear space", "nuclear basket", "nuclear lamina"]', 3, 'On the inner side of the nuclear envelope is a protein lining, the nuclear lamina, which binds chromatin for structural support.', '{"0": "The nucleolus makes ribosomal subunits.", "1": "The perinuclear space is between the two membranes.", "2": "The basket is made of fibrils on the pore complex."}'),
  ('BIO-0853', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 61 (§3.4)', 'The space between the two membranes of the nuclear envelope is continuous with the', '["Golgi lumen", "lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum", "mitochondrial matrix", "cytosol"]', 1, 'The perinuclear space is connected with the rough endoplasmic reticulum.', '{"0": "The Golgi lumen is separate.", "2": "The mitochondrial matrix is inside mitochondria.", "3": "The cytosol lies outside the envelope."}'),
  ('BIO-0854', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 61 (§3.4)', 'In the nuclear pore complex, the subunit that anchors the complex into the nuclear envelope is the', '["lumenal subunit", "annular subunit", "column subunit", "ring subunit"]', 0, 'The lumenal subunit anchors the pore complex into the nuclear envelope.', '{"1": "The annular subunit surrounds the inside of the pore.", "2": "The column subunit makes the wall of the complex.", "3": "The ring subunit is attached to the outer side of the column subunit."}'),
  ('BIO-0855', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 61 (§3.4)', 'Nucleoli are formed at chromosome sites called nucleolus organizer regions, whose DNA codes for', '["histone proteins", "ribosomal RNA", "transfer RNA", "nuclear pore proteins"]', 1, 'Nucleoli form at NORs; the DNA at NORs encodes ribosomal RNA (rRNA).', '{"0": "Histones are not coded at NORs in the book.", "2": "tRNA is not given as the NOR product.", "3": "Pore proteins are not coded there."}'),
  ('BIO-0856', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 61 (§3.4)', 'A cell that produces large amounts of protein is likely to have', '["no nucleolus", "few ribosomes", "no rough ER", "a large nucleolus"]', 3, 'Cells making large amounts of protein need many ribosomes, so their nucleolus is considerable in size.', '{"0": "Such cells need nucleoli to make ribosome subunits.", "1": "They need many ribosomes.", "2": "Protein-making cells have extensive RER."}'),
  ('BIO-0857', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 62 (§3.4)', 'The bead-like units of chromatin, in which DNA is wrapped around histone proteins, are called', '["centromeres", "chromatids", "nucleosomes", "nucleoli"]', 2, 'Chromatin is a series of bead-like nucleosomes in which DNA wraps around groups of histones.', '{"0": "The centromere is the central point of a chromosome.", "1": "Chromatids are the arms of a chromosome.", "3": "Nucleoli make ribosome subunits."}'),
  ('BIO-0858', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 62 (§3.4)', 'The genetically active form of chromatin, involved in transcribing RNA, is', '["euchromatin", "heterochromatin", "centromere", "nucleolus organizer"]', 0, 'Euchromatin is genetically active; heterochromatin is genetically inactive.', '{"1": "Heterochromatin is inactive.", "2": "The centromere is a chromosome region, not a kind of chromatin.", "3": "The organizer region codes for rRNA."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0859', 'Cell structure and function', 'Nucleus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 63 (§3.4)', 'The Indian fern Ophioglossum reticulatum, with 1,260 chromosomes, illustrates that', '["plants always have more chromosomes than animals", "ferns are the most complex plants", "chromosome number is the same in all species", "chromosome number does not correlate with the complexity of an organism"]', 3, 'The book contrasts 2 chromosomes in some ants and roundworms with 1,260 in this fern to show number does not track complexity.', '{"0": "The book does not claim this.", "1": "Number does not indicate complexity.", "2": "Chromosome number is species-specific."}'),
  ('BIO-0860', 'Cell structure and function', 'Endoplasmic reticulum', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 63 (§3.4)', 'The flattened sacs and tubules of the endoplasmic reticulum are called', '["cristae", "cisternae", "thylakoids", "grana"]', 1, 'The sacs and tubules of the ER are called cisternae; together they form one lumen, the cisternal space.', '{"0": "Cristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane.", "2": "Thylakoids are disc-shaped compartments in chloroplasts.", "3": "Grana are stacks of thylakoids."}'),
  ('BIO-0861', 'Cell structure and function', 'Endoplasmic reticulum', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 64 (§3.4)', 'Smooth endoplasmic reticulum is especially extensive in liver cells because of its role in', '["making ribosomes", "secreting digestive enzymes", "detoxification of drugs and poisons", "photosynthesis"]', 2, 'SER is chiefly involved in lipid production, carbohydrate metabolism and detoxification, and is extensive in liver cells.', '{"0": "Ribosome subunits are made in the nucleolus.", "1": "Digestive enzymes are proteins made on RER.", "3": "Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts."}'),
  ('BIO-0862', 'Cell structure and function', 'Endoplasmic reticulum', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 64 (§3.4)', 'In muscle cells, the smooth endoplasmic reticulum triggers contraction by releasing', '["sodium", "potassium", "calcium", "glucose"]', 2, 'SER stores calcium, and in muscle cells it releases calcium to trigger muscle contraction.', '{"0": "Sodium release is not described.", "1": "Potassium release is not described.", "3": "Glucose is not the trigger."}'),
  ('BIO-0863', 'Cell structure and function', 'Ribosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 64 (§3.4)', 'Ribosomes are composed of approximately', '["60 percent rRNA and 40 percent protein", "40 percent rRNA and 60 percent protein", "100 percent protein", "60 percent DNA and 40 percent protein"]', 0, 'Ribosomes are made of about 60 percent ribosomal RNA and 40 percent protein.', '{"1": "This reverses the proportions.", "2": "Ribosomes contain rRNA.", "3": "Ribosomes do not contain DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0864', 'Cell structure and function', 'Ribosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 65 (§3.4)', 'Proteins made by ribosomes bound to the rough ER are mainly', '["used inside the cytosol only", "packed into the nucleolus", "built into the cell wall of bacteria", "transported outside the cell"]', 3, 'Proteins from free ribosomes are for internal use; proteins from RER-bound ribosomes are transported out of the cell.', '{"0": "Free ribosomes make proteins for internal use.", "1": "The nucleolus makes ribosome subunits.", "2": "Bacteria have no RER."}'),
  ('BIO-0865', 'Cell structure and function', 'Ribosomes', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 65 (§3.4)', 'The Svedberg values of ribosomal subunits do not add up to that of the whole ribosome because', '["the subunits lose RNA when they join", "sedimentation rate depends on size and shape, not just molecular weight", "only eukaryotic ribosomes have subunits", "Svedberg units measure volume"]', 1, 'The rate of sedimentation depends on size and shape rather than simply molecular weight, so S values are not additive.', '{"0": "Subunits do not lose RNA on joining.", "2": "Prokaryotic ribosomes also have 30S and 50S subunits.", "3": "S values measure sedimentation rate."}'),
  ('BIO-0866', 'Cell structure and function', 'Ribosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 65 (§3.4)', 'In the cytoplasm of a eukaryotic cell, the complete ribosome is', '["70S, with 30S and 50S subunits", "80S, with 30S and 50S subunits", "70S, with 40S and 60S subunits", "80S, with 40S and 60S subunits"]', 3, 'The eukaryotic ribosome is 80S, with a 40S small subunit and a 60S large subunit.', '{"0": "70S with 30S and 50S is the prokaryotic ribosome.", "1": "The 80S ribosome has 40S and 60S subunits.", "2": "A 70S ribosome has 30S and 50S subunits."}'),
  ('BIO-0867', 'Cell structure and function', 'Ribosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 65 (§3.4)', 'Ribosomes inside mitochondria and chloroplasts are', '["more like prokaryotic ribosomes than cytoplasmic eukaryotic ones", "identical to cytoplasmic 80S ribosomes", "absent", "bound to rough ER"]', 0, 'Ribosomes in mitochondria and chloroplasts differ in size and makeup from cytoplasmic ones and resemble prokaryotic ribosomes.', '{"1": "They differ from cytoplasmic ribosomes.", "2": "They are present.", "3": "They lie free inside the organelle."}'),
  ('BIO-0868', 'Cell structure and function', 'Mitochondria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 66 (§3.4)', 'The knob-like F-1 particles on the inner surface of the cristae are', '["ribosomes", "ATP synthase enzymes", "DNA molecules", "Krebs cycle enzymes in the matrix"]', 1, 'F-1 particles on the cristae are actually the enzymes called ATP synthase.', '{"0": "Mitochondrial ribosomes are 70S and lie in the matrix.", "2": "Mitochondrial DNA is circular and in the matrix.", "3": "Krebs cycle enzymes are in the matrix, not the F-1 particles."}'),
  ('BIO-0869', 'Cell structure and function', 'Mitochondria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 66 (§3.4)', 'In a mitochondrion, the enzymes of the Krebs cycle are found in the', '["outer membrane", "intermembrane space", "matrix", "F-1 particles"]', 2, 'Enzymes in the matrix catalyse some steps of respiration such as the Krebs cycle; electron transport proteins are on the inner membrane.', '{"0": "The outer membrane acts like a sieve.", "1": "The intermembrane space lies between the two membranes.", "3": "F-1 particles are ATP synthase."}'),
  ('BIO-0870', 'Cell structure and function', 'Mitochondria', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 66 (§3.4)', 'Which feature of a mitochondrion resembles prokaryotes?', '["A double nuclear envelope", "80S ribosomes", "Linear chromosomes with histones", "Circular DNA and 70S ribosomes"]', 3, 'A mitochondrion has its own circular DNA, similar to prokaryotes, all kinds of RNA and 70S ribosomes.', '{"0": "Mitochondria do not have a nucleus.", "1": "80S ribosomes are cytoplasmic eukaryotic ribosomes.", "2": "Linear chromosomes with histones are nuclear eukaryotic DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0871', 'Cell structure and function', 'Chloroplasts', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 66 (§3.4)', 'Colourless plastids involved in storage are', '["leucoplasts", "chromoplasts", "chloroplasts", "glyoxysomes"]', 0, 'Colourless leucoplasts store materials; chromoplasts give yellow-to-red colours.', '{"1": "Chromoplasts are coloured.", "2": "Chloroplasts are green and photosynthetic.", "3": "Glyoxysomes are not plastids."}'),
  ('BIO-0872', 'Cell structure and function', 'Chloroplasts', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 67 (§3.4)', 'In a chloroplast, the semi-fluid inside the inner membrane that contains dissolved enzymes is the', '["thylakoid", "stroma", "granum", "matrix"]', 1, 'The stroma is a semi-fluid inside the inner membrane containing dissolved enzymes; it makes up most of the chloroplast volume.', '{"0": "A thylakoid is a disc-shaped membrane compartment.", "2": "A granum is a stack of thylakoids.", "3": "Matrix is the term used for mitochondria."}'),
  ('BIO-0873', 'Cell structure and function', 'Chloroplasts', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 67 (§3.4)', 'The non-green parts that connect two grana in a chloroplast are the', '["cristae", "cisternae", "stroma lamellae", "thylakoid lumen"]', 2, 'The lamellae are the non-green compartments that connect two grana.', '{"0": "Cristae are in mitochondria.", "1": "Cisternae belong to the ER and Golgi.", "3": "The lumen is inside a thylakoid."}'),
  ('BIO-0874', 'Cell structure and function', 'Chloroplasts', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 67 (§3.4)', 'Which statement about the two chloroplast membranes is correct according to the book?', '["The inner membrane is much more permeable than the outer", "Both membranes are folded into cristae", "Only one membrane is present", "The outer membrane is much more permeable than the inner one"]', 3, 'The chloroplast is enclosed by two membranes and the outer membrane is much more permeable than the inner.', '{"0": "This reverses the permeability.", "1": "Cristae are mitochondrial.", "2": "There are two membranes."}'),
  ('BIO-0875', 'Cell structure and function', 'Golgi apparatus', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 68 (§3.4)', 'The Golgi apparatus was discovered by', '["Christian de Duve", "Camillo Golgi", "Robert Brown", "Rudolf Virchow"]', 1, 'Camillo Golgi, using a new staining technique on the nervous system, observed the structure and announced it in 1898.', '{"0": "De Duve discovered lysosomes and peroxisomes.", "2": "Brown discovered the nucleus.", "3": "Virchow said cells come from cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0876', 'Cell structure and function', 'Golgi apparatus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 68 (§3.4)', 'The ''cis'' face of a Golgi stack is located', '["near the endoplasmic reticulum", "near the plasma membrane", "inside the nucleus", "inside the vacuole"]', 0, 'The cis face lies near the ER and receives vesicles; the trans face is near the plasma membrane.', '{"1": "The trans face is near the plasma membrane.", "2": "The Golgi is in the cytoplasm.", "3": "It is not inside the vacuole."}'),
  ('BIO-0877', 'Cell structure and function', 'Golgi apparatus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 69 (§3.4)', 'In plant cells, the Golgi apparatus also produces', '["cellulose microfibrils in the nucleus", "chlorophyll", "pectin and other polysaccharides", "lignin in mitochondria"]', 2, 'In plant cells, the Golgi produces pectin and other polysaccharides needed for plant structure.', '{"0": "Cellulose is not said to be made in the nucleus.", "1": "Chlorophyll is in chloroplasts.", "3": "Lignin is not made in mitochondria."}'),
  ('BIO-0878', 'Cell structure and function', 'Golgi apparatus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 68 (§3.4)', 'Animal cells generally contain how many Golgi stacks?', '["One only", "Between ten and twenty", "About sixty", "Thousands"]', 1, 'Animal cells generally contain between ten and twenty Golgi stacks; some unicellular flagellates have up to 60 cisternae.', '{"0": "Cells have more than one stack.", "2": "60 refers to cisternae in some flagellates.", "3": "Thousands is not given."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0879', 'Cell structure and function', 'Lysosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 69 (§3.4)', 'Lysosomes are most numerous in which animal cells?', '["Red blood cells", "Bone cells", "Fat cells", "White blood cells"]', 3, 'Lysosomes are most numerous in disease-fighting cells such as white blood cells, which digest bacteria and viruses.', '{"0": "Red blood cells are not mentioned.", "1": "Bone cells are not mentioned.", "2": "Fat cells are not mentioned."}'),
  ('BIO-0880', 'Cell structure and function', 'Lysosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 69 (§3.4)', 'If a lysosome bursts, the cell usually remains uninjured because', '["the enzymes are destroyed by ribosomes", "the enzymes cannot leave the lysosome membrane", "lysosomal enzymes need an acidic pH while the cytosol is neutral", "the cytosol contains no macromolecules"]', 2, 'Lysosomal enzymes require acidic conditions; the cytosol is neutral, so released enzymes become inactive.', '{"0": "Ribosomes do not destroy enzymes.", "1": "A burst lysosome releases its enzymes.", "3": "The cytosol contains many macromolecules."}'),
  ('BIO-0881', 'Cell structure and function', 'Lysosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 69 (§3.4)', 'The breakdown by lysosomes of cell materials that have exceeded their lifetime is called', '["autophagy", "phagocytosis", "pinocytosis", "exocytosis"]', 0, 'Lysosomes break down cellular materials that are no longer useful; this is autophagy.', '{"1": "Phagocytosis takes in solid material from outside.", "2": "Pinocytosis takes in liquid droplets.", "3": "Exocytosis exports material out of the cell."}'),
  ('BIO-0882', 'Cell structure and function', 'Lysosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 70 (§3.4)', 'Lysosomes were discovered when Christian de Duve noticed that damaged liver cells released', '["catalase", "acid phosphatase", "ATP synthase", "acetylcholine"]', 1, 'De Duve observed that damaged liver cells released acid phosphatase and suggested it was enclosed in a membrane-bound organelle, the lysosome.', '{"0": "Catalase is a peroxisome enzyme.", "2": "ATP synthase is in mitochondria.", "3": "Acetylcholine is not mentioned."}'),
  ('BIO-0883', 'Cell structure and function', 'Lysosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 71 (§3.4)', 'In Pompe''s disease, lysosomes lack an enzyme that digests', '["lipids in nerve cells", "hydrogen peroxide", "glycogen", "proteins in muscle"]', 2, 'In Pompe''s disease the lysosome lacks a glycogen-digesting enzyme, so glycogen accumulates in liver cells.', '{"0": "A missing lipid-digesting enzyme causes Tay-Sachs disease.", "1": "Hydrogen peroxide is broken down by peroxisomal catalase.", "3": "This is not the enzyme named."}'),
  ('BIO-0884', 'Cell structure and function', 'Lysosomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 71 (§3.4)', 'Destruction of tadpole tail cells during metamorphosis is carried out by', '["lysosomal enzymes", "peroxisomal catalase", "glyoxysomes", "ribosomes"]', 0, 'Selective cell death in development uses lysosomes; tail cells of a developing tadpole are destroyed by lysosomal enzymes.', '{"1": "Catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide.", "2": "Glyoxysomes are plant organelles.", "3": "Ribosomes make proteins."}'),
  ('BIO-0885', 'Cell structure and function', 'Peroxisomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 71 (§3.4)', 'Zellweger syndrome is caused by', '["missing glycogen-digesting enzyme in lysosomes", "defective ribosomes", "absence of centrioles", "absence or reduced number of peroxisomes"]', 3, 'Zellweger syndrome is characterised by absence or reduced number of peroxisomes; it is congenital and usually fatal in the first year.', '{"0": "That is Pompe''s disease.", "1": "Ribosome defects are not given.", "2": "Centriole absence is normal in plant cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0886', 'Cell structure and function', 'Peroxisomes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 71 (§3.4)', 'Glyoxysomes, found only in plant cells, are most abundant in', '["root hair cells", "lipid-rich seeds such as castor beans", "guard cells", "xylem vessels"]', 1, 'Glyoxysomes convert lipids into carbohydrates and are most abundant in lipid-rich seeds like castor beans and soybeans.', '{"0": "Root hairs are not named.", "2": "Guard cells are not named.", "3": "Xylem vessels are dead conducting cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0887', 'Cell structure and function', 'Vacuoles', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 72 (§3.4)', 'The membrane surrounding the central vacuole of a plant cell is the', '["plasmalemma", "nuclear envelope", "tonoplast", "thylakoid membrane"]', 2, 'The plant vacuole is enclosed by a membrane called the tonoplast; its contents are cell sap.', '{"0": "Plasmalemma is another name for the plasma membrane.", "1": "The nuclear envelope surrounds the nucleus.", "3": "Thylakoid membranes are in chloroplasts."}'),
  ('BIO-0888', 'Cell structure and function', 'Vacuoles', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 72 (§3.4)', 'The central vacuole supports a plant mainly by', '["storing calcium for contraction", "making cellulose", "producing ATP", "building up turgor pressure as it swells with water"]', 3, 'The vacuole''s structural role is linked to its control of turgor pressure, which keeps the plant firm along with the cell wall.', '{"0": "Calcium release for contraction is an SER function.", "1": "Cellulose is not made in the vacuole.", "2": "ATP is made in mitochondria and chloroplasts."}'),
  ('BIO-0889', 'Cell structure and function', 'Centrioles', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 73 (§3.4)', 'In ciliated cells, the centriole that anchors each cilium is called the', '["basal body", "centromere", "axoneme", "nucleosome"]', 0, 'Each cilium and flagellum is anchored by a centriole known as the basal body.', '{"1": "The centromere is part of a chromosome.", "2": "The axoneme is the core of the cilium itself.", "3": "A nucleosome is a unit of chromatin."}'),
  ('BIO-0890', 'Cell structure and function', 'Centrioles', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 73 (§3.4)', 'Which cells lack centrioles?', '["Animal cells", "Cells of plants and fungi", "Most protists", "Ciliated cells of the trachea"]', 1, 'Plant and fungal cells lack centrioles and basal bodies; their microtubules are organised by other cytoplasmic structures.', '{"0": "Animal cells have centrioles.", "2": "Most protists have centrioles.", "3": "Ciliated cells have basal bodies (centrioles)."}'),
  ('BIO-0891', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cytoskeleton', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 73 (§3.4)', 'Microfilaments are solid rods made of the protein', '["actin", "tubulin", "vimentin", "keratin"]', 0, 'Microfilaments are solid rods made of actin; with myosin they help in contraction.', '{"1": "Tubulin forms microtubules.", "2": "Vimentin is the common intermediate filament protein.", "3": "Keratin forms intermediate filaments in skin cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0892', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cytoskeleton', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 73 (§3.4)', 'Microtubules are hollow cylinders whose subunits contain', '["actin and myosin", "vimentin", "flagellin", "alpha-tubulin and beta-tubulin"]', 3, 'Each microtubule subunit is made of alpha-tubulin and beta-tubulin.', '{"0": "Actin forms microfilaments and myosin works with them.", "1": "Vimentin forms intermediate filaments.", "2": "Flagellin forms prokaryotic flagella."}'),
  ('BIO-0893', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cytoskeleton', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 73 (§3.4)', 'Intermediate filaments in skin cells are made of the protein', '["actin", "tubulin", "keratin", "collagen"]', 2, 'The most common intermediate filament protein is vimentin, but skin cells contain keratin.', '{"0": "Actin forms microfilaments.", "1": "Tubulin forms microtubules.", "3": "Collagen is not given as a cytoskeletal protein."}'),
  ('BIO-0894', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cilia and flagella', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 74 (§3.4)', 'In the axoneme of a eukaryotic cilium, the molecules that bridge adjacent microtubule doublets are', '["flagellin", "dynein", "histones", "actin"]', 1, 'Dynein molecules around the axoneme bridge the gaps between adjacent microtubule doublets.', '{"0": "Flagellin builds prokaryotic flagella.", "2": "Histones package DNA.", "3": "Actin forms microfilaments."}'),
  ('BIO-0895', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cilia and flagella', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 75 (§3.4)', 'The basal body differs from the axoneme in that its nine outer sets are', '["doublets of microtubules with a central pair", "single microtubules", "triplets of microtubules, with no central pair", "made of flagellin"]', 2, 'The basal body has the outer ring structure of the axoneme, but each of the nine sets is a triplet rather than a doublet; it is a centriole.', '{"0": "Doublets with a central pair describe the axoneme (9+2).", "1": "The sets are triplets.", "3": "Flagellin is a prokaryotic flagellar protein."}'),
  ('BIO-0896', 'Cell structure and function', 'Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 75 (§3.5)', 'Which structure is found in BOTH prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?', '["Golgi apparatus", "Mitochondria", "Nuclear envelope", "Ribosomes"]', 3, 'Both kinds of cells have DNA, a plasma membrane and ribosomes; prokaryotes lack membrane-bounded organelles.', '{"0": "Prokaryotes lack a Golgi apparatus.", "1": "Prokaryotes lack mitochondria.", "2": "Prokaryotes lack a nucleus."}'),
  ('BIO-0897', 'Cell structure and function', 'Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 75 (§3.5)', 'According to the book, most prokaryotic cells are', '["2 to 8 µm long, about one-tenth the size of a typical eukaryotic cell", "about the same size as eukaryotic cells", "ten times larger than eukaryotic cells", "20 to 250 nm across"]', 0, 'Most prokaryotic cells range from 2 to 8 µm, about one-tenth the size of a typical eukaryotic cell.', '{"1": "Prokaryotic cells are much smaller.", "2": "They are smaller, not larger.", "3": "20 to 250 nm is the size range of viruses."}'),
  ('BIO-0898', 'Cell structure and function', 'Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 76 (§3.5)', 'In a photosynthetic prokaryote, chlorophyll is', '["enclosed in chloroplasts", "stored in the vacuole", "located in mitochondria", "scattered in the cytoplasm, not in chloroplasts"]', 3, 'The comparison table says prokaryotes lack chloroplasts and their chlorophyll is scattered in the cytoplasm.', '{"0": "Prokaryotes have no chloroplasts.", "1": "Prokaryotes have no vacuole for this.", "2": "Prokaryotes have no mitochondria."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0899', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell signalling', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 77 (§3.6)', 'In cell signalling, a molecule such as cAMP that carries the signal from the receptor to target molecules inside the cell is called a', '["ligand", "receptor", "second messenger", "transcription factor"]', 2, 'Small molecules like cAMP, calcium ions and IP3 act as second messengers inside the cell.', '{"0": "The ligand is the signal molecule that binds the receptor.", "1": "The receptor binds the ligand.", "3": "Transcription factors are not described as second messengers."}'),
  ('BIO-0900', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell signalling', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 77 (§3.6)', 'The correct order of the steps of cell signalling is', '["transduction, reception, response", "reception, transduction, response", "response, reception, transduction", "reception, response, transduction"]', 1, 'Signalling begins with reception (ligand binds receptor), then transduction (cascade), then cellular response.', '{"0": "Reception comes first.", "2": "Response comes last.", "3": "Transduction comes before response."}'),
  ('BIO-0901', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell signalling', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 78 (§3.6)', 'Protein or peptide signal molecules act through receptors on the plasma membrane because they', '["are water-soluble and cannot pass through the membrane", "are lipophilic and diffuse freely", "are too small to bind receptors", "act only in the nucleus"]', 0, 'Protein or peptide signals are water-soluble, so they cannot pass through the plasma membrane and bind surface receptors.', '{"1": "Lipophilic signals are steroids.", "2": "Size is not the reason given.", "3": "They act from the cell surface."}'),
  ('BIO-0902', 'Cell structure and function', 'Cell signalling', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 79 (§3.6)', 'Steroid hormones affect a target cell by', '["binding surface receptors that make cAMP", "diffusing through the membrane and binding intracellular receptors that regulate gene transcription", "opening ion channels in the membrane", "being taken in by phagocytosis"]', 1, 'Lipophilic steroid hormones diffuse in, bind receptors in cytoplasm or nucleus, and the complex binds DNA to regulate transcription.', '{"0": "Surface receptors and cAMP describe protein signalling.", "2": "Ion channels are not the steroid route.", "3": "Steroids enter by diffusion."}'),
  ('BIO-0903', 'Cell structure and function', 'Membrane transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 80 (§3.7)', 'Aquaporins are channel proteins that', '["pump sodium out of nerve cells", "carry glucose against its gradient", "allow rapid transport of water molecules", "bind LDL for endocytosis"]', 2, 'Aquaporins facilitate the rapid transport of water molecules across the membrane.', '{"0": "The sodium-potassium pump moves Na+ and K+.", "1": "Glucose transporters work by facilitated diffusion.", "3": "LDL receptors are used in receptor-mediated endocytosis."}'),
  ('BIO-0904', 'Cell structure and function', 'Membrane transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 80 (§3.7)', 'A feature of carrier proteins in facilitated diffusion is that they', '["use ATP for every molecule moved", "move substances against the gradient", "are not specific for any molecule", "can become saturated, giving a maximum rate of transport"]', 3, 'Carriers bind specific molecules, change shape and can become saturated, so there is a maximum rate.', '{"0": "Facilitated diffusion needs no energy.", "1": "It moves substances down the gradient.", "2": "Carriers are highly specific."}'),
  ('BIO-0905', 'Cell structure and function', 'Membrane transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 81 (§3.7)', 'Simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer typically involves', '["small, nonpolar molecules such as oxygen and carbon dioxide", "glucose and amino acids", "charged ions such as Na+", "proteins"]', 0, 'Simple diffusion involves small nonpolar or lipid-soluble molecules; polar or charged molecules need transport proteins.', '{"1": "Glucose and amino acids use facilitated diffusion.", "2": "Ions need channels.", "3": "Proteins are too large."}'),
  ('BIO-0906', 'Cell structure and function', 'Membrane transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 81 (§3.7)', 'A red blood cell placed in a hypotonic solution will', '["lose water by osmosis", "neither gain nor lose water", "actively pump water out", "gain water by osmosis"]', 3, 'Water moves from a hypotonic solution (less solute) to a hypertonic one, so water enters the cell.', '{"0": "Water is lost in a hypertonic solution.", "1": "No net movement occurs in an isotonic solution.", "2": "Osmosis is passive; water is not pumped."}'),
  ('BIO-0907', 'Cell structure and function', 'Membrane transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 82 (§3.7)', 'Reverse osmosis purifies water by', '["applying external pressure to push water from higher to lower solute concentration through a membrane", "heating water until it evaporates", "adding solutes to the pure side", "using aquaporins in living cells"]', 0, 'Reverse osmosis applies pressure to push water through a semi-permeable membrane from higher to lower solute concentration, leaving salts behind.', '{"1": "Evaporation is distillation.", "2": "Adding solute would not purify water.", "3": "It is a technology, not a cellular process."}'),
  ('BIO-0908', 'Cell structure and function', 'Membrane transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 82 (§3.7)', 'In a resting nerve cell, the sodium-potassium pump uses ATP to', '["move Na+ into the cell and K+ out of it", "move both ions into the cell", "move Na+ out of the cell and K+ into it", "move water into the cell"]', 2, 'The pump keeps K+ higher and Na+ lower inside the cell by moving Na+ out and K+ in.', '{"0": "This reverses the direction.", "1": "The ions move in opposite directions.", "3": "Water is not pumped."}'),
  ('BIO-0909', 'Cell structure and function', 'Membrane transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 83 (§3.7)', 'Liver cells take up LDL cholesterol from the blood by', '["simple diffusion", "receptor-mediated endocytosis", "pinocytosis of all droplets without receptors", "exocytosis"]', 1, 'Liver cells have receptor proteins for LDL and take it up by receptor-mediated endocytosis.', '{"0": "LDLs are too large for simple diffusion.", "2": "Uptake is via specific receptors.", "3": "Exocytosis exports material."}'),
  ('BIO-0910', 'Cell structure and function', 'Stem cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 84 (§3.8)', 'The zygote and the cells from its first few divisions are', '["totipotent", "pluripotent", "multipotent", "unipotent"]', 0, 'Totipotent stem cells can differentiate into all possible cell types, e.g. the zygote and its first few divisions.', '{"1": "Pluripotent cells (early embryo) can make almost any cell.", "2": "Multipotent cells make a related family.", "3": "Unipotent cells make only their own type."}'),
  ('BIO-0911', 'Cell structure and function', 'Stem cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 84 (§3.8)', 'Haematopoietic stem cells that can become red cells, white cells and platelets are', '["totipotent", "unipotent", "multipotent", "oligopotent"]', 2, 'Multipotent cells differentiate into a closely related family of cells, e.g. haematopoietic stem cells.', '{"0": "Totipotent cells form all cell types.", "1": "Unipotent cells form one type.", "3": "Oligopotent examples given are adult lymphoid or myeloid stem cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0912', 'Cell structure and function', 'Stem cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 84 (§3.8)', 'Adult muscle stem cells, which produce only their own cell type but can renew themselves, are', '["oligopotent", "unipotent", "pluripotent", "totipotent"]', 1, 'Unipotent stem cells produce only one kind of cell, their own, but are still stem cells because they self-renew.', '{"0": "Oligopotent cells form a few types.", "2": "Pluripotent cells form almost any cell.", "3": "Totipotent cells form all cell types."}'),
  ('BIO-0913', 'Cell structure and function', 'Stem cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 85 (§3.8)', 'Embryonic stem cells are obtained from the', '["bone marrow of adults", "reprogrammed skin fibroblasts", "fat tissue", "inner cell mass of the blastocyst"]', 3, 'ESCs are derived from the inner cell mass of blastocysts (early-stage embryos) and are pluripotent.', '{"0": "Bone marrow gives adult stem cells.", "1": "Reprogrammed somatic cells are iPSCs.", "2": "Fat is a source of adult stem cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0914', 'Cell structure and function', 'Stem cells', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 85 (§3.8)', 'Induced pluripotent stem cells are produced by', '["taking cells from the inner cell mass", "reprogramming adult somatic cells with specific transcription factors", "fusing two gametes", "isolating cells from bone marrow"]', 1, 'iPSCs are generated in the laboratory by reprogramming adult somatic cells to a pluripotent state using transcription factors.', '{"0": "That is how embryonic stem cells are obtained.", "2": "Fusing gametes forms a zygote.", "3": "Bone marrow gives adult stem cells."}'),
  ('BIO-0915', 'Cell structure and function', 'Stem cells', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Cells and Subcellular Organelles, p. 86 (§3.8)', 'Which is a disadvantage of induced pluripotent stem cells mentioned in the book?', '["They require destruction of human embryos", "They are always rejected by the patient''s immune system", "They could form tumours (teratomas) when transplanted", "They cannot become any other cell type"]', 2, 'Reprogramming can introduce genetic changes, and iPSCs could form teratomas after transplantation.', '{"0": "Not needing embryos is an advantage of iPSCs.", "1": "Made from the patient''s own cells, they reduce rejection.", "3": "They are pluripotent."}'),
  ('BIO-0916', 'Biological molecules', 'Biochemistry', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 92 (§4.1)', 'Breaking down complex molecules into simpler ones in metabolism is called', '["anabolism", "condensation", "polymerisation", "catabolism"]', 3, 'In catabolism complex molecules are broken down into simpler ones; anabolism builds complex substances.', '{"0": "Anabolism combines simple substances into complex ones.", "1": "Condensation joins monomers.", "2": "Polymerisation builds polymers."}'),
  ('BIO-0917', 'Biological molecules', 'Biological molecules', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 92 (§4.1)', 'Carbohydrates, proteins, lipids and nucleic acids together make up about what percentage of the dry mass of living organisms?', '["7%", "50%", "80%", "93%"]', 3, 'These four organic molecules make 93% of the dry mass; the remaining 7% is small organic and inorganic molecules.', '{"0": "7% is the remainder of small molecules.", "1": "50% is roughly the share of proteins alone.", "2": "At least 80% of the fresh mass is water."}'),
  ('BIO-0918', 'Biological molecules', 'Biological molecules', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 93 (§4.1)', 'Proteins, carbohydrates and nucleic acids are polymers of, respectively,', '["amino acids, sugars and nucleotides", "sugars, amino acids and nucleotides", "nucleotides, sugars and amino acids", "fatty acids, sugars and amino acids"]', 0, 'Carbohydrates, proteins and nucleic acids are polymers of sugars, amino acids and nucleotides.', '{"1": "The order is mixed: proteins are made of amino acids.", "2": "Proteins are not made of nucleotides.", "3": "Fatty acids are components of lipids, which are not polymers in this sense."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0919', 'Biological molecules', 'Types of bonds', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 93 (§4.2)', 'Which bond in organic molecules is described as the potential source of chemical energy for cellular activities?', '["C-O bond in glycosidic linkages", "C-N bond in peptides", "C-H bond", "Hydrogen bond"]', 2, 'The book says the carbon-hydrogen bond is the potential source of chemical energy; C-O in glycosidic linkages gives stability.', '{"0": "Glycosidic C-O linkages provide stability to complex carbohydrates.", "1": "C-N links form peptide bonds.", "3": "Hydrogen bonds are weak attractions holding structures."}'),
  ('BIO-0920', 'Biological molecules', 'Types of bonds', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 94 (§4.2)', 'Hydrophobic interactions are important for', '["dissolving salts in water", "folding of proteins and formation of lipid bilayers", "forming peptide bonds", "ionisation of water"]', 1, 'Nonpolar molecules cluster in water to minimise contact; this is crucial for protein folding and lipid bilayers.', '{"0": "Dissolving salts depends on hydrophilic interactions.", "2": "Peptide bonds are covalent bonds formed by condensation.", "3": "Ionisation involves breaking covalent bonds of water."}'),
  ('BIO-0921', 'Biological molecules', 'Condensation and hydrolysis', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 94 (§4.3)', 'During condensation (dehydration synthesis) of two monomers,', '["water is added to break a bond", "an -OH is removed from one monomer and an -H from the other", "both monomers lose -OH groups", "a phosphate group is released"]', 1, 'In condensation an -OH is removed from one monomer and an -H from another, releasing a water molecule.', '{"0": "Adding water to break a bond is hydrolysis.", "2": "Only one -OH is removed.", "3": "No phosphate is released."}'),
  ('BIO-0922', 'Biological molecules', 'Condensation and hydrolysis', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 95 (§4.3)', 'Breakdown of maltose into two glucose molecules by adding water is an example of', '["condensation", "dehydration synthesis", "ionisation", "hydrolysis"]', 3, 'Hydrolysis breaks a polymer by adding water; maltose to two glucose is the book''s example.', '{"0": "Condensation joins monomers and releases water.", "1": "Dehydration synthesis is another name for condensation.", "2": "Ionisation splits water into ions."}'),
  ('BIO-0923', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 95 (§4.4)', 'According to the book, which cells contain about 85 percent water?', '["Brain cells", "Bone cells", "Red blood cells", "Fat cells"]', 0, 'Bone cells contain about 20 percent water and brain cells about 85 percent.', '{"1": "Bone cells are about 20 percent water.", "2": "Red blood cells are not given a figure.", "3": "Fat cells are not given a figure."}'),
  ('BIO-0924', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 95 (§4.4)', 'The two properties of water that make it a good solvent are', '["its low specific heat and low density", "its covalent bonds and non-polar nature", "polarity of its molecules and their ability to form hydrogen bonds", "its ionic bonds and high melting point"]', 2, 'Water dissolves many substances because of the polarity of its molecules and its ability to form hydrogen bonds.', '{"0": "Water has a high specific heat.", "1": "Water is polar.", "3": "Water molecules are covalent, not ionic."}'),
  ('BIO-0925', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 96 (§4.4)', 'In a water molecule,', '["oxygen is partially positive and hydrogen is partially negative", "hydrogen is partially positive and oxygen is partially negative", "both atoms carry no charge", "both atoms carry full charges"]', 1, 'Hydrogen atoms are partially positive and the oxygen atom partially negative, making water polar.', '{"0": "This reverses the partial charges.", "2": "Water is polar, so its ends carry partial charges.", "3": "The charges are partial, not full."}'),
  ('BIO-0926', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 96 (§4.4)', 'According to the book, without hydrogen bonding water would boil at about', '["-80 °C", "0 °C", "4 °C", "100 °C"]', 0, 'The book notes that without hydrogen bonding water would boil at -80 °C and freeze at -100 °C, making life impossible.', '{"1": "0 °C is the actual freezing point.", "2": "4 °C is the temperature of maximum density.", "3": "100 °C is the actual boiling point."}'),
  ('BIO-0927', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 96 (§4.4)', 'Molecules such as sugars and amino acids, which dissolve readily in water, are called', '["hydrophobic", "amphoteric", "hydrophilic", "non-polar"]', 2, 'Charged or polar molecules such as salts, sugars and amino acids dissolve readily and are hydrophilic.', '{"0": "Hydrophobic molecules such as lipids do not dissolve.", "1": "Amphoteric is not the term used.", "3": "Non-polar molecules are insoluble."}'),
  ('BIO-0928', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 97 (§4.4)', 'Glucose dissolves in water because water molecules', '["break its carbon ring", "ionise it into Na+ and Cl-", "exclude it as a hydrophobic molecule", "form hydrogen bonds with its polar hydroxyl groups"]', 3, 'Water forms hydrogen bonds with the polar hydroxyl groups of glucose, so glucose dissolves.', '{"0": "The ring is not broken by dissolving.", "1": "That describes sodium chloride.", "2": "Glucose is polar, not excluded."}'),
  ('BIO-0929', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 97 (§4.4)', 'Hydrophobic exclusion explains why', '["lipids cluster together in water and help form cell membranes", "salts dissolve in water", "ice floats on water", "water has a high specific heat"]', 0, 'Non-polar molecules are pushed out by water and associate with one another; this helps lipids maintain membranes.', '{"1": "Salts dissolve because water surrounds ions.", "2": "Ice floats because it is less dense.", "3": "Specific heat relates to breaking hydrogen bonds."}'),
  ('BIO-0930', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 98 (§4.4)', 'Because of its high specific heat, water', '["heats up slowly and holds its temperature longer", "evaporates at low temperature", "freezes from the bottom up", "cannot dissolve ions"]', 0, 'High specific heat means water absorbs and releases heat with little temperature change; it acts as a temperature stabiliser.', '{"1": "Evaporation relates to heat of vaporisation.", "2": "Ice forms on the surface because it is less dense.", "3": "Water dissolves ions readily."}'),
  ('BIO-0931', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 98 (§4.4)', 'Sweating and transpiration cool organisms because water has a', '["low specific heat", "maximum density at 4 °C", "high heat of vaporisation", "low cohesion"]', 2, 'Water''s high heat of vaporisation means each gram evaporating removes much heat from the body surface.', '{"0": "Water has a high specific heat.", "1": "Maximum density at 4 °C explains floating ice.", "3": "Water has high cohesion."}'),
  ('BIO-0932', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 98 (§4.4)', 'The attraction between water molecules that helps pull water up xylem vessels from roots to leaves is', '["adhesion to lipids", "ionisation", "hydrophobic exclusion", "cohesion"]', 3, 'Hydrogen bonds make water molecules stick together (cohesion), so the pull of evaporation is relayed down xylem to the roots.', '{"0": "Adhesion to lipids is not described.", "1": "Ionisation forms H+ and OH-.", "2": "Hydrophobic exclusion concerns non-polar molecules."}'),
  ('BIO-0933', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 99 (§4.4)', 'A water strider can walk on water because of water''s', '["high heat of vaporisation", "high surface tension", "ionisation", "maximum density at 4 °C"]', 1, 'Hydrogen bonds give water high surface tension, so the insect walks without breaking the surface.', '{"0": "Heat of vaporisation concerns evaporation.", "2": "Ionisation produces ions.", "3": "Density at 4 °C concerns ice floating."}'),
  ('BIO-0934', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 99 (§4.4)', 'In pure water at 25 °C, the concentration of H+ ions is', '["10^-7 moles per litre", "10^-14 moles per litre", "1 mole per litre", "10^-1 moles per litre"]', 0, 'The concentration of each of H+ and OH- in pure water remains at 10^-7 moles/litre.', '{"1": "10^-14 is the product of both ion concentrations.", "2": "This would be strongly acidic.", "3": "This would be strongly acidic."}'),
  ('BIO-0935', 'Biological molecules', 'Importance of water', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 99 (§4.4)', 'Water has its maximum density at', '["-4 °C", "0 °C", "4 °C", "100 °C"]', 2, 'Water shows its maximum density at 4 °C; ice at 0 °C is less dense and floats.', '{"0": "Below 0 °C water is ice.", "1": "Ice at 0 °C is less dense than liquid water.", "3": "At 100 °C water boils."}'),
  ('BIO-0936', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 100 (§4.5)', 'The empirical formula of carbohydrates is', '["CnH2n+2", "(CH2)nCOOH", "CnH2nO2N", "C(H2O)n"]', 3, 'Carbohydrates contain C, H and O in a 1:2:1 ratio; their empirical formula is C(H2O)n.', '{"0": "This is an alkane formula.", "1": "This resembles a fatty acid.", "2": "Carbohydrates do not contain nitrogen."}'),
  ('BIO-0937', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 100 (§4.5)', 'Ribulose, an intermediate of photosynthesis, is a', '["triose", "hexose", "pentose", "disaccharide"]', 2, 'The classification table lists ribulose among pentoses; glucose, fructose and galactose are hexoses.', '{"0": "Trioses include glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone.", "1": "Hexoses include glucose, fructose and galactose.", "3": "Ribulose is a single sugar."}'),
  ('BIO-0938', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 101 (§4.5)', 'Glucose and fructose are structural isomers because', '["they differ in the orientation of the OH on carbon 4", "in fructose the double-bonded oxygen is on carbon 2 rather than a terminal carbon", "they have different molecular formulas", "fructose has five carbons"]', 1, 'In fructose the carbonyl oxygen is attached to internal carbon 2, so glucose and fructose are structural isomers.', '{"0": "Differing OH orientation at carbon 4 makes glucose and galactose stereoisomers.", "2": "Both are C6H12O6.", "3": "Fructose has six carbons."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0939', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 101 (§4.5)', 'Glucose and galactose are stereoisomers that differ in the orientation of the OH group on carbon', '["1", "2", "4", "5"]', 2, 'Glucose and galactose differ in the orientation of one hydroxyl group at carbon 4.', '{"0": "Carbon 1 OH orientation distinguishes alpha- and beta-glucose.", "1": "Carbon 2 holds the carbonyl in fructose.", "3": "Carbon 5 forms the ring bridge."}'),
  ('BIO-0940', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 102 (§4.5)', 'When fructose forms a ring in solution, the oxygen bridge forms between carbons', '["1 and 5, giving a six-cornered ring", "2 and 5, giving a five-cornered ring", "1 and 4, giving a five-cornered ring", "2 and 6, giving a six-cornered ring"]', 1, 'In fructose the bridge is between carbons 2 and 5, giving a five-cornered ring.', '{"0": "1 and 5 is glucose and galactose.", "2": "1 and 4 is ribose.", "3": "This is not given."}'),
  ('BIO-0941', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 102 (§4.5)', 'Alpha-D-glucose and beta-D-glucose differ only in the direction of the OH group on carbon', '["1", "2", "4", "6"]', 0, 'The alpha and beta forms differ only in the direction of the OH on carbon 1.', '{"1": "Carbon 2 is not the difference.", "2": "Carbon 4 distinguishes glucose and galactose.", "3": "Carbon 6 is not the difference."}'),
  ('BIO-0942', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 103 (§4.5)', 'The Haworth projection shows sugars', '["in their cyclic (ring) forms", "in their open-chain forms", "as three-dimensional crystals", "only as disaccharides"]', 0, 'The Fischer projection shows the open chain; the Haworth projection, named after Sir Norman Haworth, shows cyclic forms.', '{"1": "Open chain is the Fischer projection.", "2": "Crystals are not described.", "3": "It is used for any sugar ring."}'),
  ('BIO-0943', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 103 (§4.5)', 'In maltose, the two glucose units are joined by a', '["1,2-glycosidic bond", "1,6-glycosidic bond", "1,4-glycosidic bond", "peptide bond"]', 2, 'Maltose has a 1,4-glycosidic bond between carbon 1 of one glucose and carbon 4 of the other.', '{"0": "A 1,2 linkage joins glucose and fructose in sucrose.", "1": "1,6 linkages form branches in amylopectin and glycogen.", "3": "Peptide bonds join amino acids."}'),
  ('BIO-0944', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 104 (§4.5)', 'Lactose is made of', '["glucose and fructose", "two glucose units", "galactose and fructose", "galactose and glucose"]', 3, 'Lactose (milk sugar) is galactose 1-4 glucose and is found only in mammalian milk.', '{"0": "Glucose and fructose make sucrose.", "1": "Two glucose units make maltose.", "2": "This combination is not described."}'),
  ('BIO-0945', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 104 (§4.5)', 'Sucrose is called the transport disaccharide because it', '["is found only in milk", "forms starch granules", "is carried in the phloem to move sugar around the plant", "is the main sugar in cereals"]', 2, 'Sucrose is found in phloem of higher plants as a transport product, so it is called the transport disaccharide.', '{"0": "Lactose is the milk sugar.", "1": "Starch granules are made of starch.", "3": "Maltose is found in cereals."}'),
  ('BIO-0946', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 104 (§4.5)', 'High-fructose corn syrup replaced sucrose in many foods because', '["glucose is twice as sweet as sucrose", "fructose made from corn starch is sweeter than sucrose and cheap", "fructose cannot be digested", "corn syrup contains no sugar"]', 1, 'Glucose from corn starch was converted to its isomer fructose, which is sweeter than sucrose; the syrup is inexpensive.', '{"0": "Glucose is only half as sweet as sucrose.", "2": "Fructose is a digestible sugar.", "3": "Corn syrup is a sugar syrup."}'),
  ('BIO-0947', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 105 (§4.5)', 'Starch does not cause plant cells to take up water by osmosis because it is', '["highly branched", "made of beta-glucose", "a disaccharide", "insoluble"]', 3, 'Starch is insoluble, so it does not change the water potential of plant cells.', '{"0": "Branching affects speed of breakdown, not osmosis.", "1": "Starch is made of alpha-glucose.", "2": "Starch is a polysaccharide."}'),
  ('BIO-0948', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 105 (§4.5)', 'Amylopectin differs from amylose in having', '["1,6-glycosidic branches as well as 1,4 linkages", "only beta-1,4 linkages", "no glucose units", "a straight, unbranched chain"]', 0, 'Amylose is unbranched with 1,4 linkages; amylopectin also has branches with 1,6 linkages, giving more ends for amylase.', '{"1": "Beta-1,4 linkages are in cellulose.", "2": "Both are made of glucose.", "3": "A straight unbranched chain is amylose."}'),
  ('BIO-0949', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 105 (§4.5)', 'Glycogen can be broken down to glucose very quickly because it is', '["highly branched", "insoluble", "made of beta-glucose", "a straight helix"]', 0, 'Glycogen is so highly branched that it can be broken down to glucose very quickly.', '{"1": "Insolubility is not the reason given.", "2": "Glycogen contains alpha-glucose.", "3": "It is branched, not a straight helix."}'),
  ('BIO-0950', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 106 (§4.5)', 'Cellulose forms strong straight chains, unlike coiled starch, because it is made of', '["alpha-glucose with 1,6 branches", "fructose units", "glucose with nitrogen-containing groups", "beta-glucose, with alternate glucose units inverted"]', 3, 'Cellulose contains beta-glucose; alternate molecules are inverted and the chains are straight and linked by hydrogen bonds into microfibrils.', '{"0": "Alpha-glucose with branches is amylopectin or glycogen.", "1": "Cellulose is a glucose polymer.", "2": "Glucose with nitrogen-containing groups is chitin."}'),
  ('BIO-0951', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 106 (§4.5)', 'Cows and termites can use cellulose because', '["their saliva contains amylase", "bacteria in their gut have the enzyme cellulase", "they make cellulase in their liver", "cellulose is digested by stomach acid"]', 1, 'The beta-glycosidic bond needs cellulase; herbivores such as cows and termites have bacteria in their guts that digest cellulose.', '{"0": "Amylase cannot break the beta bond.", "2": "The book credits gut bacteria, not the liver.", "3": "Acid does not digest cellulose."}'),
  ('BIO-0952', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 106 (§4.5)', 'Chitin differs from cellulose in that each glucose unit carries', '["a phosphate group", "an extra carbon ring", "a nitrogen-containing group", "an alpha linkage"]', 2, 'Chitin is a modified cellulose in which each glucose has an added nitrogen-containing group.', '{"0": "Phosphate is not added.", "1": "No extra ring is described.", "3": "Its linkages are like those of cellulose."}'),
  ('BIO-0953', 'Biological molecules', 'Carbohydrates', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 107 (§4.5)', 'Agar, used as a medium for growing bacteria and fungi, is obtained from the cell walls of', '["red algae", "fungi", "bacteria", "green plants"]', 0, 'Agar is found in the cell walls of red algae and is used as a thickener and a culture medium.', '{"1": "Fungal walls contain chitin.", "2": "Bacterial walls contain murein.", "3": "Plant walls contain cellulose, pectin and lignin."}'),
  ('BIO-0954', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 107 (§4.6)', 'The term ''protein'' was coined by', '["Emil Fischer", "J. J. Berzelius", "Linus Pauling", "Erwin Chargaff"]', 1, 'Berzelius coined the term protein, from the Greek proteios, "of the first rank".', '{"0": "Fischer devised the Fischer projection.", "2": "Pauling studied the helical form of DNA fibres.", "3": "Chargaff found base ratios in DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0955', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 107 (§4.6)', 'In an amino acid, the amino group, carboxyl group, hydrogen and R group are all attached to the', '["nitrogen atom", "carboxyl carbon", "beta carbon", "alpha carbon"]', 3, 'In an amino acid four groups are attached to the same carbon, the alpha carbon.', '{"0": "The nitrogen is part of the amino group.", "1": "The carboxyl carbon is part of COOH.", "2": "No beta carbon is described."}'),
  ('BIO-0956', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 108 (§4.6)', 'The unique properties of each amino acid are determined by its', '["amino group", "carboxyl group", "side group (R)", "alpha hydrogen"]', 2, 'The identity and properties of each amino acid are determined by its R group; e.g. H in glycine, CH3 in alanine.', '{"0": "All amino acids share the amino group.", "1": "All share the carboxyl group.", "3": "All share the hydrogen on the alpha carbon."}'),
  ('BIO-0957', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 108 (§4.6)', 'Which amino acid is essential (cannot be made by the human body)?', '["Alanine", "Glycine", "Lysine", "Serine"]', 2, 'Essential amino acids include methionine, valine, tryptophan, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, threonine, phenylalanine and histidine.', '{"0": "Alanine is non-essential.", "1": "Glycine is non-essential.", "3": "Serine is non-essential."}'),
  ('BIO-0958', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 108 (§4.6)', 'According to the book, how many of the 20 protein amino acids can the human body make?', '["Five", "Nine", "Eleven", "Twenty"]', 2, 'Our bodies can make eleven (non-essential) amino acids; the other nine are essential.', '{"0": "Five is not given.", "1": "Nine is the number of essential amino acids.", "3": "The body cannot make all twenty."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0959', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 109 (§4.6)', 'Glycylalanine is a', '["disaccharide", "polypeptide of 20 amino acids", "nucleotide", "dipeptide formed from glycine and alanine"]', 3, 'Glycylalanine is a dipeptide formed by linking glycine and alanine through a peptide bond.', '{"0": "Disaccharides are two sugars.", "1": "It contains only two amino acids.", "2": "Nucleotides are units of nucleic acids."}'),
  ('BIO-0960', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 110 (§4.6)', 'Insulin consists of two polypeptide chains with', '["21 and 30 amino acids", "141 and 146 amino acids", "51 and 51 amino acids", "20 and 20 amino acids"]', 0, 'The smaller alpha chain of insulin has 21 amino acids and the longer beta chain 30.', '{"1": "141 and 146 are haemoglobin''s alpha and beta chains.", "2": "51 is the total for insulin.", "3": "This is not given."}'),
  ('BIO-0961', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 110 (§4.6)', 'Each beta chain of haemoglobin contains', '["30 amino acids", "141 amino acids", "146 amino acids", "574 amino acids"]', 2, 'Haemoglobin has two alpha chains of 141 and two beta chains of 146 amino acids.', '{"0": "30 is insulin''s beta chain.", "1": "141 is the alpha chain.", "3": "574 is the total for haemoglobin."}'),
  ('BIO-0962', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 110 (§4.6)', 'Sickle cell haemoglobin results from replacement of', '["valine by glutamic acid in each alpha chain", "glutamic acid by valine at position six of each beta chain", "glycine by alanine at position one", "histidine by leucine in each beta chain"]', 1, 'In sickle cell haemoglobin valine replaces glutamic acid at position six in each beta chain.', '{"0": "This reverses the change and names the wrong chain.", "2": "This is not the mutation.", "3": "This is not the mutation."}'),
  ('BIO-0963', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 110 (§4.6)', 'The alpha helix and pleated sheet of a protein are maintained by', '["disulphide bonds only", "hydrogen bonds between amino and carboxyl groups of nearby amino acids", "peptide bonds between chains", "hydrophobic interactions between separate polypeptides"]', 1, 'Secondary structures are maintained by hydrogen bonds between amino and carboxyl groups of nearby amino acids.', '{"0": "Disulphide bonds help maintain tertiary structure.", "2": "Peptide bonds form the primary chain.", "3": "Interactions between polypeptides give quaternary structure."}'),
  ('BIO-0964', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 111 (§4.6)', 'In the tertiary structure of a globular protein in water,', '["hydrophobic amino acids are buried inside and hydrophilic ones face outwards", "hydrophilic amino acids are buried inside", "all amino acids face outwards", "the chain stays as a straight line"]', 0, 'Non-polar amino acids aggregate inside while polar ones turn toward the water at the surface.', '{"1": "This reverses the arrangement.", "2": "Hydrophobic ones are buried.", "3": "Tertiary structure is folded and globular."}'),
  ('BIO-0965', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 111 (§4.6)', 'A protein made of two or more polypeptide chains, each with tertiary structure, held together has', '["primary structure", "secondary structure", "only tertiary structure", "quaternary structure"]', 3, 'Aggregation of tertiary structures makes the quaternary structure.', '{"0": "Primary structure is the amino acid sequence.", "1": "Secondary structure is helix or sheet folding.", "2": "Combining chains goes beyond tertiary."}'),
  ('BIO-0966', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 112 (§4.6)', 'Which is a fibrous protein?', '["Haemoglobin", "Antibodies", "Keratin", "Enzymes such as pepsin"]', 2, 'Fibrous proteins include silk, actin, fibrin, keratin and collagen; globular proteins include enzymes, antibodies and haemoglobin.', '{"0": "Haemoglobin is globular.", "1": "Antibodies are globular.", "3": "Enzymes are globular."}'),
  ('BIO-0967', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 112 (§4.6)', 'Compared with fibrous proteins, globular proteins', '["do not disorganise easily", "form silk and hair", "are easily disorganised by changes in the environment", "are mainly structural"]', 2, 'The table says fibrous proteins do not disorganise easily, while globular proteins are disorganised by environmental changes.', '{"0": "Not disorganising easily is a fibrous protein trait.", "1": "Silk and hair are fibrous.", "3": "Globular proteins are mainly functional."}'),
  ('BIO-0968', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 114 (§4.6)', 'The main intracellular iron-storage protein is', '["casein", "ferritin", "albumin", "fibrinogen"]', 1, 'Ferritin is the main intracellular iron storage protein; low ferritin indicates risk of iron lack.', '{"0": "Casein stores potassium and calcium in milk.", "2": "Albumin maintains osmotic concentration of blood.", "3": "Fibrinogen forms fibrin in clotting."}'),
  ('BIO-0969', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 114 (§4.6)', 'Proteins that regulate gene action by preventing synthesis of RNA are called', '["repressors", "antibodies", "cytochromes", "hormones"]', 0, 'Repressors are proteins that regulate gene action by preventing RNA synthesis.', '{"1": "Antibodies combine with antigens.", "2": "Cytochromes are electron carriers.", "3": "Hormones regulate body activities from outside the cell."}'),
  ('BIO-0970', 'Biological molecules', 'Proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 114 (§4.6)', 'Which blood protein maintains the osmotic concentration of blood?', '["Fibrinogen", "Myoglobin", "Ferritin", "Albumin"]', 3, 'Albumin maintains osmotic concentration of blood and keeps its ability to flow.', '{"0": "Fibrinogen is converted to fibrin in clotting.", "1": "Myoglobin transports oxygen.", "2": "Ferritin stores iron."}'),
  ('BIO-0971', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 114 (§4.7)', 'Lipids are', '["soluble in water but insoluble in ether", "insoluble in water but soluble in organic solvents such as ether", "always polymers of glycerol", "made only of amino acids"]', 1, 'Lipids are non-polar molecules insoluble in water but soluble in organic solvents like ether and alcohol.', '{"0": "This reverses their solubility.", "2": "Lipids are a diverse group, not glycerol polymers.", "3": "Amino acids make proteins."}'),
  ('BIO-0972', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 114 (§4.7)', 'Acylglycerols that are liquid at room temperature are called', '["fats", "waxes", "oils", "steroids"]', 2, 'Acylglycerols liquid at room temperature are oils; solid ones are fats.', '{"0": "Fats are solid at room temperature.", "1": "Waxes are a separate lipid group.", "3": "Steroids have four fused rings."}'),
  ('BIO-0973', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 115 (§4.7)', 'In a triacylglycerol,', '["one fatty acid is joined to three glycerols", "two fatty acids and a phosphate are joined to glycerol", "isoprene units are joined in a chain", "three fatty acids are joined to one glycerol"]', 3, 'In triacylglycerols (triglycerides), three fatty acids are joined to a single glycerol backbone.', '{"0": "Only one glycerol is present.", "1": "Two fatty acids with phosphate make phosphatidic acid.", "2": "Isoprene units make terpenes."}'),
  ('BIO-0974', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 115 (§4.7)', 'Unsaturated fatty acids, compared with saturated ones,', '["have double bonds, bent chains and lower melting points", "have no double bonds and straight chains", "have the maximum number of hydrogen atoms", "have higher melting points"]', 0, 'Unsaturated fatty acids have double bonds that replace some hydrogens, making bent chains with low melting points.', '{"1": "This describes saturated fatty acids.", "2": "Saturated fatty acids carry the maximum hydrogens.", "3": "Saturated fatty acids have higher melting points."}'),
  ('BIO-0975', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 116 (§4.7)', 'Animal fats contain more energy than plant oils because they', '["contain unsaturated fatty acids", "are soluble in water", "contain more glycerol molecules", "contain saturated fatty acids with more C-H bonds"]', 3, 'Animal fats have saturated fatty acids with more C-H bonds; plant oils have unsaturated fatty acids with fewer.', '{"0": "Unsaturated fatty acids are typical of plant oils.", "1": "Fats are insoluble.", "2": "Each triacylglycerol has one glycerol."}'),
  ('BIO-0976', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 116 (§4.7)', 'Chemically, waxes are', '["single triacylglycerol molecules", "mixtures of long-chain alkanes, alcohols, ketones and long-chain fatty acids", "polymers of isoprene", "steroids with four rings"]', 1, 'Waxes have no well-defined structure; they are mixtures of long-chain alkanes (odd numbers, 25–35 C), alcohols other than glycerol, ketones and fatty acids.', '{"0": "Triacylglycerols are fats and oils.", "2": "Isoprene polymers are terpenes.", "3": "Four fused rings define steroids."}'),
  ('BIO-0977', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 117 (§4.7)', 'Phosphatidic acid is composed of', '["one glycerol and three fatty acids", "four fused carbon rings", "one glycerol, two fatty acids and one phosphate", "isoprene units and phosphate"]', 2, 'Phosphatidic acid is made of one glycerol, two fatty acids and one phosphoric acid; adding a nitrogenous base makes a phospholipid.', '{"0": "Three fatty acids with glycerol make a triacylglycerol.", "1": "Four fused rings are steroids.", "3": "Isoprene units make terpenes."}'),
  ('BIO-0978', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 117 (§4.7)', 'Lecithin, which forms the lipid bilayer of plasma membranes, is', '["phosphatidyl choline", "phosphatidyl serine", "cholesterol", "a prostaglandin"]', 0, 'Phosphatidyl choline (lecithin) forms the lipid bilayer in plasma membranes.', '{"1": "Phosphatidyl serine is another phospholipid but not lecithin.", "2": "Cholesterol is a steroid.", "3": "Prostaglandins are local messengers."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0979', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 117 (§4.7)', 'Chlorophyll, retinal pigment, vitamin A and rubber all belong to the lipid group', '["steroids", "waxes", "prostaglandins", "terpenes"]', 3, 'Terpenes are made of isoprene units and include chlorophyll, retinal pigments, vitamin A and rubber.', '{"0": "Steroids include cholesterol and sex hormones.", "1": "Waxes are protective coatings.", "2": "Prostaglandins are modified fatty acids acting locally."}'),
  ('BIO-0980', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 118 (§4.7)', 'All steroids have a carbon skeleton of', '["three six-cornered rings and one five-cornered ring", "four six-cornered rings", "two five-cornered rings", "a single five-carbon ring with tails"]', 0, 'Steroids have four fused rings: three 6-cornered and one 5-cornered.', '{"1": "One of the rings is five-cornered.", "2": "There are four rings.", "3": "A five-carbon ring with tails describes prostaglandins."}'),
  ('BIO-0981', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 118 (§4.7)', 'Aspirin reduces inflammation, pain and fever because it', '["inhibits cholesterol synthesis", "dissolves waxes", "inhibits prostaglandins", "blocks terpene formation"]', 2, 'Aspirin is a prostaglandin inhibitor, so it reduces inflammation, pain and fever.', '{"0": "Cholesterol synthesis is not its action.", "1": "Waxes are unrelated.", "3": "Terpenes are unrelated."}'),
  ('BIO-0982', 'Biological molecules', 'Lipids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 119 (§4.7)', 'Cholesterol acts as a precursor of', '["vitamin A and rubber", "vitamin D, bile acids and steroid hormones", "insulin and glucagon", "haemoglobin"]', 1, 'Cholesterol is a precursor of vitamin D, bile acids and steroid hormones such as androgens and oestrogens.', '{"0": "Vitamin A and rubber are terpenes.", "2": "Insulin and glucagon are peptide hormones.", "3": "Haemoglobin is a protein."}'),
  ('BIO-0983', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 119 (§4.8)', 'A nucleoside is made of', '["a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and phosphate", "two nucleotides", "a phosphate and a sugar only", "a nitrogenous base and a pentose sugar"]', 3, 'A nucleoside is a nitrogen base plus a pentose; adding phosphoric acid makes a nucleotide.', '{"0": "With phosphate it is a nucleotide.", "1": "Two nucleotides form a dinucleotide.", "2": "A base is needed."}'),
  ('BIO-0984', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 120 (§4.8)', 'Which nitrogenous base is found only in RNA?', '["Uracil", "Thymine", "Cytosine", "Adenine"]', 0, 'Cytosine is in both DNA and RNA, thymine only in DNA and uracil only in RNA.', '{"1": "Thymine is only in DNA.", "2": "Cytosine is in both.", "3": "Adenine is a purine in both."}'),
  ('BIO-0985', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 120 (§4.8)', 'Purines differ from pyrimidines in that purines', '["have a single ring", "are found only in RNA", "have a double ring", "contain no nitrogen"]', 2, 'Purines (adenine and guanine) are double-ringed; pyrimidines (C, T, U) are single-ringed.', '{"0": "Single rings are pyrimidines.", "1": "Purines are in both DNA and RNA.", "3": "Both are nitrogenous bases."}'),
  ('BIO-0986', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 120 (§4.8)', 'In a nucleotide, the phosphoric acid forms an ester linkage with which carbon of the pentose?', '["Carbon 1", "Carbon 5", "Carbon 2", "Carbon 4"]', 1, 'Phosphoric acid is linked by an ester bond to C-5 of the pentose; the base attaches at C-1.', '{"0": "The nitrogenous base attaches at carbon 1.", "2": "Carbon 2 is not the phosphate site.", "3": "Carbon 4 is not the phosphate site."}'),
  ('BIO-0987', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 121 (§4.8)', 'A phosphodiester linkage joins two nucleotides by ester bonds of one phosphate with', '["C-1 of both sugars", "the bases of both nucleotides", "C-2 and C-4 of one sugar", "C-5 of one sugar and C-3 of the next sugar"]', 3, 'The phosphate has an ester linkage at C-5 of one pentose and develops another at C-3 of the next, forming a phosphodiester linkage.', '{"0": "C-1 carries the base.", "1": "Bases are paired by hydrogen bonds, not phosphodiester links.", "2": "These carbons are not used."}'),
  ('BIO-0988', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 121 (§4.8)', 'NAD (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) acts in the cell as', '["a coenzyme that accepts hydrogen in oxidation-reduction reactions", "the energy currency of the cell", "a structural part of ribosomes", "a hereditary molecule"]', 0, 'NAD is a coenzyme that acts as a hydrogen acceptor in redox reactions; ATP is the energy currency.', '{"1": "The energy currency is ATP.", "2": "Ribosomes contain rRNA and protein.", "3": "DNA is the hereditary molecule."}'),
  ('BIO-0989', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 122 (§4.8)', 'Chargaff''s observation, important in working out DNA structure, was that', '["DNA is a single helix", "DNA contains uracil", "adenine equals thymine and guanine equals cytosine in DNA", "the two strands run parallel"]', 2, 'Chargaff found that adenine and thymine are equal in ratio in DNA, and so are guanine and cytosine.', '{"0": "DNA is a double helix.", "1": "Uracil is in RNA.", "3": "The strands are anti-parallel."}'),
  ('BIO-0990', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 122 (§4.8)', 'In the Watson and Crick model, the number of hydrogen bonds between G and C is', '["one", "two", "three", "four"]', 2, 'There are two hydrogen bonds between A and T and three between G and C.', '{"0": "One is too few.", "1": "Two bonds join A and T.", "3": "Four is not given."}'),
  ('BIO-0991', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 122 (§4.8)', 'In the DNA ladder model, the ''poles'' (sides) are made of', '["nitrogenous base pairs", "sugars and phosphate groups", "histone proteins", "amino acids"]', 1, 'The poles are sugars and phosphates; the rungs are base pairs.', '{"0": "Base pairs form the rungs.", "2": "Histones are proteins around which DNA wraps.", "3": "Amino acids are not part of DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0992', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 123 (§4.8)', 'A gene is defined in the book as', '["a sequence of DNA nucleotides that codes for a polypeptide", "a whole chromosome", "a molecule of mRNA", "a protein that switches DNA on"]', 0, 'A gene is a sequence of DNA nucleotides that codes for the formation of a polypeptide.', '{"1": "A chromosome contains many genes.", "2": "mRNA is a copy of a gene.", "3": "Regulatory proteins are not genes."}'),
  ('BIO-0993', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 123 (§4.8)', 'Which type of RNA makes up about 80% of the total RNA in a cell?', '["Messenger RNA", "Ribosomal RNA", "Transfer RNA", "Viral RNA"]', 1, 'rRNA comprises about 80% of cellular RNA; mRNA is 3–4% and tRNA 10–15%.', '{"0": "mRNA is about 3–4%.", "2": "tRNA is about 10–15%.", "3": "Viral RNA is not one of the three types."}'),
  ('BIO-0994', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 123 (§4.8)', 'The RNA molecule that resembles a clover leaf and carries amino acids to the ribosome is', '["messenger RNA", "ribosomal RNA", "DNA", "transfer RNA"]', 3, 'tRNA is small, resembles a clover leaf and transports amino acids to the ribosome and mRNA.', '{"0": "mRNA carries the genetic message.", "1": "rRNA forms ribosomes.", "2": "DNA is not RNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0995', 'Biological molecules', 'Nucleic acids', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 124 (§4.8)', 'In the central dogma, the transfer of information from RNA to protein is called', '["transcription", "replication", "translation", "reverse transcription"]', 2, 'DNA to RNA is transcription; RNA to protein is translation.', '{"0": "Transcription makes RNA from DNA.", "1": "Replication copies DNA.", "3": "Reverse transcription makes DNA from RNA."}'),
  ('BIO-0996', 'Biological molecules', 'Conjugated molecules', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 124 (§4.9)', 'Glycolipids are formed by', '["hydrophobic interactions between lipid and protein", "ionic bonds between DNA and protein", "covalent linkage between a lipid and a carbohydrate", "covalent linkage between protein and carbohydrate"]', 2, 'Glycolipids form by covalent linkage of a lipid and a carbohydrate and are part of membranes.', '{"0": "This describes lipoproteins.", "1": "This describes nucleoproteins.", "3": "This describes glycoproteins."}'),
  ('BIO-0997', 'Biological molecules', 'Conjugated molecules', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 124 (§4.9)', 'Lipoproteins are held together by', '["covalent bonds", "ionic bonds", "glycosidic bonds", "hydrophobic interactions between lipids and proteins"]', 3, 'Lipoproteins form by hydrophobic interactions, not covalent or ionic bonds, between lipids and proteins.', '{"0": "The book specifies non-covalent links.", "1": "Ionic bonds hold nucleoproteins.", "2": "Glycosidic bonds link sugars."}'),
  ('BIO-0998', 'Biological molecules', 'Conjugated molecules', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Biomolecules, p. 125 (§4.9)', 'Nucleoproteins such as nucleosomes are formed by', '["ionic bonds between DNA and histone proteins", "covalent bonds between RNA and lipids", "hydrophobic interactions between DNA and lipids", "peptide bonds between DNA and protein"]', 0, 'Nucleoproteins form by ionic bonds between chromosomal DNA and proteins, e.g. histones in nucleosomes.', '{"1": "Lipids are not involved.", "2": "Lipids are not involved.", "3": "Peptide bonds join amino acids only."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-0999', 'Enzymes', 'Enzymes', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 127 (§5.1)', 'Enzymes speed up chemical reactions by', '["raising the activation energy", "changing the products formed", "being used up in the reaction", "lowering the activation energy needed"]', 3, 'Enzymes are specific proteins that speed up reactions by lowering the activation energy and are unaltered in the process.', '{"0": "They lower, not raise, activation energy.", "1": "Enzymes do not change the nature of the products.", "2": "Enzymes are unaltered by the reaction."}'),
  ('BIO-1000', 'Enzymes', 'Enzymes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 127 (§5.1)', 'Red blood cells and nerve cells carry out very different chemical reactions because they', '["contain different genetic codes", "contain different sets of enzymes", "have no enzymes", "use only one enzyme each"]', 1, 'All cells do not have the same set of enzymes; RBCs and nerve cells contain different sets.', '{"0": "The book explains it by different enzyme sets.", "2": "Both have enzymes.", "3": "Each cell uses many enzymes."}'),
  ('BIO-1001', 'Enzymes', 'Enzymes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 128 (§5.1)', 'The enzymes of glycolysis are located', '["bound to ribosomes", "inside the nucleus", "dissolved in the cytoplasm", "in the cell wall"]', 2, 'Many enzymes, for example those of glycolysis, are dissolved in the cytoplasm.', '{"0": "Enzymes of protein synthesis are part of ribosomes.", "1": "Glycolysis is not in the nucleus.", "3": "Not in the cell wall."}'),
  ('BIO-1002', 'Enzymes', 'Enzymes', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 128 (§5.1)', 'Pepsin is produced in the inactive form pepsinogen because', '["active pepsin could digest protein structures inside the cell that makes it", "pepsin cannot be made by ribosomes", "pepsinogen digests fats", "the stomach cannot activate enzymes"]', 0, 'Pepsin digests proteins and could destroy structures in the cell where it is made, so it is secreted as pepsinogen and activated at its target.', '{"1": "All enzymes are made by ribosomes.", "2": "Pepsinogen is an inactive protease, not a lipase.", "3": "It is activated when it reaches its site of action."}'),
  ('BIO-1003', 'Enzymes', 'Active site', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 128 (§5.1)', 'The small cleft on an enzyme where catalysis occurs is the', '["prosthetic group", "allosteric site of the substrate", "active site", "apoenzyme"]', 2, 'A small cleft made of a few amino acids, the active site, is where catalysis occurs.', '{"0": "A prosthetic group is a tightly bound cofactor.", "1": "This is not the term used.", "3": "The apoenzyme is the whole protein part."}'),
  ('BIO-1004', 'Enzymes', 'Active site', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 128 (§5.1)', 'The two regions of an active site are', '["the apoenzyme and the holoenzyme", "the binding site and the catalytic site", "the cofactor and the coenzyme", "the substrate and the product"]', 1, 'The active site has a binding site, where the substrate attaches by weak forces, and a catalytic site, which transforms it.', '{"0": "These are terms for the protein part and complete enzyme.", "2": "These are kinds of non-protein helpers.", "3": "These are reactants and results, not regions of the enzyme."}'),
  ('BIO-1005', 'Enzymes', 'Active site', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 128 (§5.1)', 'A substrate is held in the binding site of an enzyme by', '["weak chemical forces such as hydrogen bonds", "covalent bonds", "peptide bonds", "ionic bonds with metal ions only"]', 0, 'The substrate fits into the binding site by weak chemical forces such as hydrogen bonds.', '{"1": "Covalent binding is how irreversible inhibitors and prosthetic groups attach.", "2": "Peptide bonds join amino acids.", "3": "The book names weak forces such as hydrogen bonds."}'),
  ('BIO-1006', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 128 (§5.2)', 'A complete enzyme, including its protein part and its cofactor, is called a', '["apoenzyme", "coenzyme", "prosthetic group", "holoenzyme"]', 3, 'The protein part is the apoenzyme; the complete enzyme including the cofactor is the holoenzyme.', '{"0": "The apoenzyme is only the protein part.", "1": "A coenzyme is a loosely attached organic cofactor.", "2": "A prosthetic group is a covalently bound cofactor."}'),
  ('BIO-1007', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 128 (§5.2)', 'Metal ion cofactors such as Mg2+ and Zn2+ help enzymes by', '["acting as substrates", "forming covalent bonds with products", "carrying hydrogen between enzymes", "changing non-functional active sites into functional ones"]', 3, 'Metal ions change non-functional active sites into functional sites and alter the enzyme''s shape so it can bind substrate.', '{"0": "Cofactors are not substrates.", "1": "They do not bond to products.", "2": "Carrying hydrogen is a coenzyme role, e.g. NAD+."}'),
  ('BIO-1008', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 129 (§5.2)', 'A cofactor that forms covalent bonds with the enzyme is called a', '["prosthetic group", "coenzyme", "metal activator", "substrate"]', 0, 'Cofactors that form covalent bonds with the enzyme are prosthetic groups, e.g. hematin.', '{"1": "A coenzyme is loosely attached.", "2": "Metal ions are a separate kind of cofactor.", "3": "The substrate is what the enzyme acts on."}'),
  ('BIO-1009', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 129 (§5.2)', 'Which of these is given as an example of a prosthetic group?', '["NAD+", "Hematin", "Mg2+", "Riboflavin"]', 1, 'The book gives hematin as an organic prosthetic group.', '{"0": "NAD+ is a coenzyme.", "2": "Mg2+ is a metal ion cofactor.", "3": "Riboflavin is a vitamin that acts as a coenzyme."}'),
  ('BIO-1010', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 129 (§5.2)', 'A coenzyme is a cofactor that is', '["a metal ion such as Cu2+", "covalently bonded to the enzyme", "a non-protein organic molecule loosely attached to the enzyme", "a part of the polypeptide chain"]', 2, 'When the cofactor is a non-protein organic molecule loosely attached to the enzyme, it is a coenzyme.', '{"0": "Metal ions are a different type of cofactor.", "1": "Covalently bonded cofactors are prosthetic groups.", "3": "Cofactors are non-protein."}'),
  ('BIO-1011', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 129 (§5.2)', 'Which vitamins are named in the book as functioning as coenzymes?', '["Vitamins A and D", "Vitamin C and vitamin E", "Niacin and riboflavin", "Vitamin K and vitamin B12"]', 2, 'Many vitamins, e.g. niacin and riboflavin, function as coenzymes.', '{"0": "A and D are not given as coenzymes here.", "1": "Not named.", "3": "K and B12 are mentioned as products of gut bacteria, not here."}'),
  ('BIO-1012', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 129 (§5.2)', 'When food is oxidised in the cell, NAD+ becomes NADH by', '["accepting a hydrogen atom drawn from food molecules", "losing a phosphate group", "binding a metal ion", "being hydrolysed"]', 0, 'Enzymes draw electrons from food and transfer them to NAD+, which is reduced to NADH and carries their energy.', '{"1": "Losing phosphate is ATP to ADP.", "2": "Metal binding is not this change.", "3": "NAD+ is reduced, not hydrolysed."}'),
  ('BIO-1013', 'Enzymes', 'Mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 129 (§5.3)', 'Activation energy is described as the energy that', '["is stored in the products", "is released by the enzyme when it breaks", "keeps the enzyme folded", "destabilises existing chemical bonds to start a reaction"]', 3, 'Activation energy is the energy which works to destabilise existing chemical bonds and initiate the reaction.', '{"0": "It is not stored in products.", "1": "The enzyme is not broken.", "2": "Enzyme folding is maintained by bonds within it."}'),
  ('BIO-1014', 'Enzymes', 'Mechanism', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 129 (§5.3)', 'Sucrose is hydrolysed into glucose and fructose whether or not sucrase is present. This shows that enzymes', '["are consumed in the reaction", "do not change the nature of the products", "only work in acidic conditions", "make reactions irreversible"]', 1, 'Enzymes do not affect the nature or properties of the end products; sucrose always gives glucose and fructose.', '{"0": "Enzymes are unaltered.", "2": "pH is not the point of this example.", "3": "Reversibility is not discussed here."}'),
  ('BIO-1015', 'Enzymes', 'Mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 130 (§5.3)', 'In a metabolic pathway such as respiration, the product of one enzyme''s reaction', '["serves as the substrate for the enzyme of the next step", "always inhibits the next enzyme", "is immediately excreted", "is converted back to the first substrate"]', 0, 'In complex pathways, products of one enzyme serve as substrates for the next; the end product finally inhibits the first enzyme.', '{"1": "The end product inhibits the first enzyme, not each next one.", "2": "Intermediates are used in the next step.", "3": "They are not converted back."}'),
  ('BIO-1016', 'Enzymes', 'Mechanism', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 130 (§5.3)', 'The lock-and-key model of enzyme action was proposed in 1894 by', '["Daniel Koshland", "Emil Fischer", "Hans Krebs", "Christian de Duve"]', 1, 'The German chemist Emil Fischer proposed the lock-and-key model in 1894.', '{"0": "Koshland proposed the induced fit model in 1958.", "2": "Krebs is not linked with enzyme models here.", "3": "De Duve discovered lysosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-1017', 'Enzymes', 'Mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 130 (§5.3)', 'The lock-and-key model assumes that the active site', '["changes shape when the substrate binds", "is absent until the substrate arrives", "can bind any substrate", "is rigid and does not change before, during or after catalysis"]', 3, 'The lock-and-key model postulates a rigid active site with no flexibility.', '{"0": "Shape change on binding is the induced fit model.", "1": "The active site exists before binding.", "2": "Lock and key stresses specificity."}'),
  ('BIO-1018', 'Enzymes', 'Mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 130 (§5.3)', 'According to Koshland''s induced fit model,', '["the active site is a rigid structure", "enzymes are permanently changed by the reaction", "substrate binding induces changes in the enzyme that make catalysis more effective", "substrates change shape but enzymes never do"]', 2, 'In the induced fit model the substrate induces changes in enzyme structure; the active site is flexible, and the enzyme regains its shape afterwards.', '{"0": "A rigid site is the lock-and-key idea.", "1": "The enzyme regains its original structure.", "3": "The enzyme changes shape."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1019', 'Enzymes', 'Temperature', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 131 (§5.4)', 'The optimum temperature for human enzymes is', '["25 °C", "37 °C", "50 °C", "70 °C"]', 1, 'The optimum temperature for human enzymes is 37 °C.', '{"0": "25 °C is below the optimum.", "2": "50 °C is above the optimum.", "3": "70 °C or higher is where enzymes of thermophilic bacteria work."}'),
  ('BIO-1020', 'Enzymes', 'Temperature', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 131 (§5.4)', 'Thermophilic bacteria in hot springs have enzymes that work at 70 °C or higher because their proteins', '["lack hydrogen bonds", "are made of nucleic acids", "have stronger bonding between polypeptide chains", "are denatured only by cold"]', 2, 'Thermophilic bacteria have proteins with stronger bonding between their polypeptide chains.', '{"0": "Their stability comes from stronger bonding, not lack of bonds.", "1": "Their enzymes are proteins.", "3": "This is not stated."}'),
  ('BIO-1021', 'Enzymes', 'Temperature', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 131 (§5.4)', 'Below the optimum temperature, enzyme action is slow mainly because', '["the enzyme is denatured", "the bonds become less flexible and do not allow the induced change in the active site", "the substrate is destroyed", "the products inhibit the enzyme"]', 1, 'At lower temperature the bonds become less flexible and do not permit the induced change needed for action.', '{"0": "Denaturation occurs well above the optimum.", "2": "The substrate is not destroyed.", "3": "Feedback is a different mechanism."}'),
  ('BIO-1022', 'Enzymes', 'Temperature', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 132 (§5.4)', 'Denaturation of an enzyme at high temperature is caused by', '["violent vibrations of atoms breaking the bonds that hold the globular structure", "loss of the cofactor only", "addition of extra amino acids", "binding of a competitive inhibitor"]', 0, 'Well above optimum, atomic vibrations become too violent and bonds cannot hold the chains, so the globular structure is lost.', '{"1": "Loss of a cofactor is not denaturation by heat.", "2": "No amino acids are added.", "3": "Inhibitor binding is not denaturation."}'),
  ('BIO-1023', 'Enzymes', 'pH', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 132 (§5.4)', 'Which enzyme is described as working in both acidic and alkaline media?', '["Pepsin", "Trypsin", "Salivary amylase", "Papain"]', 3, 'Papain from green papaya works in both acidic and alkaline media; pepsin needs acid and trypsin alkaline conditions.', '{"0": "Pepsin is active in acidic medium.", "1": "Trypsin works best in alkaline medium.", "2": "Not described as working in both."}'),
  ('BIO-1024', 'Enzymes', 'pH', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 132 (§5.4)', 'Trypsin shows its optimum activity in', '["a strongly acidic medium", "pure water only", "an alkaline medium", "any pH equally"]', 2, 'Pepsin is active in acidic medium while trypsin shows optimum activity in alkaline medium.', '{"0": "Acidic medium suits pepsin.", "1": "Not stated.", "3": "Each enzyme has a narrow optimum range."}'),
  ('BIO-1025', 'Enzymes', 'pH', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 132 (§5.4)', 'A change in pH alters enzyme activity because it', '["changes the ionisation of amino acids at the active site and of the substrate", "adds energy to the substrate", "changes the sequence of amino acids", "removes cofactors permanently"]', 0, 'Bonds between oppositely charged amino acids (e.g. glutamic acid and lysine) are sensitive to H+ concentration; pH changes ionisation at the active site and of the substrate.', '{"1": "Adding energy describes heating.", "2": "pH does not change the sequence.", "3": "Not given as the mechanism."}'),
  ('BIO-1026', 'Enzymes', 'Concentration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 132 (§5.4)', 'If substrate is unlimited, increasing the enzyme concentration will', '["decrease the rate", "have no effect at any concentration", "denature the enzyme", "increase the rate of reaction in direct proportion"]', 3, 'The overall rate depends directly on the amount of enzyme present if substrate is unlimited.', '{"0": "More enzyme means more active sites and faster reaction.", "1": "Rate rises while substrate is available.", "2": "Concentration does not denature enzymes."}'),
  ('BIO-1027', 'Enzymes', 'Concentration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 133 (§5.4)', 'At very high substrate concentration, further increase in substrate does not increase the reaction rate because', '["the enzyme is denatured", "all active sites are occupied", "the substrate becomes an inhibitor", "products are no longer formed"]', 1, 'When all active sites are occupied, extra substrate molecules cannot find free active sites, so rate stays constant.', '{"0": "Substrate does not denature enzymes.", "2": "Substrate is not an inhibitor.", "3": "Products are still formed at the maximum rate."}'),
  ('BIO-1028', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 133 (§5.5)', 'Cyanide is a poison because it', '["competes with glucose for hexokinase", "denatures all proteins by heat", "irreversibly inhibits cytochrome oxidase and blocks cellular respiration", "inhibits prostaglandin synthesis"]', 2, 'Cyanide is an irreversible inhibitor that combines with copper and iron in the active site of cytochrome oxidase and blocks respiration.', '{"0": "Not the mechanism given.", "1": "It is an inhibitor, not heat denaturation.", "3": "Prostaglandin enzymes are inhibited by aspirin."}'),
  ('BIO-1029', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 134 (§5.5)', 'A competitive inhibitor', '["binds away from the active site and changes the enzyme''s shape", "always forms covalent bonds", "is the final product of a pathway", "resembles the substrate and competes for the active site"]', 3, 'A competitive inhibitor resembles the substrate and competes for the same binding site.', '{"0": "Binding elsewhere is non-competitive inhibition.", "1": "Competitive inhibitors can be reversible.", "2": "End products act in feedback inhibition."}'),
  ('BIO-1030', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 134 (§5.5)', 'Malonic acid inhibits succinic dehydrogenase because it', '["is structurally similar to succinic acid and occupies the active site", "binds the enzyme away from the active site", "denatures the enzyme", "is a cofactor of the enzyme"]', 0, 'Malonic acid resembles succinic acid and competes for the active site, a classic case of competitive inhibition.', '{"1": "That would be non-competitive inhibition.", "2": "It does not denature the enzyme.", "3": "It blocks, not assists, the enzyme."}'),
  ('BIO-1031', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 134 (§5.5)', 'Succinic dehydrogenase normally catalyses the conversion of', '["malonic acid to succinic acid", "succinic acid to fumaric acid", "pyruvic acid to acetyl CoA", "glucose to fructose"]', 1, 'Succinic dehydrogenase catalyses oxidation of succinic acid to fumaric acid.', '{"0": "Malonic acid is the inhibitor.", "2": "Not this enzyme.", "3": "Glucose to fructose is done by an isomerase."}'),
  ('BIO-1032', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 134 (§5.5)', 'A non-competitive inhibitor reduces enzyme activity by', '["entering the active site", "resembling the substrate", "acting as a coenzyme", "binding at another site and altering the shape so the active site no longer fits the substrate"]', 3, 'A non-competitive inhibitor has no structural similarity to the substrate; it binds elsewhere and changes the enzyme''s shape.', '{"0": "Entering the active site is competitive inhibition.", "1": "Resemblance to the substrate is competitive inhibition.", "2": "Coenzymes help, not block."}'),
  ('BIO-1033', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 135 (§5.5)', 'In the book''s example of non-competitive inhibition, succinyl CoA', '["binds succinyl-CoA synthetase and stops more succinyl CoA being made", "competes with succinic acid for succinic dehydrogenase", "covalently disables the bacterial wall enzyme", "acts as a cofactor"]', 0, 'After its formation, succinyl CoA binds the enzyme succinyl-CoA synthetase as a non-competitive inhibitor, so no more is produced.', '{"1": "That is malonic acid''s competitive action.", "2": "That describes penicillin.", "3": "It inhibits, not assists."}'),
  ('BIO-1034', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 135 (§5.5)', 'Irreversible inhibitors', '["form weak hydrogen bonds", "form covalent bonds and cannot be removed by dilution or extra substrate", "are overcome by adding more substrate", "only work at high temperature"]', 1, 'Irreversible inhibitors make covalent bonds with the enzyme; dilution, dialysis or more substrate does not release them.', '{"0": "Weak bonds describe reversible inhibitors.", "2": "Extra substrate overcomes reversible inhibition.", "3": "Temperature is not involved."}'),
  ('BIO-1035', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 135 (§5.5)', 'Penicillin acts as an irreversible inhibitor by', '["blocking succinate dehydrogenase", "inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis", "permanently disabling the enzyme that builds bacterial cell walls", "stopping cytochrome oxidase"]', 2, 'Penicillin permanently disables the enzyme responsible for building bacterial cell walls.', '{"0": "Malonate blocks succinate dehydrogenase.", "1": "Aspirin inhibits prostaglandin-producing enzymes.", "3": "Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase."}'),
  ('BIO-1036', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 135 (§5.5)', 'Inhibitors of enzymes involved in blood clotting are used as', '["antibiotics", "biopesticides", "anticoagulants", "coenzymes"]', 2, 'Some inhibitors of clotting enzymes are used as anticoagulants.', '{"0": "Antibiotics inhibit bacterial enzymes.", "1": "Biopesticides are organisms used against pests.", "3": "Coenzymes assist enzymes."}'),
  ('BIO-1037', 'Enzymes', 'Inhibition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 135 (§5.5)', 'In feedback inhibition, the enzyme that is inhibited is usually', '["the last enzyme, inhibited by the first substrate", "every enzyme, inhibited by heat", "an enzyme of another cell", "an early enzyme of the pathway, inhibited by the final product"]', 3, 'The final product reacts with an initial enzyme and changes its conformation, shutting the pathway.', '{"0": "It is the end product acting on an early enzyme.", "1": "Heat is not involved.", "2": "It acts within the pathway."}'),
  ('BIO-1038', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 136 (§5.6)', 'Hexokinase, which transfers a phosphate group from ATP to glucose, is a', '["transferase", "hydrolase", "isomerase", "lyase"]', 0, 'Transferases move a functional group from one substrate to another; hexokinase is the example given.', '{"1": "Hydrolases add water to break bonds.", "2": "Isomerases rearrange a molecule.", "3": "Lyases add or remove groups without hydrolysis."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1039', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 136 (§5.6)', 'Pyruvate decarboxylase, which removes CO2 from pyruvic acid, belongs to the class', '["ligases", "lyases", "hydrolases", "oxidoreductases"]', 1, 'Lyases catalyse non-hydrolytic addition or removal of groups such as CO2; pyruvate decarboxylase is the example.', '{"0": "Ligases join molecules using ATP.", "2": "Hydrolases use water.", "3": "Oxidoreductases add or remove electrons or H."}'),
  ('BIO-1040', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 136 (§5.6)', 'Hexose isomerase, which converts glucose to fructose, is an example of', '["transferases", "lyases", "isomerases", "hydrolases"]', 2, 'Isomerases catalyse intramolecular rearrangement; hexose isomerase converts glucose to fructose.', '{"0": "Transferases move groups between substrates.", "1": "Lyases remove groups like CO2.", "3": "Hydrolases break bonds with water."}'),
  ('BIO-1041', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 136 (§5.6)', 'Cytochrome oxidase belongs to the enzyme class', '["transferases", "isomerases", "ligases", "oxidoreductases"]', 3, 'Oxidoreductases catalyse oxidation and reduction; cytochrome oxidase oxidises cytochrome.', '{"0": "Transferases move functional groups.", "1": "Isomerases rearrange molecules.", "2": "Ligases join molecules using ATP."}'),
  ('BIO-1042', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 137 (§5.6)', 'Erepsin breaks down', '["large polypeptides into smaller ones", "dipeptides into amino acids", "small polypeptides into dipeptides", "lipids into fatty acids"]', 1, 'Pepsin and trypsin break large polypeptides; aminopeptidases give dipeptides; erepsin breaks dipeptides into amino acids.', '{"0": "That is the work of pepsin and trypsin.", "2": "That is the work of aminopeptidases.", "3": "That is the work of lipases."}'),
  ('BIO-1043', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 137 (§5.6)', 'Cellulase breaks cellulose into', '["cellobiose or glucose", "maltose", "fructose and glucose", "galactose and glucose"]', 0, 'Cellulase breaks cellulose into cellobiose (a disaccharide) or glucose.', '{"1": "Amylase gives maltose from starch or glycogen.", "2": "Sucrase gives glucose and fructose.", "3": "Lactase gives glucose and galactose."}'),
  ('BIO-1044', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 137 (§5.6)', 'Lactase breaks lactose into', '["two glucose molecules", "glucose and fructose", "glucose and galactose", "cellobiose"]', 2, 'Lactase breaks lactose into glucose and galactose.', '{"0": "Maltase gives two glucose molecules.", "1": "Sucrase gives glucose and fructose.", "3": "Cellobiose comes from cellulose."}'),
  ('BIO-1045', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 137 (§5.6)', 'RNAase, DNAase and ATPase are grouped, on the basis of substrate, as', '["proteases", "nucleases", "lipases", "carbohydrases"]', 1, 'Nucleases act on nucleic acids; RNAase, DNAase and ATPase break RNA, DNA and ATP.', '{"0": "Proteases act on proteins.", "2": "Lipases act on lipids.", "3": "Carbohydrases act on carbohydrates."}'),
  ('BIO-1046', 'Enzymes', 'Classification', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Enzymes, p. 137 (§5.6)', 'Ligases catalyse reactions in which', '["water is added to split a molecule", "one isomer is converted to another", "CO2 is removed without water", "two molecules are joined by new bonds using energy from ATP"]', 3, 'Ligases join two molecules by forming new C-C, C-N, C-O or C-S bonds using ATP energy, e.g. polymerases.', '{"0": "Adding water to split is hydrolysis.", "1": "Isomer conversion is done by isomerases.", "2": "CO2 removal is done by lyases."}'),
  ('BIO-1047', 'Bioenergetics', 'ATP', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 141 (Intro)', 'The three subunits of an ATP molecule are', '["adenine, deoxyribose and three phosphate groups", "adenosine, glucose and two phosphate groups", "adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups", "guanine, ribose and three phosphate groups"]', 2, 'ATP is a nucleotide made of adenine (a nitrogen base), ribose (a five-carbon sugar) and three phosphate groups.', '{"0": "Deoxyribose is the sugar of DNA, not ATP.", "1": "The sugar is ribose and there are three phosphates.", "3": "The base in ATP is adenine."}'),
  ('BIO-1048', 'Bioenergetics', 'ATP', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 141 (Intro)', 'ATP was discovered in 1929 by', '["Karl Lohmann", "Fritz Lipmann", "Melvin Calvin", "Hans Krebs"]', 0, 'Karl Lohmann discovered ATP in 1929; in 1941 Fritz Lipmann proposed that it is the main energy-transfer molecule.', '{"1": "Lipmann proposed its energy-transfer role in 1941.", "2": "Calvin worked out the dark reactions.", "3": "Krebs worked out the citric acid cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-1049', 'Bioenergetics', 'ATP', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 141 (Intro)', 'Fritz Lipmann''s contribution in 1941 was to propose that ATP', '["is made only in chloroplasts", "contains deoxyribose", "stores genetic information", "is the main energy-transfer molecule in the cell"]', 3, 'In 1941 Fritz Lipmann proposed that ATP is the main energy-transfer molecule in the cell.', '{"0": "ATP is made in both mitochondria and chloroplasts, and Lipmann''s point was its role.", "1": "ATP contains ribose.", "2": "Genetic information is stored in DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-1050', 'Bioenergetics', 'ATP', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 141 (Intro)', 'Breaking the terminal phosphate bond of one mole of ATP releases about', '["3.65 kcal", "7.3 kcal", "14.6 kcal", "36 kcal"]', 1, 'Hydrolysis of one high-energy phosphate bond releases about 7.3 kcal (7,300 calories) per mole of ATP.', '{"0": "This is half the correct value.", "2": "14.6 kcal is the energy in two ATP.", "3": "36 is the net ATP count from aerobic oxidation of glucose, not kcal."}'),
  ('BIO-1051', 'Bioenergetics', 'ATP', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 141 (Intro)', 'In common energy-releasing reactions, ATP is converted to', '["AMP and two water molecules", "ADP and inorganic phosphate", "adenosine and three phosphates", "NADP and inorganic phosphate"]', 1, 'Usually only the outer P-P bond breaks, so ATP becomes ADP and one Pi is released.', '{"0": "AMP forms only in some cases when ADP is split further.", "2": "Only one phosphate is removed in common reactions.", "3": "NADP is a separate coenzyme."}'),
  ('BIO-1052', 'Bioenergetics', 'ATP', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 141 (Intro)', 'Which statement about ATP is correct?', '["It is made during energy-consuming processes", "It stores energy only in the bond between adenine and ribose", "It is broken down only during photosynthesis", "It is made during energy-releasing processes and broken during energy-consuming ones"]', 3, 'Cells combine ADP with Pi to make ATP during energy-releasing processes and break ATP in energy-consuming processes, so ATP transfers energy between reactions.', '{"0": "This reverses the relationship.", "1": "The high-energy bonds are between phosphate groups.", "2": "ATP is used in many processes, not just photosynthesis."}'),
  ('BIO-1053', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 142 (§6.1)', 'In the balanced equation for photosynthesis, water appears on both sides because', '["it is used in some reactions and released in others", "it is a catalyst", "glucose contains water molecules", "plants absorb more water than they release"]', 0, 'Water is a reactant in some steps and a product in others; there is no net yield of water.', '{"1": "Water is consumed and produced, not a catalyst.", "2": "Glucose does not contain water molecules.", "3": "The book says there is no net yield of water."}'),
  ('BIO-1054', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 142 (§6.1)', 'The compensation point is reached when', '["photosynthesis stops completely", "respiration stops completely", "the rates of photosynthesis and respiration are equal", "stomata close at noon"]', 2, 'At dawn and dusk the rates of photosynthesis and respiration may be equal for a short time, so there is no net gas exchange; this is the compensation point.', '{"0": "Both processes continue at the compensation point.", "1": "Respiration goes on day and night.", "3": "Noon has high light and net oxygen release."}'),
  ('BIO-1055', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 142 (§6.1)', 'At noon, a leaf in bright light shows', '["no net gas exchange", "net release of oxygen and net uptake of carbon dioxide", "net release of carbon dioxide", "net uptake of oxygen"]', 1, 'When light is strong, photosynthesis exceeds respiration, so oxygen is released and carbon dioxide is taken up overall.', '{"0": "No net exchange happens at the compensation point.", "2": "Net CO2 release happens in darkness.", "3": "Net O2 uptake happens in darkness."}'),
  ('BIO-1056', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 142 (§6.1)', 'Plants convert about what fraction of the solar energy they receive into chemical energy?', '["10%", "25–50%", "1–2%", "about 40%"]', 2, 'Plants convert only about 1–2% of the solar energy they receive into chemical energy, yet this sustains almost all life.', '{"0": "10% is the book''s figure for photosynthesis on land.", "1": "25–50% is the carbon lost to photorespiration in C-3 plants.", "3": "This is far too high."}'),
  ('BIO-1057', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 143 (§6.1)', 'The first action spectrum was made in 1883 by Engelmann using', '["Chlamydomonas", "corn leaves", "cyanobacteria", "Spirogyra"]', 3, 'T. W. Engelmann made the first action spectrum in 1883, working on the filamentous alga Spirogyra.', '{"0": "Chlamydomonas is not mentioned.", "1": "Corn is used as a C-4 example.", "2": "Cyanobacteria are mentioned for phycobilins."}'),
  ('BIO-1058', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 143 (§6.1)', 'In Engelmann''s experiment, most oxygen was released from Spirogyra cells receiving', '["blue and red light", "green and yellow light", "only green light", "only infrared light"]', 0, 'Maximum photosynthesis, and so maximum oxygen release, occurred in cells receiving the blue and red parts of the spectrum.', '{"1": "Green is least absorbed by chlorophyll.", "2": "Green is reflected or transmitted.", "3": "Infrared is not described as effective."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1059', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 143 (§6.1)', 'An action spectrum shows', '["the wavelengths absorbed by one pigment", "how effective different wavelengths are in driving photosynthesis", "the structure of chlorophyll", "the rate of respiration in darkness"]', 1, 'The action spectrum plots the rate of photosynthesis against each wavelength of light used.', '{"0": "That is an absorption spectrum.", "2": "Structure is not shown by a spectrum.", "3": "It concerns photosynthesis in light."}'),
  ('BIO-1060', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 143 (§6.1)', 'Aquatic photosynthetic organisms use as their carbon source', '["only atmospheric CO2", "dissolved CO2, bicarbonates and soluble carbonates", "glucose from the water", "methane"]', 1, 'Aquatic photosynthesizers use dissolved CO2, bicarbonates and soluble carbonates; land plants use atmospheric CO2.', '{"0": "Atmospheric CO2 is used by land organisms.", "2": "Photosynthesizers make sugar, not absorb it.", "3": "Methane is not a carbon source here."}'),
  ('BIO-1061', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 143 (§6.1)', 'Carbon dioxide reaches the mesophyll cells of a leaf by', '["being absorbed by the roots", "being made in the chloroplast", "entering through stomata and dissolving in water on mesophyll cell walls", "diffusing through the cuticle only"]', 2, 'CO2 enters leaves through stomata and dissolves in the water held by mesophyll cell walls; its entry depends on stomatal opening.', '{"0": "Roots absorb water, not the CO2 used here.", "1": "CO2 is taken in, not produced, in photosynthesis.", "3": "The book names stomata as the route."}'),
  ('BIO-1062', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 143 (§6.1)', 'Van Niel''s hypothesis that plants split water was based on studies of', '["yeast fermentation", "animal muscle cells", "fungi growing in the dark", "photosynthetic bacteria that make carbohydrate without releasing oxygen"]', 3, 'Van Niel studied photosynthetic bacteria that fix CO2 but release no oxygen, and proposed that plants split water as a hydrogen source.', '{"0": "Yeast does not photosynthesize.", "1": "Muscle cells do not photosynthesize.", "2": "Fungi do not photosynthesize."}'),
  ('BIO-1063', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'Plants were given H2 18O and normal CO2. The oxygen they released was', '["heavy oxygen (18O), showing it came from water", "normal oxygen (16O), showing it came from CO2", "a 50:50 mixture of 18O and 16O", "none, because 18O blocks photosynthesis"]', 0, 'Plants given H2 18O released 18O, while those given C18O2 released normal oxygen; so the oxygen released comes from water.', '{"1": "Normal oxygen was released by the group given labelled CO2.", "2": "The labelled group released 18O only.", "3": "Both groups photosynthesized."}'),
  ('BIO-1064', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'The ''reducing power'' used to reduce CO2 to sugar is', '["ATP", "NADPH", "FADH2", "oxygen"]', 1, 'Hydrogen from water reduces NADP to NADPH, which serves as the reducing power for reducing CO2.', '{"0": "ATP supplies energy, not hydrogen.", "2": "FADH2 belongs to respiration.", "3": "Oxygen is a by-product."}'),
  ('BIO-1065', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 147 (§6.1)', 'Photosynthesis is a redox process because', '["water is reduced and carbon dioxide is oxidized", "oxygen is reduced to water", "water is oxidized and carbon dioxide is reduced", "glucose is oxidized to CO2"]', 2, 'Splitting water oxidizes it (it loses electrons and H+) and releases O2, while CO2 is reduced to sugar as electrons and H+ are added.', '{"0": "This reverses the redox roles.", "1": "Oxygen is reduced to water in respiration.", "3": "Glucose oxidation is respiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1066', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthesis', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 147 (§6.1)', 'The light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis take place in the', '["thylakoid membranes", "stroma", "outer chloroplast membrane", "cytosol"]', 0, 'Light-dependent reactions occur on the thylakoid membranes of the grana; light-independent reactions occur in the stroma.', '{"1": "The stroma holds the light-independent reactions.", "2": "The outer membrane does not hold photosystems.", "3": "The cytosol holds glycolysis."}'),
  ('BIO-1067', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'Compared with red photons, blue photons', '["carry less energy", "carry no energy", "have longer wavelengths", "carry more energy"]', 3, 'Short-wavelength (blue) photons have higher energy than long-wavelength (red) photons.', '{"0": "Red photons carry less energy.", "1": "All photons carry energy.", "2": "Blue light has the shorter wavelength."}'),
  ('BIO-1068', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'Bacteriochlorophylls are found in', '["red algae", "flowering plants", "green algae", "photosynthetic bacteria"]', 3, 'Chlorophylls a, b, c and d occur in plants and algae; others found in photosynthetic bacteria are bacteriochlorophylls.', '{"0": "Red algae have phycobilins along with chlorophylls.", "1": "Plants use chlorophylls a and b.", "2": "Green algae use chlorophylls a and b."}'),
  ('BIO-1069', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'The head of a chlorophyll molecule is a porphyrin ring made of', '["four pyrrole rings with a magnesium atom in the centre", "four pyrrole rings with an iron atom in the centre", "six pyrrole rings with magnesium", "a long hydrocarbon chain"]', 0, 'The hydrophilic head is a porphyrin ring of four pyrrole rings held together by a central magnesium atom.', '{"1": "Iron is the central atom of heme, not chlorophyll.", "2": "There are four pyrrole rings.", "3": "The hydrocarbon chain is the tail."}'),
  ('BIO-1070', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'The hydrocarbon tail of chlorophyll', '["absorbs light", "anchors the molecule in the thylakoid membrane", "contains the magnesium atom", "is hydrophilic"]', 1, 'The long hydrocarbon tail is hydrophobic and anchors chlorophyll in the thylakoid membrane; the porphyrin head absorbs light.', '{"0": "The porphyrin ring absorbs light.", "2": "Magnesium is in the head.", "3": "The tail is hydrophobic."}'),
  ('BIO-1071', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'Light of which colour is absorbed least by chlorophylls?', '["Violet-blue", "Orange-red", "Green", "Blue"]', 2, 'Chlorophylls absorb mainly violet-blue and orange-red light; green is least absorbed and is reflected or transmitted.', '{"0": "Violet-blue is strongly absorbed.", "1": "Orange-red is strongly absorbed.", "3": "Blue is strongly absorbed."}'),
  ('BIO-1072', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 144 (§6.1)', 'Besides absorbing light, carotenoids such as beta-carotene', '["protect the photosynthetic apparatus from damage by excess light", "split water to release oxygen", "fix CO2 in the stroma", "act as the reaction centre of PS-I"]', 0, 'Carotenoids broaden the range of light used and protect the photosynthetic apparatus from damage by excess light.', '{"1": "Water is split at PS-II.", "2": "CO2 is fixed by rubisco.", "3": "The PS-I reaction centre is P700 chlorophyll a."}'),
  ('BIO-1073', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 145 (§6.1)', 'Which pigment is NOT an accessory pigment?', '["Chlorophyll b", "Chlorophyll a", "Xanthophyll", "Phycobilin"]', 1, 'Accessory pigments are all pigments other than chlorophyll a that gather light; they pass the energy on to chlorophyll a.', '{"0": "Chlorophyll b is an accessory pigment.", "2": "Xanthophylls are accessory carotenoids.", "3": "Phycobilins are accessory pigments of red algae and cyanobacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-1074', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 145 (§6.1)', 'Phycobilins are accessory pigments found in', '["all flowering plants", "fungi", "mosses only", "red algae and cyanobacteria"]', 3, 'Chlorophyll b and carotenoids occur in plants, while phycobilins occur in red algae and cyanobacteria.', '{"0": "Plants use chlorophyll b and carotenoids.", "1": "Fungi are not photosynthetic.", "2": "The book links phycobilins to red algae and cyanobacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-1075', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 145 (§6.1)', 'The generally accepted order of energy transfer between pigments in plants is', '["chlorophyll a → chlorophyll b → carotenoids", "chlorophyll b → carotenoids → chlorophyll a", "carotenoids → chlorophyll b → chlorophyll a", "carotenoids → chlorophyll a → chlorophyll b"]', 2, 'Accessory pigments pass energy towards chlorophyll a: carotenoids → chlorophyll b → chlorophyll a.', '{"0": "This is the reverse order.", "1": "Carotenoids come first.", "3": "Chlorophyll a is the final receiver."}'),
  ('BIO-1076', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 145 (§6.1)', 'Absorption by chlorophylls is maximum at about', '["500 nm and 550 nm", "680 nm and 700 nm", "430 nm and 670 nm", "300 nm and 800 nm"]', 2, 'The absorption spectrum of chlorophylls shows maximum absorption of blue light (430 nm) and red light (670 nm).', '{"0": "These are green wavelengths, least absorbed.", "1": "680 and 700 nm name the reaction-centre chlorophylls.", "3": "These are outside the peaks given."}'),
  ('BIO-1077', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 145 (§6.1)', 'The action spectrum of photosynthesis closely matches the absorption spectrum of photosynthetic pigments. This shows that', '["green light drives photosynthesis best", "only carotenoids are used in photosynthesis", "light is not needed for photosynthesis", "the light absorbed by the pigments drives photosynthesis"]', 3, 'Both spectra peak in the blue and red, so the wavelengths the pigments absorb are those that are effective in photosynthesis.', '{"0": "Green is least absorbed and least effective.", "1": "Chlorophylls also absorb and are effective.", "2": "Light is essential for the light reactions."}'),
  ('BIO-1078', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 145 (§6.1)', 'Some wavelengths poorly absorbed by chlorophyll a are absorbed well by chlorophyll b. The advantage is that this', '["increases the range of light the plant can use", "stops the plant absorbing blue light", "makes chlorophyll b the reaction centre", "lets chlorophyll b fix CO2"]', 0, 'Differences between the absorption of chlorophyll a and b increase the range of light absorbed.', '{"1": "Both absorb blue light.", "2": "The reaction centres contain chlorophyll a.", "3": "CO2 is fixed by rubisco, not by pigments."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1079', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 146 (§6.1)', 'Each photosystem consists of', '["a stroma and a lumen", "an antenna complex and a reaction centre", "rubisco and RuBP", "ATP synthase and ferredoxin only"]', 1, 'Each photosystem has a light-gathering antenna complex that passes energy to a reaction centre.', '{"0": "These are compartments of the chloroplast.", "2": "These belong to the Calvin cycle.", "3": "These are separate proteins, not the parts of a photosystem."}'),
  ('BIO-1080', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 146 (§6.1)', 'The reaction centre of a photosystem passes its high-energy electrons to', '["a primary electron acceptor", "the antenna complex", "rubisco", "oxygen directly"]', 0, 'The reaction-centre chlorophyll a passes excited electrons to a primary electron acceptor, which feeds the electron transport chain.', '{"1": "The antenna passes energy to the reaction centre, not the reverse.", "2": "Rubisco fixes CO2.", "3": "Oxygen is the final acceptor in respiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1081', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 146 (§6.1)', 'Photosystem I and photosystem II are numbered according to', '["the order in which electrons pass through them", "the order of their discovery", "their wavelength of maximum absorption", "their size"]', 1, 'PS-I and PS-II are named in the order of their discovery; electrons in fact pass through PS-II first.', '{"0": "Electrons go from PS-II to PS-I, opposite to the numbering.", "2": "Their peaks are 700 and 680 nm, not the basis of the numbering.", "3": "Size is not the basis."}'),
  ('BIO-1082', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 147 (§6.1)', 'The reaction centre chlorophyll of photosystem II is', '["P430", "P670", "P680", "P700"]', 2, 'PS-II has P680 chlorophyll a, which absorbs best at 680 nm; PS-I has P700.', '{"0": "430 nm is the blue absorption peak.", "1": "670 nm is the red absorption peak of chlorophylls.", "3": "P700 belongs to PS-I."}'),
  ('BIO-1083', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 148 (§6.1)', 'During light-dependent reactions, light energy is converted into chemical energy stored in', '["glucose and oxygen", "RuBP and CO2", "ATP and NADPH", "FADH2 and NADH"]', 2, 'Light-dependent reactions form ATP and NADPH (assimilatory and reducing powers) used by the light-independent reactions.', '{"0": "Glucose is made in the Calvin cycle.", "1": "These are inputs of the Calvin cycle.", "3": "These are respiratory coenzymes."}'),
  ('BIO-1084', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 148 (§6.1)', 'In non-cyclic photophosphorylation', '["only PS-I takes part", "only PS-II takes part", "no electron transport chain is used", "both PS-I and PS-II take part"]', 3, 'Non-cyclic photophosphorylation is the usual route; both photosystems and two electron chains are involved.', '{"0": "Only PS-I is used in cyclic flow.", "1": "PS-I is needed to reduce NADP+.", "2": "Two electron chains are used."}'),
  ('BIO-1085', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 148 (§6.1)', 'After P680 loses its excited electrons, it becomes', '["a strong oxidizing agent that takes electrons from water", "a strong reducing agent that gives electrons to NADP+", "permanently inactive", "a carrier that pumps H+ ions"]', 0, 'Losing electrons leaves an electron ''hole'' in P680, which becomes a strong oxidizing agent; the hole is filled by electrons from water.', '{"1": "NADP+ is reduced by electrons from PS-I via ferredoxin.", "2": "It is refilled from water and keeps working.", "3": "Pumping is linked to the electron transport chain."}'),
  ('BIO-1086', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 148 (§6.1)', 'When one water molecule is split during photolysis, the products are', '["two H+ ions and one O2 molecule", "one H+ ion and one hydroxide ion", "glucose and oxygen", "two H+ ions, two electrons and one oxygen atom"]', 3, 'Water reacting with oxidized P680 breaks into two hydrogen ions, an oxygen atom (which joins another to form O2) and two electrons.', '{"0": "One water gives one oxygen atom, not an O2 molecule.", "1": "Photolysis releases electrons, not hydroxide.", "2": "Glucose is made in the stroma."}'),
  ('BIO-1087', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 148 (§6.1)', 'The electron carriers between PS-II and PS-I, in order, are', '["plastocyanin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone", "plastoquinone, cytochrome complex, plastocyanin", "ferredoxin, NADP reductase, plastocyanin", "coenzyme Q, cytochrome b, cytochrome c"]', 1, 'Electrons pass from PS-II to PS-I through plastoquinone (PQ), the cytochrome complex and plastocyanin (PC).', '{"0": "This is the reverse order.", "2": "Ferredoxin and NADP reductase come after PS-I.", "3": "These are carriers of the mitochondrial chain."}'),
  ('BIO-1088', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 149 (§6.1)', 'The enzyme that transfers electrons from ferredoxin to NADP+ is', '["ATP synthase", "rubisco", "NADP reductase", "plastocyanin"]', 2, 'NADP reductase transfers electrons from ferredoxin to NADP+, which takes an H+ and becomes NADPH.', '{"0": "ATP synthase makes ATP.", "1": "Rubisco fixes CO2.", "3": "Plastocyanin is a carrier between PS-II and PS-I."}'),
  ('BIO-1089', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 149 (§6.1)', 'The zigzag path of electrons through both photosystems and their chains is called the', '["Z-scheme", "Calvin cycle", "Krebs cycle", "C-4 pathway"]', 0, 'The zigzag path of electrons through PS-II, PS-I and the electron transport chains is called the Z-scheme.', '{"1": "The Calvin cycle fixes CO2.", "2": "The Krebs cycle is in mitochondria.", "3": "The C-4 pathway fixes CO2 into 4-C acids."}'),
  ('BIO-1090', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 149 (§6.1)', 'Cyclic photophosphorylation produces', '["ATP, NADPH and oxygen", "ATP only", "NADPH and oxygen only", "glucose"]', 1, 'In cyclic flow electrons return from PS-I''s acceptor to P700; ATP is made but no NADPH and no oxygen.', '{"0": "That is non-cyclic flow.", "2": "No NADPH or oxygen is made in cyclic flow.", "3": "Glucose is made in the Calvin cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-1091', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 149 (§6.1)', 'Cyclic electron flow is most likely when', '["water is plentiful and light is dim", "PS-I is damaged", "the Calvin cycle slows and NADPH accumulates in the chloroplast", "NADP+ is abundant"]', 2, 'Cyclic flow happens when the Calvin cycle slows down and NADPH accumulates, so electrons cycle back to P700 to make extra ATP.', '{"0": "The book links it to accumulated NADPH.", "1": "Cyclic flow needs PS-I.", "3": "Abundant NADP+ favours non-cyclic flow."}'),
  ('BIO-1092', 'Bioenergetics', 'Light reactions', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 150 (§6.1)', 'During chemiosmosis in the chloroplast, H+ ions are actively pumped', '["from the lumen into the stroma", "from the cytosol into the stroma", "from the matrix into the intermembrane space", "from the stroma into the thylakoid lumen"]', 3, 'Energy from electrons is used to pump H+ from the stroma into the lumen; H+ then diffuses back to the stroma through ATP synthase.', '{"0": "This is the direction of diffusion through ATP synthase.", "1": "The cytosol is outside the chloroplast.", "2": "This is the direction in mitochondria."}'),
  ('BIO-1093', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 151 (§6.1)', 'Melvin Calvin received the Nobel Prize in 1961 for', '["discovering ATP", "the action spectrum", "the citric acid cycle", "working out the light-independent reactions"]', 3, 'Melvin Calvin and colleagues at the University of California discovered the details of the dark reactions; he received the Nobel Prize in 1961.', '{"0": "ATP was discovered by Lohmann.", "1": "The first action spectrum was by Engelmann.", "2": "The citric acid cycle is named after Krebs."}'),
  ('BIO-1094', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 151 (§6.1)', 'Light-independent reactions can occur in the dark', '["only if oxygen is absent", "as long as ATP and NADPH are available", "only in C-4 plants", "never"]', 1, 'Dark reactions can occur with or without light provided ATP and NADPH are available.', '{"0": "Oxygen is not the condition named.", "2": "All plants run the Calvin cycle.", "3": "They do not need light directly."}'),
  ('BIO-1095', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 151 (§6.1)', 'During carbon fixation, three CO2 molecules combine with three RuBP to give', '["three molecules of glucose", "six molecules of G3P directly", "six molecules of 3-phosphoglyceric acid", "three molecules of oxaloacetic acid"]', 2, 'Rubisco combines 3 CO2 with 3 RuBP to form six molecules of the three-carbon 3-PGA.', '{"0": "Glucose is made later from G3P.", "1": "G3P forms in the reduction phase.", "3": "Oxaloacetic acid is the first product of C-4 fixation."}'),
  ('BIO-1096', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 151 (§6.1)', 'Rubisco is described as probably', '["the most abundant protein on Earth", "the smallest enzyme known", "a pigment of PS-I", "an enzyme of glycolysis"]', 0, 'Ribulose biphosphate carboxylase (rubisco) is probably the most abundant protein on Earth.', '{"1": "The book does not say it is small.", "2": "It is an enzyme, not a pigment.", "3": "It works in the Calvin cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-1097', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 151 (§6.1)', 'Plants that fix carbon only through the Calvin cycle are called C-3 plants because', '["they use three photosystems", "they need three ATP per CO2", "they fix carbon only three times a day", "the first product of carbon fixation is a three-carbon compound"]', 3, 'The first product of carbon fixation, 3-PGA, has three carbons, hence C-3 plants.', '{"0": "Chloroplasts have two photosystems.", "1": "The ATP count is not the reason for the name.", "2": "This is not the basis of the name."}'),
  ('BIO-1098', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 152 (§6.1)', 'In the reduction phase of the Calvin cycle (for three CO2 fixed), the energy and hydrogen are supplied by', '["six ATP and six NADPH", "three ATP and three NADPH", "nine ATP and no NADPH", "six NADH and six FADH2"]', 0, 'Six ATP phosphorylate six 3-PGA to 1,3-biphosphoglyceric acid, which NADPH then reduces to six G3P.', '{"1": "Three ATP are used later to regenerate RuBP.", "2": "NADPH is needed for reduction.", "3": "NADH and FADH2 are respiratory coenzymes."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1099', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 152 (§6.1)', 'Of the six G3P molecules made in the Calvin cycle, how many leave the cycle?', '["Two", "One", "Three", "Five"]', 1, 'One G3P leaves (and two such make glucose); the other five are used to regenerate three RuBP.', '{"0": "Only one leaves per turn of three CO2.", "2": "Three RuBP are regenerated, not three G3P released.", "3": "Five G3P are recycled."}'),
  ('BIO-1100', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 152 (§6.1)', 'During regeneration of RuBP, five G3P molecules are first converted into', '["five molecules of RuBP", "three molecules of glucose", "three molecules of ribulose phosphate", "six molecules of 3-PGA"]', 2, 'Five G3P are converted into three ribulose phosphate (RuP), each then phosphorylated by ATP to RuBP.', '{"0": "Three RuBP, not five, are regenerated.", "1": "Glucose forms from the G3P that leaves.", "3": "3-PGA is formed in fixation."}'),
  ('BIO-1101', 'Bioenergetics', 'Calvin cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 152 (§6.1)', 'Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (G3P) is significant because it is', '["the CO2 acceptor", "the same three-carbon sugar formed in glycolysis", "a five-carbon sugar", "a product of the light reactions"]', 1, 'G3P of the Calvin cycle is the same three-carbon sugar that is formed in glycolysis by splitting glucose.', '{"0": "RuBP is the CO2 acceptor.", "2": "G3P has three carbons.", "3": "G3P is made in the stroma."}'),
  ('BIO-1102', 'Bioenergetics', 'Cellular respiration', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 152 (§6.2)', 'Glucose is called respiratory fuel because', '["it is made in mitochondria", "it contains no C-H bonds", "almost all cells in all organisms use it as an energy source", "it is the only molecule that can be oxidized"]', 2, 'Almost all cells in all organisms use glucose as their energy source, so it is known as respiratory fuel.', '{"0": "Glucose is broken down, not made, in respiration.", "1": "Glucose has many C-H bonds.", "3": "Fats and proteins can also be oxidized."}'),
  ('BIO-1103', 'Bioenergetics', 'Cellular respiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 152 (§6.2)', 'Glucose stores more energy than CO2 and water because glucose has', '["more oxygen atoms", "phosphate bonds", "a ring of nitrogen atoms", "many C-H bonds"]', 3, 'Glucose has many C-H bonds while CO2 and H2O have none, so its arrangement of atoms stores more energy.', '{"0": "CO2 and water are richer in oxygen.", "1": "Glucose has no phosphate.", "2": "Glucose contains no nitrogen."}'),
  ('BIO-1104', 'Bioenergetics', 'Cellular respiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 152 (§6.2)', 'The exchange of O2 and CO2 between an organism and its environment is called', '["external respiration (breathing)", "cellular respiration", "photorespiration", "fermentation"]', 0, 'External respiration or breathing is gas exchange with the environment; cellular respiration is step-by-step oxidation of food in cells.', '{"1": "Cellular respiration happens inside cells.", "2": "Photorespiration happens in green cells in light.", "3": "Fermentation is anaerobic respiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1105', 'Bioenergetics', 'Fermentation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 153 (§6.2)', 'Anaerobic respiration of one glucose molecule yields', '["36 ATP", "4 ATP, about 10% of the energy", "2 ATP, about 2% of the energy in glucose", "no ATP"]', 2, 'Anaerobic respiration yields only two ATP, about 2% of glucose''s energy, equal to 14.6 kcal.', '{"0": "36 ATP is the aerobic net yield.", "1": "The book gives 2 ATP and 2%.", "3": "Two ATP are produced by glycolysis."}'),
  ('BIO-1106', 'Bioenergetics', 'Fermentation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 153 (§6.2)', 'In anaerobic respiration, pyruvic acid is reduced to lactic acid or alcohol by', '["FADH2", "NADPH", "oxygen", "NADH"]', 3, 'Anaerobic respiration is glycolysis followed by reduction of pyruvic acid by NADH to lactic acid or alcohol and CO2.', '{"0": "FADH2 is made in the Krebs cycle.", "1": "NADPH is used in photosynthesis.", "2": "Oxygen is absent."}'),
  ('BIO-1107', 'Bioenergetics', 'Fermentation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 153 (§6.2)', 'Lactic acid fermentation occurs in human muscle cells when', '["the muscle is at rest", "oxygen cannot be supplied as fast as it is needed during extreme activity", "glucose is absent", "blood oxygen is very high"]', 1, 'During extreme physical activity, oxygen cannot reach muscle cells quickly enough, so they use lactic acid fermentation.', '{"0": "At rest aerobic respiration suffices.", "2": "Glucose is needed for glycolysis.", "3": "High oxygen allows aerobic respiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1108', 'Bioenergetics', 'Fermentation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 153 (§6.2)', 'The products of alcoholic fermentation of pyruvic acid are', '["ethyl alcohol and carbon dioxide", "lactic acid only", "lactic acid and carbon dioxide", "acetyl CoA and NADH"]', 0, 'In alcoholic fermentation pyruvic acid is broken down into ethanol (C2H5OH) and CO2.', '{"1": "Lactic acid is made in lactic acid fermentation.", "2": "Lactic fermentation releases no CO2.", "3": "Acetyl CoA forms in aerobic pyruvate oxidation."}'),
  ('BIO-1109', 'Bioenergetics', 'Cellular respiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 154 (§6.2)', 'Early life on Earth could carry out only anaerobic respiration because', '["free oxygen was not available until photosynthesis evolved", "glucose did not exist", "mitochondria had no membranes", "ATP had not evolved"]', 0, 'Free O2 was unavailable when life evolved; it accumulated after photosynthesis evolved, making aerobic respiration possible.', '{"1": "The book links it to oxygen, not glucose.", "2": "This is not the reason given.", "3": "ATP is made by glycolysis anaerobically."}'),
  ('BIO-1110', 'Bioenergetics', 'Cellular respiration', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 154 (§6.2)', 'Which stage of aerobic respiration occurs in the cytosol?', '["Pyruvic acid oxidation", "Krebs cycle", "Electron transport chain", "Glycolysis"]', 3, 'Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol and does not need oxygen; the other three stages occur in mitochondria.', '{"0": "Pyruvic acid oxidation is in the mitochondrion.", "1": "The Krebs cycle is in the mitochondrion.", "2": "The ETC is in the inner mitochondrial membrane."}'),
  ('BIO-1111', 'Bioenergetics', 'Glycolysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 155 (§6.2)', 'In the preparatory phase of glycolysis, the first reaction converts glucose to', '["fructose 6-phosphate using NAD+", "pyruvic acid", "glucose 6-phosphate using ATP", "glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate directly"]', 2, 'A phosphate from ATP is transferred to glucose, forming glucose 6-phosphate.', '{"0": "Fructose 6-phosphate forms in step 2 without NAD+.", "1": "Pyruvic acid is the end product.", "3": "G3P forms after fructose 1,6-biphosphate splits."}'),
  ('BIO-1112', 'Bioenergetics', 'Glycolysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 155 (§6.2)', 'Fructose 1,6-biphosphate splits into', '["two molecules of pyruvic acid", "glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate and dihydroxyacetone phosphate", "glucose and fructose", "3-PGA and 2-PGA"]', 1, 'The reactive fructose 1,6-biphosphate breaks into G3P and DAP, which are interconverted to give two G3P.', '{"0": "Pyruvic acid forms at the end.", "2": "These are hexoses, not products of the split.", "3": "These form later in the oxidative phase."}'),
  ('BIO-1113', 'Bioenergetics', 'Glycolysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 155 (§6.2)', 'How many ATP are spent in the preparatory phase of glycolysis per glucose?', '["One", "Four", "None", "Two"]', 3, 'One ATP phosphorylates glucose and a second phosphorylates fructose 6-phosphate.', '{"0": "Two phosphorylation steps use ATP.", "1": "Four ATP are made in the oxidative phase.", "2": "The preparatory phase spends energy."}'),
  ('BIO-1114', 'Bioenergetics', 'Glycolysis', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'When G3P is oxidized in glycolysis, it is converted to', '["1,3-biphosphoglyceric acid, with NAD+ reduced to NADH", "3-phosphoglyceric acid with ATP made", "pyruvic acid with CO2 released", "phosphoenol pyruvic acid with water removed"]', 0, 'Two hydrogen atoms are removed from G3P and passed to NAD+, and an inorganic phosphate is added, forming 1,3-BPGA.', '{"1": "3-PGA forms in the next step.", "2": "No CO2 is released in glycolysis.", "3": "PEP forms by dehydrating 2-PGA."}'),
  ('BIO-1115', 'Bioenergetics', 'Glycolysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'Phosphoenol pyruvic acid (PEP) is formed in glycolysis by', '["adding CO2 to pyruvic acid", "oxidizing G3P", "removing water from 2-phosphoglyceric acid", "adding phosphate to pyruvic acid"]', 2, '2-PGA is dehydrated (water removed) to form PEP.', '{"0": "Adding CO2 to PEP happens in C-4 plants.", "1": "Oxidizing G3P gives 1,3-BPGA.", "3": "The pathway runs from PEP to pyruvic acid, not the reverse."}'),
  ('BIO-1116', 'Bioenergetics', 'Glycolysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'Gluconeogenesis is', '["the breakdown of glycogen", "the formation of glucose from pyruvic acid by reversing glycolysis", "the conversion of fats to glycerol", "the fermentation of glucose to ethanol"]', 1, 'Pyruvic acid can be turned back into glucose by reversing glycolysis; this is gluconeogenesis.', '{"0": "This is not the book''s definition.", "2": "Fat hydrolysis gives glycerol and fatty acids.", "3": "That is alcoholic fermentation."}'),
  ('BIO-1117', 'Bioenergetics', 'Glycolysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'Glucose enters cells from tissue fluid by', '["active transport using ATP", "passive transport through a specific carrier that can be gated by insulin", "simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer", "endocytosis"]', 1, 'Glucose enters by passive transport using a specific glucose carrier that can be controlled (gated) by hormones such as insulin.', '{"0": "The book says passive transport.", "2": "A specific carrier is used.", "3": "Endocytosis is not mentioned."}'),
  ('BIO-1118', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'During pyruvic acid oxidation, the correct sequence of intermediates is', '["pyruvic acid → acetyl group → acetaldehyde → acetyl CoA", "pyruvic acid → lactic acid → acetyl CoA", "pyruvic acid → citric acid → acetyl CoA", "pyruvic acid → acetaldehyde → acetyl group → acetyl CoA"]', 3, 'CO2 is removed to form acetaldehyde, which is oxidized to an acetyl group (NAD+ → NADH) that joins coenzyme A.', '{"0": "Acetaldehyde comes before the acetyl group.", "1": "Lactic acid is a fermentation product.", "2": "Citric acid forms after acetyl CoA enters the Krebs cycle."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1119', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'During conversion of one pyruvic acid to acetyl CoA, which is produced?', '["One CO2 and one NADH", "Two ATP", "One FADH2", "Oxygen"]', 0, 'One CO2 is removed and acetaldehyde is oxidized, reducing one NAD+ to NADH.', '{"1": "No ATP is made in this stage.", "2": "FADH2 is made in the Krebs cycle.", "3": "Oxygen is consumed, not produced, in respiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1120', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 157 (§6.2)', 'The first step of the Krebs cycle forms citric acid by combining an acetyl group with', '["pyruvic acid (3C)", "alpha-ketoglutaric acid (5C)", "oxaloacetic acid (4C)", "malic acid (4C)"]', 2, 'The two-carbon acetyl group joins four-carbon oxaloacetic acid to form six-carbon citric acid.', '{"0": "Pyruvic acid is converted to acetyl CoA first.", "1": "Alpha-ketoglutaric acid forms from citric acid.", "3": "Malic acid is oxidized to regenerate oxaloacetic acid."}'),
  ('BIO-1121', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 157 (§6.2)', 'Oxidative decarboxylation of citric acid produces', '["alpha-ketoglutaric acid (5C)", "succinic acid (4C)", "fumaric acid (4C)", "oxaloacetic acid (4C)"]', 0, 'Citric acid is decarboxylated and oxidized (NAD+ → NADH) to form five-carbon alpha-ketoglutaric acid.', '{"1": "Succinic acid forms from alpha-ketoglutaric acid.", "2": "Fumaric acid forms from succinic acid.", "3": "Oxaloacetic acid forms from malic acid."}'),
  ('BIO-1122', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 157 (§6.2)', 'Succinic acid is oxidized to fumaric acid using FAD instead of NAD+ because', '["NAD+ is used up in glycolysis", "FAD carries oxygen", "the reaction releases CO2", "the energy released is not enough to reduce NAD+"]', 3, 'When two hydrogens are removed from succinic acid, the free energy is not enough to reduce NAD+, so FAD is reduced to FADH2.', '{"0": "NAD+ is regenerated and available.", "1": "FAD carries hydrogen and electrons.", "2": "No CO2 is released at this step."}'),
  ('BIO-1123', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 158 (§6.2)', 'Fumaric acid is converted to malic acid by', '["removing CO2", "reducing FAD", "adding a molecule of water", "adding ATP"]', 2, 'To regenerate oxaloacetic acid, water is added to fumaric acid, changing it to malic acid.', '{"0": "No decarboxylation here.", "1": "FAD is reduced in the previous step.", "3": "No ATP is used here."}'),
  ('BIO-1124', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 157 (§6.2)', 'Which Krebs cycle conversion releases carbon dioxide?', '["Malic acid to oxaloacetic acid", "Alpha-ketoglutaric acid to succinic acid", "Fumaric acid to malic acid", "Succinic acid to fumaric acid"]', 1, 'Alpha-ketoglutaric acid undergoes oxidation and decarboxylation to the four-carbon succinic acid (CO2 is also released from citric acid).', '{"0": "This oxidation reduces NAD+ but removes no carbon.", "2": "This step adds water.", "3": "This step reduces FAD and releases no CO2."}'),
  ('BIO-1125', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'Which statement about respiration does the book say is more correct?', '["Respiration turns oxygen into carbon dioxide", "Respiration turns water into oxygen", "Respiration turns carbon dioxide into glucose", "Respiration turns glucose into carbon dioxide and oxygen into water"]', 3, 'CO2 is released before oxygen is involved, so respiration turns glucose into CO2 and oxygen into water.', '{"0": "The CO2 comes from glucose carbons.", "1": "That is photolysis in photosynthesis.", "2": "That is photosynthesis."}'),
  ('BIO-1126', 'Bioenergetics', 'Krebs cycle', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 156 (§6.2)', 'The Krebs cycle is named after', '["the six-carbon molecule formed in its first step", "a British biochemist, Sir Hans Krebs", "Melvin Calvin", "a plant family"]', 1, 'Much of the cycle was worked out by Sir Hans Krebs; it is also called the citric acid cycle after the six-carbon citric acid.', '{"0": "That is the source of its other name, citric acid cycle.", "2": "Calvin worked out the dark reactions.", "3": "CAM is named after a plant family."}'),
  ('BIO-1127', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 158 (§6.2)', 'The electron transport chain of respiration is located in the', '["outer mitochondrial membrane", "mitochondrial matrix", "inner mitochondrial membrane", "cytosol"]', 2, 'The respiratory ETC is built into the inner membrane of the mitochondrion.', '{"0": "The outer membrane does not hold the ETC.", "1": "The matrix holds Krebs enzymes.", "3": "Glycolysis is in the cytosol."}'),
  ('BIO-1128', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 158 (§6.2)', 'The correct order of carriers in the respiratory electron transport chain is', '["coenzyme Q → cytochrome b → cytochrome c → cytochrome a complex", "cytochrome a complex → cytochrome c → cytochrome b → coenzyme Q", "coenzyme Q → cytochrome c → cytochrome b → cytochrome a complex", "plastoquinone → cytochrome complex → plastocyanin"]', 0, 'Electrons from NADH go to coenzyme Q, then cytochrome b, cytochrome c and the cytochrome a complex, which passes them to oxygen.', '{"1": "This is reversed.", "2": "Cytochrome b comes before cytochrome c.", "3": "These are chloroplast carriers."}'),
  ('BIO-1129', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 158 (§6.2)', 'The final electron acceptor in the respiratory electron transport chain is', '["NAD+", "oxygen", "coenzyme Q", "NADP+"]', 1, 'The cytochrome a complex passes electrons to oxygen, which takes up hydrogen ions to form water.', '{"0": "NAD+ is reduced in glycolysis and Krebs.", "2": "CoQ is the first carrier.", "3": "NADP+ is the final acceptor in photosynthesis."}'),
  ('BIO-1130', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 158 (§6.2)', 'Electrons from FADH2 enter the respiratory chain at', '["coenzyme Q", "cytochrome c", "cytochrome a complex", "oxygen directly"]', 0, 'When FADH2 is oxidized its electrons also move to coenzyme Q.', '{"1": "Cytochrome c is further along.", "2": "The cytochrome a complex is at the end.", "3": "Oxygen is the final acceptor."}'),
  ('BIO-1131', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 159 (§6.2)', 'In mitochondria, H+ ions are pumped by the electron transport chain', '["from the intermembrane space into the matrix", "from the stroma into the lumen", "from the matrix into the intermembrane space", "from the cytosol into the matrix"]', 2, 'Energy from electrons pumps H+ from the matrix to the intermembrane space; H+ flows back through ATP synthase.', '{"0": "This is the direction of flow through ATP synthase.", "1": "This is the chloroplast direction.", "3": "The cytosol is not involved."}'),
  ('BIO-1132', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 159 (§6.2)', 'Oxidation of one NADH in the electron transport chain produces', '["one ATP", "two ATP", "three ATP", "four ATP"]', 2, 'Oxidation of one NADH produces three ATP, while one FADH2 produces two.', '{"0": "Too low.", "1": "Two ATP come from each FADH2.", "3": "Too high."}'),
  ('BIO-1133', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 159 (§6.2)', 'Substrate-level phosphorylation differs from chemiosmosis because it', '["needs light", "uses an H+ gradient", "makes most of the cell''s ATP", "needs no membrane or electron transport chain"]', 3, 'In substrate-level phosphorylation an enzyme transfers phosphate from an organic substrate to ADP without a membrane or ETC.', '{"0": "It does not need light.", "1": "The H+ gradient drives chemiosmosis.", "2": "It makes only a small percentage of ATP."}'),
  ('BIO-1134', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 159 (§6.2)', 'An example of substrate-level phosphorylation given in the book is', '["ATP synthesis by ATP synthase", "reduction of NADP+ by ferredoxin", "oxidation of NADH by coenzyme Q", "transfer of phosphate from PEP to ADP in glycolysis"]', 3, 'In the last step of glycolysis an enzyme transfers phosphate from PEP to ADP, giving ATP and pyruvic acid.', '{"0": "ATP synthase works by chemiosmosis.", "1": "That reduces NADP+; it makes no ATP.", "2": "That is electron transport."}'),
  ('BIO-1135', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.2)', 'Each NADH from glycolysis gives only two ATP in the final balance because', '["transporting it into the mitochondrion costs one ATP", "it is oxidized by FAD", "it enters the chain at cytochrome c", "it is used in fermentation"]', 0, 'Glycolytic NADH is made in the cytoplasm; carrying it across the mitochondrial membrane costs one ATP, so it yields two instead of three.', '{"1": "NADH is not oxidized by FAD.", "2": "The book explains it by transport cost.", "3": "In aerobic respiration it goes to the ETC."}'),
  ('BIO-1136', 'Bioenergetics', 'Electron transport chain', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.2)', 'According to the book, aerobic oxidation of one glucose molecule yields a net', '["2 ATP", "18 ATP", "36 ATP", "38 ATP"]', 2, 'Aerobic oxidation gives a net 36 ATP against 2 ATP in anaerobic respiration, making it 18 times more efficient.', '{"0": "2 ATP is the anaerobic yield.", "1": "18 is the efficiency ratio.", "3": "The book''s net figure is 36."}'),
  ('BIO-1137', 'Bioenergetics', 'Other fuels', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.2)', 'Before amino acids are used as respiratory fuel, they are', '["converted to glycerol", "converted directly to glucose in the cytosol", "deaminated, then converted to pyruvic acid, acetyl CoA or Krebs cycle acids", "stored as fat"]', 2, 'Some amino acids are deaminated and converted to compounds that enter as pyruvic acid, acetyl CoA or Krebs cycle acids.', '{"0": "Glycerol comes from fats.", "1": "The book describes entry into respiration, not glucose formation.", "3": "This is not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1138', 'Bioenergetics', 'Other fuels', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.2)', 'When fats are respired, glycerol enters respiration as', '["acetyl CoA", "glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate", "pyruvic acid", "citric acid"]', 1, 'Glycerol is converted to G3P, an intermediate of glycolysis; fatty acids are changed into acetyl CoA.', '{"0": "Fatty acids become acetyl CoA.", "2": "The book names G3P.", "3": "Citric acid forms in the Krebs cycle."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1139', 'Bioenergetics', 'Other fuels', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.2)', 'Lipids are excellent cellular fuel because they', '["are soluble in water", "contain nitrogen", "do not need to be hydrolysed", "contain many carbon-hydrogen bonds"]', 3, 'Lipids have many C-H bonds; they are hydrolysed to glycerol and fatty acids before entering respiration.', '{"0": "Lipids are insoluble in water.", "1": "Fats lack nitrogen.", "2": "They are first hydrolysed."}'),
  ('BIO-1140', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.3)', 'Photorespiration differs from aerobic respiration in that it', '["does not need oxygen", "produces no ATP", "releases no CO2", "occurs only at night"]', 1, 'Photorespiration uses O2 and releases CO2 like aerobic respiration, but no ATP is produced.', '{"0": "It does use oxygen.", "2": "It releases CO2.", "3": "It happens in green cells in light."}'),
  ('BIO-1141', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.3)', 'Rubisco adds O2 instead of CO2 to RuBP when', '["light intensity is low", "CO2 concentration is high", "CO2 concentration falls and oxygen in the leaf rises", "temperature is low"]', 2, 'When the relative CO2 concentration decreases and oxygen increases in leaf cells, rubisco adds O2 to RuBP.', '{"0": "The book links it to gas levels, not dim light.", "1": "High CO2 favours carbon fixation.", "3": "High temperature favours the oxidative activity."}'),
  ('BIO-1142', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.3)', 'When rubisco adds O2 to RuBP, the products are', '["one phosphoglycerate and one phosphoglycolate", "two phosphoglycerates", "oxaloacetate and pyruvate", "glucose and CO2"]', 0, 'Adding O2 breaks RuBP into one phosphoglycerate and one two-carbon phosphoglycolate.', '{"1": "Two phosphoglycerates form when CO2 is added.", "2": "These belong to C-4 metabolism.", "3": "No glucose is made."}'),
  ('BIO-1143', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 161 (§6.3)', 'In photorespiration, glycolate is converted to glyoxylate in the', '["peroxisome, producing hydrogen peroxide", "mitochondrion, producing ATP", "chloroplast, producing NADPH", "cytosol, producing CO2"]', 0, 'Glycolate moves to the peroxisome and is metabolized to glyoxylate using O2; toxic H2O2 is also produced.', '{"1": "Glycine is converted in the mitochondrion, and no ATP is made.", "2": "Glycolate leaves the chloroplast.", "3": "This is in the peroxisome."}'),
  ('BIO-1144', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 162 (§6.3)', 'In photorespiration, two molecules of glycine form serine in the', '["peroxisome", "mitochondrion", "chloroplast", "nucleus"]', 1, 'Glycine is transported to the mitochondrion where two glycines form one serine.', '{"0": "Serine goes back to the peroxisome to become glycerate.", "2": "Glycerate becomes phosphoglycerate in the chloroplast.", "3": "The nucleus is not involved."}'),
  ('BIO-1145', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 162 (§6.3)', 'C-3 plants may lose how much of their fixed carbon to photorespiration?', '["1% to 2%", "about 10%", "25% to 50%", "about 90%"]', 2, 'Photorespiring C-3 plants lose between 25% and 50% of their fixed carbon, reducing yields.', '{"0": "1–2% is the share of solar energy converted.", "1": "Too low.", "3": "Too high."}'),
  ('BIO-1146', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 162 (§6.3)', 'Photorespiration becomes a severe problem', '["in cold climates below 10 °C", "in darkness", "when CO2 levels are very high", "in tropical climates where temperature is often above 28 °C"]', 3, 'At higher temperatures rubisco''s oxidative activity rises; in tropics often above 28 °C the problem is severe.', '{"0": "Low temperature does not worsen it.", "1": "It needs light.", "2": "High CO2 reduces it."}'),
  ('BIO-1147', 'Bioenergetics', 'Photorespiration', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 162 (§6.3)', 'There was little photorespiration when photosynthesis first evolved because', '["the atmosphere contained little oxygen", "rubisco had not evolved", "temperatures were low", "plants had no stomata"]', 0, 'Early on there was little O2 in the air; competition between CO2 and O2 for rubisco''s active site began after O2 accumulated.', '{"1": "Rubisco was carrying out carbon fixation.", "2": "Temperature is not the reason given.", "3": "The book explains it by oxygen level."}'),
  ('BIO-1148', 'Bioenergetics', 'C-4 and CAM', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 162 (§6.3)', 'Which group of plants carries out C-4 photosynthesis?', '["Wheat, rice and peas", "Cacti and pineapple only", "Mosses and ferns", "Corn, sugarcane and sorghum"]', 3, 'Grasses such as corn, sugarcane and sorghum, and about two dozen other groups, run C-4 photosynthesis.', '{"0": "The book does not list these as C-4.", "1": "Cacti and pineapple are CAM examples.", "2": "These are not given as C-4 examples."}'),
  ('BIO-1149', 'Bioenergetics', 'C-4 and CAM', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 163 (§6.3)', 'In C-4 plants, CO2 is first attached in mesophyll cells to', '["RuBP, giving 3-PGA", "phosphoenol pyruvic acid, giving oxaloacetic acid", "pyruvic acid, giving malic acid directly", "glycerate, giving serine"]', 1, 'CO2 joins the three-carbon PEP in mesophyll cells, forming four-carbon oxaloacetic acid; hence ''C-4''.', '{"0": "This is C-3 fixation by rubisco.", "2": "Malic acid forms from oxaloacetic acid.", "3": "These are photorespiration intermediates."}'),
  ('BIO-1150', 'Bioenergetics', 'C-4 and CAM', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 163 (§6.3)', 'In C-4 plants, malic acid releases CO2 in', '["mesophyll cells, where PEP is regenerated", "guard cells", "bundle-sheath cells, where the Calvin cycle runs", "the peroxisome"]', 2, 'Malic acid moves to bundle-sheath cells and breaks into pyruvic acid and CO2; the high CO2 there lets the Calvin cycle run instead of photorespiration.', '{"0": "Pyruvic acid returns to the mesophyll to be converted to PEP.", "1": "Guard cells are not involved.", "3": "The peroxisome is part of photorespiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1151', 'Bioenergetics', 'C-4 and CAM', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 163 (§6.3)', 'In C-4 plants, pyruvic acid returning to a mesophyll cell is converted back to PEP using', '["NADPH", "oxygen", "rubisco", "ATP"]', 3, 'Pyruvic acid from the bundle-sheath cell returns to the mesophyll and is reconverted to PEP using an ATP.', '{"0": "NADH is used to make malic acid, and NADPH is not named here.", "1": "Oxygen is not used.", "2": "Rubisco works in the bundle sheath."}'),
  ('BIO-1152', 'Bioenergetics', 'C-4 and CAM', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 163 (§6.3)', 'CAM plants reduce water loss by', '["opening their stomata only at noon", "opening their stomata at night and closing them in the day", "having no stomata", "keeping stomata open all the time"]', 1, 'In CAM plants stomata open at night and close in the day, preventing water loss; CO2 comes from organic acids made the night before.', '{"0": "Opening at noon would increase water loss.", "2": "They have stomata.", "3": "This would waste water."}'),
  ('BIO-1153', 'Bioenergetics', 'C-4 and CAM', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 163 (§6.3)', 'CAM is named after', '["the plant family Crassulaceae, in which it was first discovered", "the scientist who discovered it", "cactus and maize", "the carbon atoms of malic acid"]', 0, 'Crassulacean acid metabolism is named after the plant family Crassulaceae, where it was first discovered.', '{"1": "It is named after a plant family.", "2": "Maize is a C-4 plant.", "3": "The name refers to the family."}'),
  ('BIO-1154', 'Bioenergetics', 'C-4 and CAM', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Bioenergetics, p. 163 (§6.3)', 'A key difference between C-4 and CAM plants is that', '["only C-4 plants use the Calvin cycle", "only CAM plants use PEP", "CAM separates the two fixation steps in time, while C-4 separates them between cell types", "C-4 plants open stomata only at night"]', 2, 'C-4 plants fix CO2 in mesophyll cells and run the Calvin cycle in bundle-sheath cells; CAM plants fix CO2 at night and use it by day. Both use C-4 and C-3 pathways.', '{"0": "Both run the Calvin cycle.", "1": "Both fix CO2 into organic acids first.", "3": "Night opening is the CAM pattern."}'),
  ('BIO-1155', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 167 (Intro)', 'Structural biology studies', '["the three-dimensional structures of macromolecules at the atomic level", "the classification of organisms", "the anatomy of human bones", "the distribution of species in ecosystems"]', 0, 'Structural biology deals with the 3D structures of macromolecules, including proteins and nucleic acids, at atomic levels.', '{"1": "That is taxonomy.", "2": "Skeletal anatomy is not the subject.", "3": "That is ecology."}'),
  ('BIO-1156', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 167 (§7.1)', 'Structural studies of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase identified two domains, a polymerase domain and', '["a spike domain that binds human receptors", "a protease domain that digests capsid proteins", "an RNase H domain that breaks down the RNA strand of RNA-DNA hybrids", "a lipid domain that forms the envelope"]', 2, 'HIV-1 reverse transcriptase has a polymerase domain, which synthesizes DNA, and an RNase H domain, which breaks down RNA in RNA-DNA hybrids.', '{"0": "The spike protein belongs to coronavirus.", "1": "This domain is not described.", "3": "Domains here are parts of the enzyme, not the envelope."}'),
  ('BIO-1157', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 167 (§7.1)', 'Knowing the structure of the domains of HIV-1 reverse transcriptase has helped in', '["making the virus grow faster in culture", "classifying HIV as a bacterium", "producing insulin", "designing antiviral drugs that target them specifically"]', 3, 'Knowing the location and structure of these domains has helped design antiviral drugs that specifically target them.', '{"0": "The aim is to block the virus.", "1": "HIV is a virus.", "2": "Insulin is unrelated."}'),
  ('BIO-1158', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 167 (§7.1)', 'The book uses serine proteases as an example of an enzyme whose structure reveals', '["a domain that synthesizes DNA", "a well-defined active site that breaks peptide bonds", "a receptor for the coronavirus", "a misfolded shape causing disease"]', 1, 'The structure of serine proteases shows a well-defined active site responsible for breaking down peptide bonds.', '{"0": "DNA synthesis is the role of the reverse-transcriptase polymerase domain.", "2": "The coronavirus receptor is a human cell protein.", "3": "Misfolding is a separate application."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1159', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 168 (§7.1)', 'In COVID-19 research, structural biology identified which molecule as a drug target?', '["Human haemoglobin", "Bacterial cell wall", "The spike protein of SARS-CoV-2", "Viral RNase H domain"]', 2, 'Scientists studied the 3D structure of the coronavirus spike protein, which helps the virus enter human cells, and identified it as a drug target.', '{"0": "Haemoglobin is not the target described.", "1": "SARS-CoV-2 is a virus, not a bacterium.", "3": "RNase H is a domain of HIV reverse transcriptase."}'),
  ('BIO-1160', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 168 (§7.1)', 'Disease-causing molecules, usually proteins, at which a drug can act are called', '["ligands", "antigens only", "vectors", "drug targets"]', 3, 'Places on disease-causing molecules where a drug can work, usually proteins, are called drug targets.', '{"0": "Ligands are molecules that bind macromolecules.", "1": "Not the term used.", "2": "Vectors carry genes or pathogens."}'),
  ('BIO-1161', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 168 (§7.1)', 'A drug that binds the human receptor for the coronavirus spike protein would', '["block the virus from entering host cells", "help the virus enter host cells", "destroy human DNA", "increase spike protein production"]', 0, 'Drugs that bind the receptor protein inhibit its interaction with the virus and so block viral entry.', '{"1": "The aim is to prevent entry.", "2": "The drug acts on a receptor, not DNA.", "3": "It blocks binding, not production."}'),
  ('BIO-1162', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 168 (§7.1)', 'Studying how a pathogen''s proteins attach to and enter host cells is the study of', '["protein misfolding", "host-pathogen interaction", "gene duplication", "speciation"]', 1, 'Understanding how pathogens interact with host body cells is called host-pathogen interaction.', '{"0": "Misfolding concerns incorrect protein shapes.", "2": "Gene duplication produces paralogs.", "3": "Speciation produces orthologs."}'),
  ('BIO-1163', 'Biological molecules', 'Structural biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 169 (§7.1)', 'Which disease does the book list as caused by incorrect protein folding?', '["Tuberculosis", "Parkinson''s disease", "Malaria", "Scurvy"]', 1, 'Cystic fibrosis, Parkinson''s and Alzheimer''s diseases originate from incorrect folding of proteins.', '{"0": "Tuberculosis is a bacterial infection.", "2": "Malaria is caused by a protist.", "3": "Scurvy is a vitamin C deficiency."}'),
  ('BIO-1164', 'Biological molecules', 'X-ray crystallography', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 169 (§7.2)', 'X-ray crystallography was developed in 1912 by', '["Watson and Crick", "Melvin Calvin", "William Henry Bragg and William Lawrence Bragg", "Engelmann"]', 2, 'The Braggs developed X-ray crystallography in 1912 and received the 1915 Nobel Prize in Physics.', '{"0": "Watson and Crick proposed the DNA model.", "1": "Calvin worked on dark reactions.", "3": "Engelmann made the first action spectrum."}'),
  ('BIO-1165', 'Biological molecules', 'X-ray crystallography', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 169 (§7.2)', 'The Braggs received the Nobel Prize for X-ray crystallography in', '["1915, in Physics", "1912, in Chemistry", "1961, in Chemistry", "1929, in Medicine"]', 0, 'They developed the technique in 1912 and received the 1915 Nobel Prize in Physics.', '{"1": "1912 is the year of development.", "2": "1961 is Calvin''s prize.", "3": "1929 is when ATP was discovered."}'),
  ('BIO-1166', 'Biological molecules', 'X-ray crystallography', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 169 (§7.2)', 'In X-ray crystallography, a 3D picture of a molecule is built from', '["the colour of the crystal", "the weight of the crystal", "light absorbed by chlorophyll", "the angles and intensities of X-rays diffracted by the crystal"]', 3, 'Atoms in the crystal diffract X-rays; from the angles and intensities of the diffracted beams an electron-density picture is produced.', '{"0": "Colour gives no atomic positions.", "1": "Mass is not used.", "2": "That describes absorption spectra."}'),
  ('BIO-1167', 'Biological molecules', 'X-ray crystallography', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 169 (§7.2)', 'Proteins must be crystallized for X-ray study because crystals', '["make proteins enzymatically active", "arrange the molecules in a regular, repeating pattern", "remove all water from the cell", "turn proteins into nucleic acids"]', 1, 'Crystals arrange protein molecules in a regular repeating pattern, which is needed for a clear image.', '{"0": "Crystallization is for imaging, not activity.", "2": "This is not the purpose.", "3": "Proteins stay proteins."}'),
  ('BIO-1168', 'Biological molecules', 'X-ray crystallography', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 170 (§7.2)', 'During protein crystallization, scientists must carefully control', '["light intensity and CO2", "oxygen and glucose levels", "temperature, pH and salt concentration", "magnetic field and voltage"]', 2, 'Crystallization can take hours to weeks and needs careful control of temperature, pH and salt concentration.', '{"0": "These matter for photosynthesis.", "1": "These are not listed.", "3": "These are not listed."}'),
  ('BIO-1169', 'Biological molecules', 'X-ray crystallography', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 170 (§7.2)', 'The correct order of steps in determining a protein structure by X-ray crystallography is', '["crystallization → diffraction pattern → density map → computed structure", "diffraction pattern → crystallization → density map → computed structure", "density map → crystallization → diffraction pattern → computed structure", "crystallization → density map → diffraction pattern → computed structure"]', 0, 'A crystal is grown, bombarded with X-rays to give a diffraction pattern, which is used to make a density map, then analysed to give the 3D structure.', '{"1": "A crystal is needed before diffraction.", "2": "The map is made from diffraction data.", "3": "The pattern comes before the map."}'),
  ('BIO-1170', 'Biological molecules', 'X-ray crystallography', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 170 (§7.2)', 'In X-ray crystallography, the diffraction pattern appears on the detector as', '["a continuous rainbow spectrum", "a coloured band of DNA", "a single straight line", "a series of spots"]', 3, 'Atoms in the crystal diffract the beam, creating a diffraction pattern, a series of spots, on the detectors.', '{"0": "Not a spectrum of light.", "1": "DNA bands come from electrophoresis.", "2": "It is a pattern of many spots."}'),
  ('BIO-1171', 'Biological molecules', 'Computational biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 170 (§7.3)', 'Computational biology integrates biology with', '["only chemistry", "only physics", "geology and astronomy", "computer science, mathematics and statistics"]', 3, 'Computational biology is interdisciplinary, combining biology, computer science, mathematics and statistics.', '{"0": "Chemistry alone is not the combination.", "1": "Physics alone is not it.", "2": "These are not part of the definition."}'),
  ('BIO-1172', 'Biological molecules', 'Computational biology', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 170 (§7.3)', 'The study of the complete set of DNA in a cell of an organism is', '["proteomics", "bioinformatics", "genomics", "crystallography"]', 2, 'Genomics studies genomes, the complete set of DNA in a single cell, including sequencing, assembly and analysis.', '{"0": "Proteomics studies proteins.", "1": "Bioinformatics is the computer management of biological data.", "3": "Crystallography determines 3D structure."}'),
  ('BIO-1173', 'Biological molecules', 'Computational biology', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 171 (§7.3)', 'The entire set of proteins produced by an organism is its', '["genome", "proteome", "phenotype", "karyotype"]', 1, 'Proteomics aims to map the proteome, the entire set of proteins produced by an organism.', '{"0": "The genome is the set of DNA.", "2": "Phenotype is the observable traits.", "3": "Karyotype is the set of chromosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-1174', 'Biological molecules', 'Computational biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 171 (§7.3)', 'Bioinformatics is best described as', '["the use of computer technology to store, retrieve and analyse biological data", "the growing of protein crystals", "the large-scale study of proteins", "the classification of fossils"]', 0, 'Bioinformatics applies computer technology to manage and analyse DNA, RNA and protein sequences.', '{"1": "Crystal growing is part of X-ray crystallography.", "2": "That is proteomics.", "3": "That is palaeontology."}'),
  ('BIO-1175', 'Biological molecules', 'Computational biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 171 (§7.3)', 'Computational biology aids personalized medicine mainly by', '["identifying genetic variations associated with diseases", "growing crystals of drugs", "measuring blood pressure", "counting bacteria in samples"]', 0, 'By analysing DNA sequences, computational biology identifies disease-linked variations, which can lead to personalized medicine.', '{"1": "Crystallization is not this application.", "2": "Not a computational application.", "3": "Not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1176', 'Biological molecules', 'Databases and tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 172 (§7.3)', 'GenBank is a public database of', '["3D protein structures", "genome browser maps only", "fossil records", "nucleotide sequences"]', 3, 'GenBank is a comprehensive public database of nucleotide sequences with bibliographic and biological annotations.', '{"0": "3D structures are in the Protein Data Bank.", "1": "Ensembl is the genome browser.", "2": "Not a fossil database."}'),
  ('BIO-1177', 'Biological molecules', 'Databases and tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 172 (§7.3)', 'The Protein Data Bank (PDB) provides', '["DNA sequence annotations only", "3D structural data of large biological molecules", "a tool for sequence alignment", "clinical records of patients"]', 1, 'PDB provides 3D structural data of proteins and nucleic acids, used to study function and design drugs.', '{"0": "That is GenBank.", "2": "Alignment tools are BLAST and FASTA.", "3": "Not a clinical database."}'),
  ('BIO-1178', 'Biological molecules', 'Databases and tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 172 (§7.3)', 'Ensembl is a', '["database of 3D protein structures", "sequence alignment algorithm", "genome browser for genome sequences, gene models and comparative genomics", "X-ray machine"]', 2, 'Ensembl is a genome browser providing information on genome sequences, gene models and comparative genomics for various species.', '{"0": "That is PDB.", "1": "BLAST and FASTA are algorithms.", "3": "It is a database."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1179', 'Biological molecules', 'Databases and tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 172 (§7.3)', 'BLAST and FASTA are both', '["databases of 3D structures", "tools that compare sequences to find similarity", "methods of growing crystals", "genome browsers"]', 1, 'BLAST compares primary sequence information and FASTA compares a query sequence to a database to find regions of similarity.', '{"0": "PDB stores 3D structures.", "2": "Crystals are grown in the lab.", "3": "Ensembl is a genome browser."}'),
  ('BIO-1180', 'Biological molecules', 'Homology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 173 (§7.4)', 'Sequence homology is similarity between sequences due to', '["shared ancestry", "chance alone", "identical environments", "similar crystal shapes"]', 0, 'Sequence homology refers to similarity between DNA, RNA or protein sequences because of shared ancestry.', '{"1": "Homology implies common descent.", "2": "Environment is not the basis.", "3": "Crystal shape is unrelated."}'),
  ('BIO-1181', 'Biological molecules', 'Homology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 173 (§7.4)', 'Homologous genes in different species that arose from a common ancestral gene during speciation are', '["paralogs", "analogs", "orthologs", "alleles"]', 2, 'Orthologs are sequences in different species from a common ancestral gene during speciation; they often keep the same function.', '{"0": "Paralogs arise by gene duplication within a species.", "1": "Analogs are not defined here and are not homologous.", "3": "Alleles are variants of one gene."}'),
  ('BIO-1182', 'Biological molecules', 'Homology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 173 (§7.4)', 'Human histone H1.1 and human histone H1.2 arose by gene duplication within one species. They are', '["orthologs", "analogs", "unrelated genes", "paralogs"]', 3, 'Paralogs are sequences within the same species that originated from gene duplication.', '{"0": "Orthologs are in different species.", "1": "They share an ancestral gene, so they are homologous.", "2": "They share an ancestral histone gene."}'),
  ('BIO-1183', 'Biological molecules', 'Homology', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 173 (§7.4)', 'Human histone H1.1 and chimpanzee histone H1.1 are best described as', '["orthologs", "paralogs", "products of gene duplication within humans", "unrelated sequences"]', 0, 'They are the same gene in two species, separated by speciation, so they are orthologs.', '{"1": "Paralogs are in the same species.", "2": "They are in different species.", "3": "They share an ancestor."}'),
  ('BIO-1184', 'Biological molecules', 'Homology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 173 (§7.4)', 'If an unknown protein is homologous to a protein of known function, scientists infer that the unknown protein', '["must be from the same species", "has no function", "probably has a similar function", "is a misfolded protein"]', 2, 'Sequence homology gives a clue about function; an unknown gene or protein homologous to a known one is likely to work similarly.', '{"0": "Orthologs are in different species.", "1": "Homology suggests function.", "3": "Homology says nothing about misfolding."}'),
  ('BIO-1185', 'Biological molecules', 'Homology', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Structural and Computational Biology, p. 173 (§7.4)', 'Proteins with similar 3D structures due to shared ancestry often', '["have identical amino acid sequences", "come only from bacteria", "cannot be used in drug design", "perform similar functions even when their sequences are not very similar"]', 3, 'Structural homology means similar 3D structures from shared ancestry; such proteins often work similarly even with low sequence similarity.', '{"0": "Sequences can differ.", "1": "Not limited to bacteria.", "2": "Structural homology is crucial in drug design."}'),
  ('BIO-1186', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'How many macronutrients do plants need, according to the book?', '["Seven", "Sixteen", "Five", "Nine"]', 3, 'There are nine macronutrients: C, H, O, N, K, Ca, P, Mg and S.', '{"0": "Seven is the number of micronutrients.", "1": "Sixteen is the total of both groups.", "2": "Too few."}'),
  ('BIO-1187', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'Which of the following is a micronutrient for plants?', '["Magnesium", "Molybdenum", "Sulphur", "Calcium"]', 1, 'The seven micronutrients are iron, manganese, zinc, molybdenum, copper, chlorine and boron.', '{"0": "Magnesium is a macronutrient.", "2": "Sulphur is a macronutrient.", "3": "Calcium is a macronutrient."}'),
  ('BIO-1188', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'A plant shows leaf loss and stunted growth. The most likely deficiency is of', '["nitrogen", "chlorine", "phosphorus", "potassium"]', 0, 'Nitrogen deficiency results in leaf loss and stunted growth.', '{"1": "Chlorine deficiency is described only as affecting health and growth.", "2": "Phosphorus deficiency delays flowering and browns and wrinkles leaves.", "3": "Potassium deficiency forms dark patches on leaves."}'),
  ('BIO-1189', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'Delayed flowering with browning and wrinkling of leaves suggests a shortage of', '["calcium", "sulphur", "phosphorus", "nitrogen"]', 2, 'Phosphorus is part of ATP, promotes root growth and flowering; its deficiency delays flowering and browns and wrinkles leaves.', '{"0": "Calcium shortage gives yellow and brown patches.", "1": "Sulphur shortage makes the plant lighter in colour.", "3": "Nitrogen shortage gives leaf loss and stunting."}'),
  ('BIO-1190', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'Which macronutrient stimulates the uptake of nitrates and is involved in water regulation?', '["Potassium", "Calcium", "Magnesium", "Sulphur"]', 0, 'Potassium regulates water and transport of reserve substances, enhances photosynthesis and stimulates nitrate uptake.', '{"1": "Calcium stabilizes the cell wall.", "2": "Magnesium is the core of chlorophyll.", "3": "Sulphur is linked to nitrogen metabolism."}'),
  ('BIO-1191', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'Which nutrient provides stability to the cell wall and aids seed development?', '["Potassium", "Copper", "Iron", "Calcium"]', 3, 'Calcium gives stability to and promotes development of the cell wall and aids seed development.', '{"0": "Potassium regulates water and nitrate uptake.", "1": "Copper helps lignin synthesis.", "2": "Iron is needed for chlorophyll synthesis."}'),
  ('BIO-1192', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'Loss of greenness in the oldest leaves and weak stalks indicate a deficiency of', '["sulphur", "zinc", "magnesium", "boron"]', 2, 'Magnesium is the core of chlorophyll; its deficiency causes weak stalks, loss of greenness in oldest leaves and yellow-brown spots.', '{"0": "Sulphur shortage makes the plant lighter in colour.", "1": "Zinc shortage causes stunted growth.", "3": "Boron''s deficiency is not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1193', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 178 (§8.1)', 'Interveinal chlorosis is a symptom of deficiency of', '["manganese", "iron", "chlorine", "calcium"]', 1, 'Iron is needed for chlorophyll synthesis and as an enzyme cofactor; its deficiency causes interveinal chlorosis.', '{"0": "Manganese deficiency causes early leaf fall and delayed maturity.", "2": "Chlorine deficiency affects health and growth.", "3": "Calcium deficiency gives yellow and brown patches."}'),
  ('BIO-1194', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 179 (§8.1)', 'Which micronutrient is needed for lignin synthesis?', '["Copper", "Zinc", "Molybdenum", "Iron"]', 0, 'Copper is necessary for lignin synthesis, which gives strength and rigidity to cell walls.', '{"1": "Zinc aids chlorophyll synthesis and roots.", "2": "Molybdenum is critical for nitrogen fixation.", "3": "Iron is for chlorophyll synthesis."}'),
  ('BIO-1195', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 179 (§8.1)', 'Which micronutrient is critical for nitrogen fixation?', '["Chlorine", "Copper", "Molybdenum", "Manganese"]', 2, 'Molybdenum is critical for nitrogen fixation and reduction, and sulfur and phosphorus metabolism.', '{"0": "Chlorine is involved in stomatal regulation.", "1": "Copper is for lignin synthesis.", "3": "Manganese is for photosynthesis and enzyme activation."}'),
  ('BIO-1196', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 179 (§8.1)', 'Premature leaf fall and delayed maturity suggest a deficiency of', '["copper", "manganese", "magnesium", "nitrogen"]', 1, 'Manganese deficiency results in premature falling of leaves and delayed maturity.', '{"0": "Copper deficiency causes chlorosis and twisted leaves.", "2": "Magnesium deficiency causes weak stalks and loss of green.", "3": "Nitrogen deficiency causes leaf loss and stunting."}'),
  ('BIO-1197', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 179 (§8.1)', 'Insectivorous plants capture insects mainly to obtain', '["carbon dioxide", "glucose for energy", "chlorophyll", "nitrogenous compounds"]', 3, 'All insectivorous plants are true autotrophs; the nitrogenous compounds in animal bodies benefit them.', '{"0": "They take CO2 from air.", "1": "They make glucose by photosynthesis.", "2": "They make their own chlorophyll."}'),
  ('BIO-1198', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 179 (§8.1)', 'In the pitcher plant, insects are prevented from crawling out by', '["stiff hairs inside the pitcher", "sticky dew drops", "two hinged lobes", "a sticky tentacle"]', 0, 'The pitcher has numerous stiff hairs that prevent insects that fall in from crawling out; a hood partly covers the opening.', '{"1": "Dew drops belong to sundew.", "2": "Hinged lobes belong to Venus flytrap.", "3": "Tentacles belong to sundew."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1199', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 179 (§8.1)', 'In the Venus flytrap, rapid closure of the lobes is triggered by', '["the pitcher hood", "dew on tentacles", "trichomes on the inner surfaces of the lobes", "roots touching the prey"]', 2, 'Hair-like trichomes on the inner surfaces of the lobes trigger rapid closing when prey touches them.', '{"0": "The hood belongs to the pitcher plant.", "1": "Dew-covered tentacles belong to sundew.", "3": "The trap is a leaf."}'),
  ('BIO-1200', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant nutrition', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 180 (§8.1)', 'Which insectivorous plant traps insects with shiny drops on tentacle-like hairs?', '["Pitcher plant", "Sundew", "Venus flytrap", "Cactus"]', 1, 'Sundew catches prey with shiny drops of ''dew'' at the tips of tentacle-like hairs.', '{"0": "The pitcher plant uses a water-filled sac.", "2": "Venus flytrap uses hinged lobes.", "3": "Cacti are not insectivorous."}'),
  ('BIO-1201', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 180 (§8.2)', 'Guard cells are', '["dead and lignified", "without chloroplasts", "star-shaped and colourless", "bean-shaped and contain chloroplasts"]', 3, 'Guard cells surrounding stomata are bean-shaped, contain chloroplasts and open or close the pore.', '{"0": "Guard cells are living.", "1": "They have chloroplasts.", "2": "They are bean-shaped."}'),
  ('BIO-1202', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 181 (§8.2)', 'The starch-sugar hypothesis of stomatal opening was proposed by', '["Ernst Münch in 1930", "Garner and Allard in 1920", "H. Von Mohl in 1856", "Engelmann in 1883"]', 2, 'German botanist H. von Mohl proposed in 1856 that sugar made in guard cells in daylight lowers their water potential and opens stomata.', '{"0": "Münch proposed pressure flow.", "1": "Garner and Allard studied photoperiodism.", "3": "Engelmann made the first action spectrum."}'),
  ('BIO-1203', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 181 (§8.2)', 'A weakness of the starch-sugar hypothesis is that it', '["does not explain the rapid turgor changes in guard cells", "requires light to close stomata", "involves potassium ions", "cannot explain opening at all"]', 0, 'The book says the starch-sugar mechanism does not fully explain the rapid turgor changes during stomatal movements.', '{"1": "It explains closure at night.", "2": "K+ influx is the other hypothesis.", "3": "It does give a mechanism for opening."}'),
  ('BIO-1204', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 181 (§8.2)', 'According to the K+ influx hypothesis, stomata open when', '["K+ diffuses out of guard cells", "K+ is actively transported into guard cells, so water follows by osmosis", "starch builds up in guard cells", "guard cells lose water"]', 1, 'Active K+ uptake lowers the guard cells'' osmotic potential; water enters, guard cells become turgid and the pore opens.', '{"0": "K+ leaving causes closure.", "2": "Starch formation lowers free sugar and favours closure.", "3": "Losing water makes guard cells flaccid."}'),
  ('BIO-1205', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 181 (§8.2)', 'Which light enhances K+ uptake by guard cells?', '["Green light", "Far-red light", "Infrared light", "Blue light"]', 3, 'Blue light acidifies the surroundings, promoting K+ uptake and water absorption by guard cells.', '{"0": "Green light is not named.", "1": "Far-red light acts on phytochrome.", "2": "Infrared is not named."}'),
  ('BIO-1206', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 181 (§8.2)', 'When leaf cells wilt at high temperature, stomata close because a hormone', '["increases sugar in guard cells", "stops active K+ transport into guard cells", "pumps K+ into guard cells", "softens guard cell walls"]', 1, 'Under wilting, a hormone (abscisic acid) is released that stops active K+ transport into guard cells, overriding light and CO2, so stomata close.', '{"0": "More sugar would favour opening.", "2": "Pumping K+ in opens stomata.", "3": "This is not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1207', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 181 (§8.2)', 'Palisade mesophyll cells are', '["loosely packed with large air spaces", "found in the lower epidermis", "elongated and tightly packed just beneath the upper epidermis", "dead cells with lignified walls"]', 2, 'Palisade tissue lies just beneath the upper epidermis, of elongated, tightly packed cells arranged to maximize light absorption.', '{"0": "Large air spaces are in the spongy mesophyll.", "1": "It is under the upper epidermis.", "3": "They are living photosynthetic cells."}'),
  ('BIO-1208', 'Plant physiology', 'Stomata', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 181 (§8.2)', 'After entering through stomata, CO2 reaches palisade cells by passing through', '["air spaces in the spongy mesophyll", "xylem vessels", "phloem sieve tubes", "the cuticle"]', 0, 'CO2 diffuses in through stomata, travels through air spaces of the spongy mesophyll and then into palisade cells.', '{"1": "Xylem carries water.", "2": "Phloem carries sugars.", "3": "The cuticle reduces exchange."}'),
  ('BIO-1209', 'Plant physiology', 'Support in plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 182 (§8.3)', 'The membrane surrounding the vacuole, which actively pumps ions into it, is the', '["plasmalemma", "middle lamella", "casparian strip", "tonoplast"]', 3, 'The tonoplast has active transport mechanisms that move ions into the vacuole, drawing in water and creating turgor.', '{"0": "Plasmalemma is the cell membrane.", "1": "The middle lamella joins cell walls.", "2": "The Casparian strip is in the endodermis."}'),
  ('BIO-1210', 'Plant physiology', 'Support in plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 182 (§8.3)', 'Soft tissues of herbaceous plants get mechanical support mainly from', '["lignified secondary walls", "turgor pressure in parenchyma cells", "cork cells", "annual rings"]', 1, 'Parenchyma cells absorb water, building turgor pressure that keeps cells rigid and supports herbaceous plants.', '{"0": "Lignified walls belong to sclerenchyma.", "2": "Cork protects woody stems.", "3": "Annual rings are in woody trees."}'),
  ('BIO-1211', 'Plant physiology', 'Support in plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 182 (§8.3)', 'Collenchyma cells differ from sclerenchyma cells in that collenchyma', '["lacks secondary walls and has primary walls thickened at the corners", "has thick lignified secondary walls", "is mostly dead at maturity", "is found only in seed coats"]', 0, 'Collenchyma lacks secondary walls; its primary walls are thickened at the corners with extra cellulose.', '{"1": "Thick lignified walls are typical of sclerenchyma.", "2": "Most sclerenchyma cells are non-living.", "3": "Sclereids are found in seed coats."}'),
  ('BIO-1212', 'Plant physiology', 'Support in plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 182 (§8.3)', 'Collenchyma is found', '["in the seed coats of nuts", "only in roots", "beneath the epidermis of young stems and leaf stalks", "in the bark of old trees"]', 2, 'Collenchyma occurs beneath the epidermis of young stems, leaf stalks and along leaf veins, supporting young parts.', '{"0": "Sclereids occur in seed coats and nut shells.", "1": "Not limited to roots.", "3": "It supports young parts without secondary growth."}'),
  ('BIO-1213', 'Plant physiology', 'Support in plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 182 (§8.3)', 'The walls of sclerenchyma cells are strengthened by', '["pectin only", "suberin in the primary wall only", "chitin", "lignin"]', 3, 'Sclerenchyma cells have thick secondary walls saturated with lignin, which gives strength and rigidity.', '{"0": "The book names lignin.", "1": "The book names lignin in secondary walls.", "2": "Chitin occurs in fungal walls."}'),
  ('BIO-1214', 'Plant physiology', 'Support in plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 183 (§8.3)', 'Sclereids are found in', '["the tips of roots", "seed coats and shells of nuts", "leaf stalks of young stems", "phloem sieve plates"]', 1, 'Sclereids are smaller than fibres and occur in seed coats and nut shells, where they protect.', '{"0": "Root tips have meristems.", "2": "Leaf stalks have collenchyma.", "3": "Sieve plates are in phloem."}'),
  ('BIO-1215', 'Plant physiology', 'Water potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 183 (§8.4)', 'The water potential of pure water at atmospheric pressure is', '["negative", "always positive", "minimum", "zero, the maximum value"]', 3, 'Pure water has the maximum water potential, defined as zero; all solutions have lower (negative) values.', '{"0": "Solutions, not pure water, have negative values.", "1": "It is defined as zero.", "2": "It is the maximum."}'),
  ('BIO-1216', 'Plant physiology', 'Water potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 183 (§8.4)', 'Water moves by osmosis', '["from lower water potential to higher water potential", "only from cell to soil", "from higher water potential to lower water potential", "only when pressure potential is zero"]', 2, 'Osmosis is movement of water from a region of higher to lower water potential through a partially permeable membrane.', '{"0": "This is the reverse.", "1": "Direction depends on water potential.", "3": "Not a requirement."}'),
  ('BIO-1217', 'Plant physiology', 'Water potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 184 (§8.4)', 'The water potential of a plant cell is given by', '["Ψw = Ψs + Ψp", "Ψw = Ψs − Ψp", "Ψw = Ψs × Ψp", "Ψw = Ψp − Ψs"]', 0, 'Total water potential is the sum of solute potential and pressure potential.', '{"1": "They are added.", "2": "They are added, not multiplied.", "3": "They are added."}'),
  ('BIO-1218', 'Plant physiology', 'Water potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 184 (§8.4)', 'Solute potential is', '["always negative, and more negative as more solute is present", "always positive", "zero in all cells", "increased by adding solute"]', 0, 'Solute potential is always negative; more solute molecules make it lower (more negative).', '{"1": "It is never positive.", "2": "It is zero only for pure water.", "3": "Adding solute lowers it."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1219', 'Plant physiology', 'Water potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 184 (§8.4)', 'When water enters a plant cell by osmosis and the cell becomes turgid, its', '["solute potential becomes positive", "water potential becomes more negative", "cell wall dissolves", "pressure potential increases"]', 3, 'Pressure built up inside a turgid cell increases its pressure potential.', '{"0": "Solute potential stays negative.", "1": "Water potential rises as water enters.", "2": "The wall resists the pressure."}'),
  ('BIO-1220', 'Plant physiology', 'Water transport', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 184 (§8.5)', 'Most uptake of water and minerals in roots takes place through', '["the root cap", "the endodermis", "root hairs", "lateral roots only"]', 2, 'Root hairs, extensions of epidermal cells, take up most water and minerals.', '{"0": "The root cap protects the tip.", "1": "The endodermis controls entry to xylem.", "3": "Root hairs are the main site."}'),
  ('BIO-1221', 'Plant physiology', 'Water transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 184 (§8.5)', 'In the apoplast pathway, water moves', '["through protoplasts connected by plasmodesmata", "through adjacent cell walls", "from vacuole to vacuole", "through sieve tubes"]', 1, 'The apoplast is a continuous system of adjacent cell walls; it becomes discontinuous at the endodermis because of Casparian strips.', '{"0": "That is the symplast pathway.", "2": "That is the vacuolar pathway.", "3": "Sieve tubes carry sugars."}'),
  ('BIO-1222', 'Plant physiology', 'Water transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 184 (§8.5)', 'The apoplast pathway becomes discontinuous at the endodermis because of', '["plasmodesmata", "Casparian strips", "root hairs", "the tonoplast"]', 1, 'Casparian segments in endodermal walls block the apoplast route there.', '{"0": "Plasmodesmata link protoplasts in the symplast.", "2": "Root hairs are at the surface.", "3": "The tonoplast surrounds the vacuole."}'),
  ('BIO-1223', 'Plant physiology', 'Water transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 184 (§8.5)', 'In the symplast pathway, water moves from cell to cell through', '["cell walls only", "Casparian strips", "bordered pits of vessels", "plasmodesmata"]', 3, 'Protoplasts of neighbouring cells are connected by plasmodesmata, cytoplasmic strands through pores in the walls.', '{"0": "Cell walls form the apoplast.", "1": "Casparian strips block the apoplast.", "2": "Bordered pits connect tracheids."}'),
  ('BIO-1224', 'Plant physiology', 'Water transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 185 (§8.5)', 'The vacuolar pathway for water movement in roots is', '["negligible", "the main pathway", "the only pathway at the endodermis", "used only by minerals"]', 0, 'Movement through the vacuolar pathway, crossing membranes, cytoplasm and vacuoles, is negligible.', '{"1": "The apoplast is the main route.", "2": "The symplast matters at the endodermis.", "3": "It carries water and minerals."}'),
  ('BIO-1225', 'Plant physiology', 'Xylem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 185 (§8.5)', 'Tracheids are linked to each other by', '["sieve plates", "plasmodesmata only", "bordered pits that allow lateral movement of water", "perforation plates at their ends"]', 2, 'Tracheids are long, thin cells with lignified walls and tapered ends, linked by bordered pits.', '{"0": "Sieve plates are in phloem.", "1": "Plasmodesmata link living cells.", "3": "Perforation plates are found in vessels."}'),
  ('BIO-1226', 'Plant physiology', 'Xylem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 185 (§8.5)', 'Compared with tracheids, xylem vessels are', '["longer and narrower", "shorter and broader", "living at maturity", "without lignin"]', 1, 'Vessels are shorter and broader than tracheids and form continuous channels.', '{"0": "This describes tracheids.", "2": "They are dead conducting cells.", "3": "They are lignified."}'),
  ('BIO-1227', 'Plant physiology', 'Xylem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 185 (§8.5)', 'Which component of xylem is living?', '["Xylem parenchyma", "Xylem fibres", "Tracheids", "Vessels"]', 0, 'Xylem parenchyma cells are living, thin-walled and store water and nutrients; they help lateral transport and repair.', '{"1": "Xylem fibres are dead at maturity.", "2": "Tracheids are dead conducting cells.", "3": "Vessels are dead conducting cells."}'),
  ('BIO-1228', 'Plant physiology', 'Xylem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 186 (§8.5)', 'In the TACT mechanism, the primary driving force for water transport in xylem is', '["root pressure", "adhesion", "active transport by xylem", "transpiration"]', 3, 'Evaporation through stomata creates negative pressure that pulls water upward; transpiration is the primary driving force.', '{"0": "Root pressure is not part of TACT.", "1": "Adhesion keeps the column continuous.", "2": "Xylem transport is passive."}'),
  ('BIO-1229', 'Plant physiology', 'Xylem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 186 (§8.5)', 'Cohesion in the TACT mechanism refers to', '["attraction between water and xylem walls", "the pull created by transpiration", "attraction between water molecules due to hydrogen bonding", "evaporation from leaves"]', 2, 'Cohesion is the attraction between water molecules themselves through hydrogen bonds, forming an unbroken column.', '{"0": "That is adhesion.", "1": "That is tension.", "3": "That is transpiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1230', 'Plant physiology', 'Xylem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 186 (§8.5)', 'The attraction between water molecules and the walls of xylem vessels is', '["cohesion", "tension", "osmosis", "adhesion"]', 3, 'Adhesion is attraction between water and xylem walls; it helps prevent breaks in the water column.', '{"0": "Cohesion is between water molecules.", "1": "Tension is the negative pressure.", "2": "Osmosis is movement across membranes."}'),
  ('BIO-1231', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 187 (§8.6)', 'In plants with secondary growth, phloem forms the', '["wood", "inner bark", "pith", "annual rings"]', 1, 'Phloem is found outside the xylem and constitutes the inner bark.', '{"0": "Wood is secondary xylem.", "2": "Pith is central.", "3": "Annual rings are in xylem."}'),
  ('BIO-1232', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 188 (§8.6)', 'Companion cells help sieve tube elements by supplying', '["lignin", "water only", "ATP and proteins", "chlorophyll"]', 2, 'Companion cells, linked by plasmodesmata, supply ATP and proteins to sieve tube elements.', '{"0": "Lignin is in xylem walls.", "1": "They supply ATP and proteins.", "3": "Not chlorophyll."}'),
  ('BIO-1233', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 188 (§8.6)', 'Phloem parenchyma stores', '["sugars, resins, latex and mucilage", "water and minerals only", "lignin", "starch only in roots"]', 0, 'Phloem parenchyma stores sugars, resins, latex and mucilage, which help defence and moisture retention.', '{"1": "Xylem parenchyma stores water and nutrients.", "2": "Lignin strengthens walls.", "3": "The book lists several substances."}'),
  ('BIO-1234', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 188 (§8.6)', 'Diffusion cannot explain sugar transport in phloem because', '["phloem cells are dead", "phloem moves sugar about 1 m per hour, while diffusion moves it about 1 m in eight years", "sugar cannot dissolve in water", "diffusion is faster than phloem flow"]', 1, 'Average sugar movement in phloem is 1 metre per hour, while diffusion covers 1 metre in eight years.', '{"0": "Sieve tube elements are living.", "2": "Sucrose is carried dissolved in water.", "3": "Diffusion is far slower."}'),
  ('BIO-1235', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 188 (§8.6)', 'The pressure-flow (mass-flow) theory was proposed by', '["H. von Mohl in 1856", "Garner and Allard in 1920", "Bragg in 1912", "Ernst Münch in 1930"]', 3, 'The pressure-flow theory, the most accepted model of phloem transport, was proposed by Ernst Münch in 1930.', '{"0": "Von Mohl proposed the starch-sugar hypothesis.", "1": "Garner and Allard studied photoperiodism.", "2": "The Braggs developed X-ray crystallography."}'),
  ('BIO-1236', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 188 (§8.6)', 'In the pressure-flow theory, sugar is transported in phloem in the form of', '["glucose", "starch", "sucrose", "fructose"]', 2, 'Excess glucose in mesophyll is converted into the non-reducing sugar sucrose, which is loaded into phloem.', '{"0": "Glucose is converted to sucrose first.", "1": "Starch is insoluble.", "3": "Sucrose is the form named."}'),
  ('BIO-1237', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 188 (§8.6)', 'According to the pressure-flow theory, water enters sieve tubes at the source because', '["sucrose loaded into sieve tubes lowers their water potential, so water moves in from xylem by osmosis", "xylem pumps water actively into phloem", "sugar is unloaded at the source", "transpiration pulls water into phloem"]', 0, 'Loading sucrose raises solute concentration in sieve tubes; water follows from nearby xylem by osmosis, building pressure at the source end.', '{"1": "Water moves passively by osmosis.", "2": "Sugar is unloaded at the sink.", "3": "Transpiration drives xylem flow."}'),
  ('BIO-1238', 'Plant physiology', 'Phloem', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 188 (§8.6)', 'In the pressure-flow theory, sucrose moves from mesophyll cells into companion cells by', '["simple diffusion", "osmosis", "transpiration pull", "active transport"]', 3, 'Sucrose is actively transported from mesophyll to companion cells, then diffuses into sieve tubes through plasmodesmata.', '{"0": "Diffusion carries it from companion cells into sieve tubes.", "1": "Osmosis moves water.", "2": "Transpiration pulls xylem water."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1239', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 189 (§8.7)', 'Intercalary meristems are found', '["at root and shoot tips", "between xylem and phloem", "at the bases of internodes, for example in grasses", "in the outer bark"]', 2, 'Intercalary meristems are separated from the apex by permanent tissue and lie at the bases of internodes, as in grasses; they are temporary.', '{"0": "Tips have apical meristems.", "1": "Vascular cambium lies between xylem and phloem.", "3": "Cork cambium is in the outer layer."}'),
  ('BIO-1240', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 189 (§8.7)', 'Growth in thickness of stems and roots is due to', '["apical meristems", "lateral meristems", "intercalary meristems", "root hairs"]', 1, 'Lateral meristems, the vascular and cork cambia, produce secondary growth (girth).', '{"0": "Apical meristems increase length.", "2": "Intercalary meristems help leaf and flower production.", "3": "Root hairs absorb water."}'),
  ('BIO-1241', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 190 (§8.7)', 'Cork cambium produces', '["cork cells that replace the epidermis", "secondary xylem", "secondary phloem", "root hairs"]', 0, 'Cork cambium forms in the outer layer of stems and roots and produces cork cells that form the protective bark.', '{"1": "Secondary xylem comes from vascular cambium.", "2": "Secondary phloem comes from vascular cambium.", "3": "Root hairs are epidermal."}'),
  ('BIO-1242', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 190 (§8.7)', 'Cells in the zone of cell division of a root tip are', '["large and vacuolated with peripheral nuclei", "fully differentiated", "dead and lignified", "small and non-vacuolated with central spherical nuclei"]', 3, 'In the zone of cell division cells are small and non-vacuolated with spherical central nuclei.', '{"0": "That describes the zone of elongation.", "1": "That is the zone of differentiation.", "2": "Meristem cells are living."}'),
  ('BIO-1243', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 190 (§8.7)', 'During cell elongation in primary growth, cells enlarge mainly by', '["dividing rapidly", "depositing lignin", "taking up water as wall plasticity increases", "losing their nuclei"]', 2, 'New cells increase in volume by uptake of water; wall plasticity rises and wall pressure falls.', '{"0": "Division happens in the zone of division.", "1": "Thickening happens during differentiation.", "3": "They keep nuclei."}'),
  ('BIO-1244', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 191 (§8.7)', 'Vascular cambium produces secondary xylem', '["on its outer side", "on its inner side", "only in leaves", "from cork cells"]', 1, 'Cells produced on the inner margin form secondary xylem; those on the outer margin form secondary phloem.', '{"0": "The outer side gives secondary phloem.", "2": "It acts in stems and roots.", "3": "Cork cambium gives cork."}'),
  ('BIO-1245', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 191 (§8.7)', 'The bark of a mature woody stem consists of', '["all tissues outside the vascular cambium: secondary phloem, cork cambium and cork", "only cork", "secondary xylem and pith", "the epidermis only"]', 0, 'Bark is the region outside the vascular cambium, including secondary phloem, cork cambium and cork.', '{"1": "Cork is only part of the bark.", "2": "These are inside the cambium.", "3": "The epidermis is replaced by cork."}'),
  ('BIO-1246', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 192 (§8.7)', 'Spring wood is lighter in colour than autumn wood because in spring the cambium', '["is inactive", "forms narrow, dense xylem", "is highly active and forms xylem with wider vessels", "forms only phloem"]', 2, 'In spring the cambium is very active, producing much xylem with wide vessels; in autumn it makes fewer, narrower, denser elements.', '{"0": "The cambium is most active in spring.", "1": "That forms darker autumn wood.", "3": "Xylem forms the rings."}'),
  ('BIO-1247', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 192 (§8.7)', 'The scientific dating of tree rings to study past climate is called', '["dendrology", "dendrochronology", "palynology", "phenology"]', 1, 'Dendrochronology dates and studies tree rings to analyse past climates and events.', '{"0": "Not the term used in the book.", "2": "Palynology studies pollen.", "3": "Phenology studies seasonal timing."}'),
  ('BIO-1248', 'Plant physiology', 'Growth', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 192 (§8.7)', 'In an annual ring, the boundary that is abrupt is the change from', '["spring wood to autumn wood", "secondary phloem to cork", "pith to primary xylem", "autumn wood to the next year''s spring wood"]', 3, 'The transition from spring to autumn wood is gradual, but the shift from autumn wood back to spring wood is abrupt.', '{"0": "This change is gradual.", "1": "This is not a ring boundary.", "2": "This is not a ring boundary."}'),
  ('BIO-1249', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 193 (§8.7)', 'Auxins are chemically', '["indole acetic acid or its variants", "gibberellic acid", "a gas", "purine derivatives"]', 0, 'Auxins are indole acetic acid (IAA) or its variants.', '{"1": "Gibberellins contain gibberellic acid.", "2": "Ethylene is a gas.", "3": "Not given for auxins."}'),
  ('BIO-1250', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 193 (§8.7)', 'Which hormone promotes apical dominance and inhibits abscission?', '["Auxin", "Abscisic acid", "Ethylene", "Cytokinin"]', 0, 'Auxins promote apical dominance and fruit growth, delay senescence in some species and inhibit abscission.', '{"1": "ABA promotes abscission.", "2": "Ethylene promotes ripening.", "3": "Cytokinins promote lateral bud growth."}'),
  ('BIO-1251', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 193 (§8.7)', 'Bolting of rosette plants is promoted by', '["abscisic acid", "cytokinins", "gibberellins", "ethylene"]', 2, 'Gibberellins promote bolting of some rosette plants and break bud and seed dormancy.', '{"0": "ABA promotes dormancy.", "1": "Cytokinins promote cell division.", "3": "Ethylene promotes ripening."}'),
  ('BIO-1252', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 193 (§8.7)', 'Which hormone may substitute for red light and promotes flowering in long-day plants?', '["Abscisic acid", "Auxin", "Ethylene", "Gibberellin"]', 3, 'Gibberellins can sometimes substitute for red light, promoting flowering in long-day plants and inhibiting it in short-day plants.', '{"0": "ABA has the opposite effect.", "1": "Not listed for auxin.", "2": "Ethylene promotes flowering in pineapple."}'),
  ('BIO-1253', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 193 (§8.7)', 'Cytokinins are named for their role in', '["closing stomata", "promoting cytokinesis during cell division", "fruit ripening", "inhibiting cell division"]', 1, 'Cytokinins promote cytokinesis and increase DNA replication and RNA and protein synthesis.', '{"0": "ABA closes stomata.", "2": "Ethylene ripens fruit.", "3": "They promote division."}'),
  ('BIO-1254', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 193 (§8.7)', 'Which hormone promotes lateral bud growth and stomatal opening?', '["Auxin", "Abscisic acid", "Ethylene", "Cytokinin"]', 3, 'Cytokinins promote lateral bud growth, break bud dormancy, delay senescence and promote stomatal opening.', '{"0": "Auxin promotes apical dominance.", "1": "ABA closes stomata.", "2": "Ethylene is not linked to this."}'),
  ('BIO-1255', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 194 (§8.7)', 'Which hormone promotes bud and seed dormancy?', '["Abscisic acid", "Gibberellin", "Cytokinin", "Auxin"]', 0, 'ABA promotes bud and seed dormancy, abscission and stomatal closure under water stress.', '{"1": "Gibberellins break dormancy.", "2": "Cytokinins break bud dormancy.", "3": "Not described for auxin."}'),
  ('BIO-1256', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant growth regulators', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 194 (§8.7)', 'Ethylene is known to', '["promote stem growth during stress", "promote fruit ripening and flowering in pineapple", "promote seed dormancy", "promote root growth"]', 1, 'Ethylene inhibits stem and root growth, breaks bud dormancy, promotes flowering in pineapple and fruit ripening.', '{"0": "It inhibits stem growth under stress.", "2": "ABA promotes seed dormancy.", "3": "It inhibits root growth."}'),
  ('BIO-1257', 'Plant physiology', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 194 (§8.8)', 'A plant cell placed in a hypertonic solution', '["gains water and swells", "shows no net water movement", "loses water and undergoes plasmolysis", "bursts"]', 2, 'In a hypertonic solution water moves out and the cell shrinks, a condition called plasmolysis.', '{"0": "That happens in a hypotonic solution.", "1": "That is an isotonic solution.", "3": "The wall prevents bursting; this is not hypertonic."}'),
  ('BIO-1258', 'Plant physiology', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 194 (§8.8)', 'Many marine organisms need no osmoregulation because', '["their cells have the same osmotic pressure as sea water", "sea water is hypotonic", "they have no cell membranes", "they drink fresh water"]', 0, 'Their cells are isotonic with sea water, so there is no net osmotic water movement.', '{"1": "Sea water is not hypotonic to them.", "2": "They have membranes.", "3": "Not described."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1259', 'Plant physiology', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 195 (§8.8)', 'Marine hydrophytes retain water in a hypertonic environment by', '["having thin or absent cuticles", "expelling water through hydathodes", "absorbing more water through leaves", "synthesizing organic solutes such as proline and glycine betaine"]', 3, 'Marine hydrophytes excrete salts through salt glands and make organic solutes like proline, glycine betaine and sugars; they have thick cuticles.', '{"0": "Thin cuticles are freshwater traits.", "1": "Hydathodes remove excess water in freshwater plants.", "2": "Water tends to leave in hypertonic water."}'),
  ('BIO-1260', 'Plant physiology', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 195 (§8.8)', 'Freshwater hydrophytes avoid overhydration by', '["synthesizing proline", "expelling excess water through hydathodes", "developing thick cuticles", "storing water in succulent stems"]', 1, 'In hypotonic water, freshwater plants expel excess water through hydathodes or vacuoles and actively absorb K+ and Ca2+.', '{"0": "Proline is used by marine hydrophytes.", "2": "They have thin or absent cuticles.", "3": "Water storage is a xerophyte trait."}'),
  ('BIO-1261', 'Plant physiology', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 196 (§8.8)', 'Plants that store water in fleshy stems are called', '["mesophytes", "hydrophytes", "succulents", "epiphytes"]', 2, 'Xerophytes that store water in fleshy stems are known as succulents.', '{"0": "Mesophytes need no special adaptations.", "1": "Hydrophytes live in water.", "3": "Not discussed."}'),
  ('BIO-1262', 'Plant physiology', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 196 (§8.8)', 'Which is an example of a mesophyte?', '["Blue agave", "Water lily", "Rose", "Sea lavender"]', 2, 'Rose, tomato and daisy are mesophytes living in moderate environments.', '{"0": "Blue agave is a xerophyte.", "1": "Water lily is a hydrophyte.", "3": "Sea lavender is a halophyte."}'),
  ('BIO-1263', 'Plant physiology', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 196 (§8.8)', 'Halophytes are plants that', '["grow in fresh water", "grow in soil with high concentrations of salts such as NaCl", "grow in moderate habitats", "trap insects"]', 1, 'Halophytes inhabit saline soil with salts like NaCl, MgCl2 or MgSO4; examples are sea arrowgrass and sea lavender.', '{"0": "Freshwater plants are hydrophytes.", "2": "Those are mesophytes.", "3": "Those are insectivorous plants."}'),
  ('BIO-1264', 'Plant physiology', 'Thermoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 197 (§8.9)', 'Plants under heat stress prevent denaturation of their enzymes by making', '["unsaturated fatty acids", "abscisic acid", "phytochrome", "heat-shock proteins"]', 3, 'Cells of heat-stressed plants synthesize heat-shock proteins that embrace enzymes and other proteins and prevent denaturation.', '{"0": "Unsaturated fatty acids are a cold response.", "1": "ABA closes stomata.", "2": "Phytochrome detects light."}'),
  ('BIO-1265', 'Plant physiology', 'Thermoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 197 (§8.9)', 'Plants respond to cold stress by increasing the proportion of', '["unsaturated fatty acids in membranes", "saturated fatty acids in membranes", "heat-shock proteins", "lignin in walls"]', 0, 'More unsaturated fatty acids help membranes keep their structure at low temperature by preventing crystal formation.', '{"1": "Saturated lipids crystallize more readily.", "2": "Heat-shock proteins are a heat response.", "3": "Not the response described."}'),
  ('BIO-1266', 'Plant physiology', 'Thermoregulation', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 197 (§8.9)', 'Rapid chilling is more harmful to plants than a gradual drop in temperature because', '["heat-shock proteins work only slowly", "ice never forms in cell walls", "stomata cannot close quickly", "changing membrane lipid composition takes time"]', 3, 'Adding unsaturated fatty acids to membranes requires time, so rapid chilling is more stressful than gradual cooling.', '{"0": "Heat-shock proteins are a heat response.", "1": "Ice may form in walls.", "2": "Not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-1267', 'Plant physiology', 'Thermoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 197 (§8.9)', 'Freezing kills plant cells mainly when ice crystals', '["form only around cell walls", "form within the protoplasm and pierce membranes", "form in the soil", "form on the leaf surface"]', 1, 'Ice confined around cell walls is less harmful; ice inside protoplasm perforates membranes and organelles, killing cells.', '{"0": "Wall ice is tolerated.", "2": "Soil ice is not the cause.", "3": "Not the cause described."}'),
  ('BIO-1268', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant movements', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 198 (§8.10)', 'Roots show', '["negative geotropism and positive phototropism", "positive geotropism and positive phototropism", "positive geotropism and negative phototropism", "no tropisms"]', 2, 'Roots grow toward gravity (positive geotropism) and away from light (negative phototropism); shoots do the opposite.', '{"0": "This describes shoots.", "1": "Roots show negative phototropism.", "3": "Roots show tropisms."}'),
  ('BIO-1269', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant movements', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 198 (§8.10)', 'Tendrils of a climbing vine coiling around a support is an example of', '["thigmotropism", "chemotropism", "geotropism", "phototropism"]', 0, 'Thigmotropism is growth in response to touch; growth increases on the side opposite the contact, coiling the tendril.', '{"1": "Chemotropism responds to chemicals.", "2": "Geotropism responds to gravity.", "3": "Phototropism responds to light."}'),
  ('BIO-1270', 'Plant physiology', 'Plant movements', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 198 (§8.10)', 'The growth of fungal hyphae towards certain chemicals is', '["thigmotropism", "chemotropism", "phototropism", "photoperiodism"]', 1, 'Movement in response to chemicals is chemotropism; fungal hyphae are chemotropic.', '{"0": "Thigmotropism is to touch.", "2": "Phototropism is to light.", "3": "Photoperiodism is a response to day length."}'),
  ('BIO-1271', 'Plant physiology', 'Photoperiodism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 198 (§8.11)', 'Photoperiodism was first studied in 1920 by Garner and Allard using', '["Spirogyra", "pineapple", "tobacco", "henbane"]', 2, 'Garner and Allard found that tobacco flowers only after exposure to a series of short days.', '{"0": "Spirogyra was used by Engelmann.", "1": "Pineapple is induced to flower by ethylene.", "3": "Henbane is a long-day example."}'),
  ('BIO-1272', 'Plant physiology', 'Photoperiodism', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 198 (§8.11)', 'Short-day plants are better described as long-night plants because', '["interrupting their long night with a short light period prevents flowering", "they flower only in total darkness", "they do not respond to light", "day length alone controls their flowering"]', 0, 'Later studies showed the length of the dark period is critical; a light break in the long night prevents flowering in short-day plants.', '{"1": "They need light too.", "2": "They respond to light breaks.", "3": "The dark period is what matters."}'),
  ('BIO-1273', 'Plant physiology', 'Photoperiodism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 198 (§8.11)', 'The inactive form of phytochrome absorbs red light at', '["730 nm and is converted to the inactive form", "430 nm and is destroyed", "680 nm and splits water", "660 nm and is converted to the active form"]', 3, 'P660 absorbs red light at 660 nm and is converted to active P730, which absorbs far-red at 730 nm and reverts to P660.', '{"0": "730 nm far-red is absorbed by the active form.", "1": "430 nm is a chlorophyll peak.", "2": "P680 is the PS-II reaction centre."}'),
  ('BIO-1274', 'Plant physiology', 'Photoperiodism', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 199 (§8.11)', 'Red light given during the night generally', '["inhibits flowering in short-day plants but promotes it in long-day plants", "promotes flowering in short-day plants", "inhibits flowering in long-day plants", "has no effect on flowering"]', 0, 'Red light inhibits flowering in short-day plants but promotes it in long-day plants.', '{"1": "It inhibits short-day plants.", "2": "It promotes long-day plants.", "3": "It has a clear effect."}'),
  ('BIO-1275', 'Plant physiology', 'Photoperiodism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 199 (§8.11)', 'Florigen, the flowering hormone, is made in', '["roots and travels through xylem", "floral buds only", "the shoot apex of seeds", "leaves and travels through phloem to the buds"]', 3, 'Once stimulated, the biological clock causes leaves to produce florigen, which moves through phloem to floral buds.', '{"0": "It is made in leaves.", "1": "It travels to the buds.", "2": "The shoot apex receives the vernalisation stimulus."}'),
  ('BIO-1276', 'Plant physiology', 'Photoperiodism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 199 (§8.11)', 'Which of the following is a day-neutral plant?', '["Cocklebur", "Cucumber", "Henbane", "Chrysanthemum"]', 1, 'Cucumber, tomato, garden pea, maize and cotton flower independently of photoperiod.', '{"0": "Cocklebur is a short-day plant.", "2": "Henbane is a long-day plant.", "3": "Chrysanthemum is a short-day plant."}'),
  ('BIO-1277', 'Plant physiology', 'Photoperiodism', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 199 (§8.11)', 'Henbane (Hyoscyamus niger) is a', '["short-day plant", "day-neutral plant", "long-day plant", "plant that needs no light"]', 2, 'Henbane flowers when dark periods are shorter than its critical length (13 h), so it is a long-day plant.', '{"0": "Short-day plants include cocklebur and tobacco.", "1": "Day-neutral plants include cucumber.", "3": "All need light."}'),
  ('BIO-1278', 'Plant physiology', 'Vernalisation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 199 (§8.12)', 'The low-temperature stimulus in vernalisation is received by the', '["leaves", "roots", "shoot apex of a mature stem or the embryo of a seed", "flowers"]', 2, 'Unlike photoperiodism, where leaves receive the stimulus, the shoot apex or seed embryo receives the cold stimulus.', '{"0": "Leaves receive the photoperiodic stimulus.", "1": "Not the site described.", "3": "Flowers form later."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1279', 'Plant physiology', 'Vernalisation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 199 (§8.12)', 'A temperature that is very effective for vernalisation is about', '["-20 °C", "4 °C", "28 °C", "40 °C"]', 1, 'Temperatures around 4 °C are effective; the chilling period required ranges from four days to three months.', '{"0": "Freezing temperatures form ice crystals.", "2": "28 °C relates to photorespiration.", "3": "40 °C is a heat-stress temperature."}'),
  ('BIO-1280', 'Plant physiology', 'Vernalisation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Plant Physiology, p. 199 (§8.12)', 'Photoperiodism and vernalisation help cross-pollination because they', '["make members of the same species flower at the same time", "stop flowering entirely", "make plants self-pollinating", "make every plant flower at a different time"]', 0, 'They synchronise reproduction with the environment and make members of a species flower together, encouraging cross-pollination.', '{"1": "They control, not stop, flowering.", "2": "They encourage cross-pollination.", "3": "They synchronise flowering."}'),
  ('BIO-1281', 'Human physiology', 'Digestive tract', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 202 (§9.1)', 'Which of the following is an accessory digestive organ rather than part of the GI tract?', '["Pharynx", "Pancreas", "Oesophagus", "Duodenum"]', 1, 'Accessory organs are the salivary glands, liver, gallbladder and pancreas; the GI tract runs from mouth to anus.', '{"0": "The pharynx is part of the GI tract.", "2": "The oesophagus is part of the GI tract.", "3": "The duodenum is part of the small intestine."}'),
  ('BIO-1282', 'Human physiology', 'Oral cavity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 203 (§9.1)', 'Lips help in phonation and retain food during chewing. They are made of', '["highly vascularized skeletal muscle with many sensory nerve endings", "smooth muscle with few nerves", "cartilage covered by mucosa", "cardiac muscle"]', 0, 'Lips are made of highly vascularized skeletal muscle tissue with many sensory nerve endings.', '{"1": "The book says skeletal muscle.", "2": "Lips are not cartilage.", "3": "Cardiac muscle is only in the heart."}'),
  ('BIO-1283', 'Human physiology', 'Oral cavity', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'Breaking food physically into smaller pieces by the teeth is called', '["peristalsis", "emulsification", "segmentation", "mastication"]', 3, 'Mechanical digestion in the mouth is chewing, or mastication, which increases surface area for enzymes.', '{"0": "Peristalsis moves food along the tract.", "1": "Emulsification by bile breaks fats into droplets.", "2": "Segmentation mixes food in the intestines."}'),
  ('BIO-1284', 'Human physiology', 'Oral cavity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'The parotid salivary glands are located', '["below the tongue", "behind the jaws", "in front of the ears", "in the pharynx wall"]', 2, 'Sublingual glands are below the tongue, submaxillary glands behind the jaws and parotid glands in front of the ears.', '{"0": "Those are the sublingual glands.", "1": "Those are the submaxillary glands.", "3": "No major salivary gland is there."}'),
  ('BIO-1285', 'Human physiology', 'Oral cavity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'Which component of saliva kills microorganisms?', '["Thiocyanate ions", "Bicarbonate ions", "Mucus", "Salivary amylase"]', 0, 'Saliva contains thiocyanate ions that kill microorganisms, while bicarbonate buffers the mouth.', '{"1": "Bicarbonate acts as a buffer.", "2": "Mucus moistens and lubricates food.", "3": "Amylase digests starch."}'),
  ('BIO-1286', 'Human physiology', 'Oral cavity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'Fresh saliva has a pH of about 8 but soon falls to about 6 because it', '["gains hydrochloric acid", "loses bicarbonate to the blood", "loses carbon dioxide", "mixes with bile"]', 2, 'Fresh saliva is alkaline (pH 8) but quickly loses CO2 and reaches pH 6.', '{"0": "HCl is secreted in the stomach.", "1": "Not the reason given.", "3": "Bile enters the duodenum."}'),
  ('BIO-1287', 'Human physiology', 'Oral cavity', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'Salivary amylase partially digests starch and glycogen into', '["glucose and fructose", "maltose", "amino acids", "glycerol"]', 1, 'Salivary amylase partially digests polysaccharides into the disaccharide maltose.', '{"0": "Sucrose digestion gives glucose and fructose.", "2": "Amino acids come from proteins.", "3": "Glycerol comes from fats."}'),
  ('BIO-1288', 'Human physiology', 'Oral cavity', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'The small, moist mass of food formed in the mouth is the', '["chyme", "chyle", "faeces", "bolus"]', 3, 'After mechanical and chemical digestion in the mouth, food forms a small moist mass called a bolus.', '{"0": "Chyme forms in the stomach.", "1": "Chyle forms in the small intestine.", "2": "Faeces form in the colon."}'),
  ('BIO-1289', 'Human physiology', 'Swallowing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'During swallowing, the passage between the nasal cavity and pharynx is closed by', '["lowering the epiglottis", "raising the soft palate against the back wall of the pharynx", "closing the cardiac sphincter", "raising the tongue tip only"]', 1, 'The soft palate is raised against the back of the pharynx, closing the nasal passage; the epiglottis covers the trachea.', '{"0": "The epiglottis covers the trachea.", "2": "The cardiac sphincter is at the stomach.", "3": "The tongue pushes the bolus back."}'),
  ('BIO-1290', 'Human physiology', 'Swallowing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'Which statement about swallowing is correct?', '["It is completely involuntary", "It is voluntary throughout", "It starts voluntarily but becomes automatic once food reaches the back of the mouth", "It is controlled by gastrin"]', 2, 'The start of swallowing is voluntary, but once food reaches the back of the mouth it becomes automatic.', '{"0": "The start is voluntary.", "1": "It becomes automatic.", "3": "Gastrin regulates gastric juice."}'),
  ('BIO-1291', 'Human physiology', 'Swallowing', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 204 (§9.1)', 'The pharynx is', '["the common passage for the digestive and respiratory tracts", "a part of the stomach", "an accessory gland", "the site of fat absorption"]', 0, 'The pharynx is a cavity behind the mouth shared by the digestive and respiratory tracts.', '{"1": "It lies before the oesophagus.", "2": "It is part of the GI tract.", "3": "Fats are absorbed in the small intestine."}'),
  ('BIO-1292', 'Human physiology', 'Oesophagus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 205 (§9.1)', 'In an adult human, the oesophagus is about', '["6 m long", "2.4 m long", "20–25 cm wide", "25 cm long"]', 3, 'The adult oesophagus is about 25 cm long and connects the pharynx to the stomach.', '{"0": "6 m is the small intestine.", "1": "2.4 m is the jejunum.", "2": "This confuses length with width."}'),
  ('BIO-1293', 'Human physiology', 'Oesophagus', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 205 (§9.1)', 'The cardiac (lower oesophageal) sphincter', '["releases chyme into the duodenum", "lets food enter the stomach and prevents backflow of stomach contents", "closes the trachea", "controls defecation"]', 1, 'The cardiac sphincter opens in response to food pressure and prevents stomach contents flowing back into the oesophagus.', '{"0": "The pyloric sphincter releases chyme.", "2": "The epiglottis closes the trachea.", "3": "Anal sphincters control defecation."}'),
  ('BIO-1294', 'Human physiology', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 205 (§9.1)', 'Peristalsis is', '["back-and-forth mixing in one place", "reverse movement of food causing vomiting", "voluntary contraction of skeletal muscle", "rhythmic waves of smooth-muscle contraction that push food along the tract"]', 3, 'Peristalsis is the rhythmic sequence of contraction waves in smooth muscles of the gut wall that squeeze food along.', '{"0": "That is segmentation.", "1": "That is antiperistalsis.", "2": "Gut walls have smooth muscle."}'),
  ('BIO-1295', 'Human physiology', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 206 (§9.1)', 'Hunger pangs are caused by', '["peristaltic contractions triggered by low blood glucose", "antiperistalsis", "secretion of bile", "gastrin release"]', 0, 'Hunger contractions are peristaltic movements triggered by low blood glucose, giving hunger pangs.', '{"1": "Antiperistalsis causes vomiting.", "2": "Bile is unrelated.", "3": "Gastrin stimulates gastric juice."}'),
  ('BIO-1296', 'Human physiology', 'Motility', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 206 (§9.1)', 'Vomiting results from', '["segmentation", "hunger contractions", "antiperistalsis", "the defecation reflex"]', 2, 'When peristaltic movements reverse (antiperistalsis), food is pushed back towards the stomach and mouth, causing vomiting.', '{"0": "Segmentation mixes food.", "1": "Hunger contractions cause hunger pangs.", "3": "The defecation reflex empties the rectum."}'),
  ('BIO-1297', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 206 (§9.1)', 'The stomach is located', '["on the left side of the abdominal cavity just below the diaphragm", "on the right side below the liver", "in the thoracic cavity", "behind the rectum"]', 0, 'The U-shaped stomach lies on the left side of the abdominal cavity, right below the diaphragm.', '{"1": "The book places it on the left.", "2": "It is below the diaphragm.", "3": "Not its position."}'),
  ('BIO-1298', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 206 (§9.1)', 'The muscularis externa of the stomach consists of', '["only circular muscles", "skeletal muscle fibres", "connective tissue with blood vessels", "outer longitudinal, middle circular and inner oblique muscles"]', 3, 'The stomach''s muscle layer has outer longitudinal, middle circular and inner oblique muscles.', '{"0": "There are three muscle layers.", "1": "The stomach wall has smooth muscle.", "2": "That describes the submucosa."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1299', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 206 (§9.1)', 'The stomach layer of connective tissue containing blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves is the', '["mucosa", "serosa", "submucosa", "muscularis externa"]', 2, 'The submucosa is connective tissue carrying blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves.', '{"0": "The mucosa contains glands.", "1": "The serosa is the outer protective layer.", "3": "The muscularis is muscle."}'),
  ('BIO-1300', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 206 (§9.1)', 'Besides churning food, contractions of the stomach wall', '["digest proteins", "produce enough heat to melt solid fats", "absorb glucose", "release bile"]', 1, 'Vigorous stomach contractions churn and mix food and generate heat that melts solid fats.', '{"0": "Protein digestion is chemical, by pepsin.", "2": "Glucose is absorbed in the small intestine.", "3": "Bile comes from the gallbladder."}'),
  ('BIO-1301', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 207 (§9.1)', 'The parietal (oxyntic) cells of gastric glands secrete', '["hydrochloric acid", "pepsinogen", "mucus", "gastrin"]', 0, 'Parietal cells secrete HCl, which brings the stomach pH to about 2–3.', '{"1": "Chief cells secrete pepsinogen.", "2": "Mucous cells secrete mucus.", "3": "Gastrin comes from endocrine cells."}'),
  ('BIO-1302', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 207 (§9.1)', 'Pepsinogen is secreted by', '["parietal cells", "mucous cells", "chief cells", "goblet cells of the colon"]', 2, 'Chief cells of the gastric glands secrete the enzyme precursor pepsinogen.', '{"0": "Parietal cells secrete HCl.", "1": "Mucous cells secrete mucus.", "3": "Not the source."}'),
  ('BIO-1303', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 207 (§9.1)', 'Hydrochloric acid brings the pH of stomach contents to about', '["6", "2–3", "8", "7.4"]', 1, 'HCl adjusts the pH of stomach contents to about 2–3.', '{"0": "pH 6 is saliva after losing CO2.", "2": "pH 8 is fresh saliva or pancreatic juice.", "3": "Not the stomach value."}'),
  ('BIO-1304', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 207 (§9.1)', 'Pepsin digests proteins into', '["amino acids only", "glycerol and fatty acids", "nucleotides", "polypeptides and peptides"]', 3, 'Pepsin breaks proteins down into polypeptides and peptides.', '{"0": "Amino acids are released later by other enzymes.", "1": "These come from fats.", "2": "These come from nucleic acids."}'),
  ('BIO-1305', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 207 (§9.1)', 'The semi-liquid acidic mass in the stomach three to four hours after a meal is', '["bolus", "chyme", "chyle", "bile"]', 1, 'After 3–4 hours of mixing, stomach contents form semi-liquid acidic chyme.', '{"0": "The bolus forms in the mouth.", "2": "Chyle is the alkaline emulsion of the small intestine.", "3": "Bile comes from the liver."}'),
  ('BIO-1306', 'Human physiology', 'Stomach', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 207 (§9.1)', 'The release of chyme into the small intestine is regulated by the', '["cardiac sphincter", "internal anal sphincter", "pyloric sphincter", "epiglottis"]', 2, 'The pyloric sphincter regulates release of chyme into the small intestine.', '{"0": "The cardiac sphincter is between oesophagus and stomach.", "1": "This is at the anus.", "3": "The epiglottis covers the trachea."}'),
  ('BIO-1307', 'Human physiology', 'Gastric regulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 207 (§9.1)', 'The sight, smell or thought of food causes a small secretion of gastric juice through signals from the', '["medulla of the brain", "pancreas", "duodenal walls", "liver"]', 0, 'In response to the smell, sight or thought of food, the medulla sends messages to gastric glands to secrete some gastric juice.', '{"1": "The pancreas is not involved here.", "2": "Duodenal hormones act on pancreas and gallbladder.", "3": "Not involved."}'),
  ('BIO-1308', 'Human physiology', 'Gastric regulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'Gastrin is released by endocrine cells of the stomach mucosa in response to', '["fats in the duodenum", "acid chyme in the duodenum", "low blood glucose", "proteins in food"]', 3, 'Proteins in food stimulate stomach endocrine cells to release gastrin, which travels in blood to gastric glands.', '{"0": "Fats in chyme stimulate CCK.", "1": "Acid in the duodenum stimulates secretin.", "2": "Low glucose triggers hunger contractions."}'),
  ('BIO-1309', 'Human physiology', 'Gastric regulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'Which hormone stops the release of hydrochloric acid when food moves into the small intestine?', '["Gastrin", "Secretin", "Somatostatin", "Insulin"]', 2, 'As food moves from stomach to small intestine, somatostatin stops the release of HCl.', '{"0": "Gastrin stimulates gastric juice.", "1": "Secretin stimulates pancreas and gallbladder.", "3": "Insulin regulates blood glucose."}'),
  ('BIO-1310', 'Human physiology', 'Small intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'In an adult, the small intestine is about', '["1 m long and 6 cm in diameter", "6 m long and 2–3 cm in diameter", "25 cm long", "3.5 m long in total"]', 1, 'The adult small intestine is about 6 m long and 2–3 cm in diameter.', '{"0": "The large intestine is about the last metre.", "2": "25 cm is the oesophagus.", "3": "3.5 m is the ileum alone."}'),
  ('BIO-1311', 'Human physiology', 'Small intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'The duodenum is about', '["20–25 cm long", "2.4 m long", "3.5 m long", "6 m long"]', 0, 'The first 20–25 cm of the small intestine is the duodenum.', '{"1": "2.4 m is the jejunum.", "2": "3.5 m is the ileum.", "3": "6 m is the whole small intestine."}'),
  ('BIO-1312', 'Human physiology', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'Pancreatic juice neutralizes the acidity of chyme because it contains', '["bile salts", "pepsin", "hydrochloric acid", "bicarbonate"]', 3, 'Pancreatic juice is slightly alkaline (pH 8) due to bicarbonate, which neutralizes acidic chyme.', '{"0": "Bile salts emulsify fats.", "1": "Pepsin is a stomach enzyme.", "2": "HCl is acidic."}'),
  ('BIO-1313', 'Human physiology', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'Trypsinogen is activated to trypsin by', '["hydrochloric acid", "enterokinase from the duodenal wall", "bile salts", "pepsin"]', 1, 'Enterokinase secreted by the duodenal wall activates trypsinogen into trypsin, which digests proteins into polypeptides.', '{"0": "HCl activates pepsinogen.", "2": "Bile salts emulsify fats.", "3": "Pepsin digests proteins in the stomach."}'),
  ('BIO-1314', 'Human physiology', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'Which pancreatic enzymes digest DNA and RNA into nucleotides?', '["Carboxypeptidases", "Lipases", "Nucleases", "Amylases"]', 2, 'Pancreatic nucleases digest DNA and RNA into nucleotides.', '{"0": "Carboxypeptidase digests peptides.", "1": "Lipase digests fats.", "3": "Amylase digests polysaccharides."}'),
  ('BIO-1315', 'Human physiology', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'Bile helps fat digestion because its salts', '["hydrolyse fats into fatty acids", "activate trypsinogen", "neutralize HCl in the stomach", "emulsify fats into small droplets, increasing surface area for lipase"]', 3, 'Bile salts emulsify fats into small droplets, giving a large surface area for lipid-digesting enzymes; bile has no digestive enzymes.', '{"0": "Lipase hydrolyses fats.", "1": "Enterokinase activates trypsinogen.", "2": "Bile acts in the duodenum."}'),
  ('BIO-1316', 'Human physiology', 'Small intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 209 (§9.1)', 'Which enzyme of intestinal juice digests dipeptides into amino acids?', '["Erepsin", "Aminopeptidase", "Trypsin", "Pepsin"]', 0, 'Aminopeptidase digests polypeptides to dipeptides and erepsin digests dipeptides into amino acids.', '{"1": "Aminopeptidase gives dipeptides.", "2": "Trypsin is pancreatic and gives polypeptides.", "3": "Pepsin works in the stomach."}'),
  ('BIO-1317', 'Human physiology', 'Small intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 209 (§9.1)', 'Sucrase digests sucrose into', '["glucose and galactose", "glucose and fructose", "two glucose molecules", "maltose"]', 1, 'Intestinal sucrase digests sucrose into glucose and fructose.', '{"0": "Lactase gives glucose and galactose.", "2": "Maltase gives two glucose.", "3": "Maltose comes from starch."}'),
  ('BIO-1318', 'Human physiology', 'Small intestine', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 209 (§9.1)', 'After the action of intestinal juice, chyme becomes an alkaline emulsion called', '["chyle", "bolus", "bile", "lymph"]', 0, 'Intestinal enzymes convert chyme into an alkaline emulsion called chyle.', '{"1": "The bolus forms in the mouth.", "2": "Bile comes from the liver.", "3": "Lymph is tissue fluid in lymph vessels."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1319', 'Human physiology', 'Small intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 209 (§9.1)', 'The ileum forms about', '["the first 20–25 cm", "2.4 m next to the duodenum", "the last metre of the intestinal tract", "the last three-fifths of the small intestine, about 3.5 m"]', 3, 'The ileum is the last three-fifths (about 3.5 m) of the small intestine.', '{"0": "That is the duodenum.", "1": "That is the jejunum.", "2": "That is the large intestine."}'),
  ('BIO-1320', 'Human physiology', 'Absorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 209 (§9.1)', 'Each villus contains blood capillaries and a lymph vessel called a', '["hepatic portal vein", "thoracic duct", "lacteal", "microvillus"]', 2, 'Each villus is supplied with blood capillaries and a lymphatic vessel, the lacteal.', '{"0": "The portal vein forms from joined capillaries.", "1": "The thoracic duct receives lymph from lacteals.", "3": "Microvilli are on epithelial cells."}'),
  ('BIO-1321', 'Human physiology', 'Absorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'Glucose and amino acids absorbed in villi are carried to the liver by the', '["lacteals", "hepatic portal vein", "hepatic artery", "thoracic lymphatic duct"]', 1, 'Capillaries of villi join to form the hepatic portal vein, carrying sugars and amino acids to the liver.', '{"0": "Lacteals carry chylomicrons.", "2": "The hepatic artery brings oxygenated blood.", "3": "The thoracic duct carries lymph."}'),
  ('BIO-1322', 'Human physiology', 'Absorption', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'Fatty acids and glycerol absorbed into villus cells leave the villus as', '["free fatty acids in the portal vein", "glycogen in the liver", "bile salts in the blood", "chylomicrons that enter the lacteals"]', 3, 'Inside villus cells they recombine into triglycerides, coated with protein to form chylomicrons, which enter lacteals and then the thoracic duct.', '{"0": "They go into lymph, not the portal vein.", "1": "Glycogen is stored glucose.", "2": "Bile salts are not formed here."}'),
  ('BIO-1323', 'Human physiology', 'Absorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'Fatty acids and glycerol are absorbed into the epithelial cells of villi by', '["passive transport", "active transport only", "pinocytosis by lacteals", "osmosis"]', 0, 'The products of fat digestion are absorbed by passive transport into villus epithelial cells.', '{"1": "Active transport is named for sugars and amino acids.", "2": "Lacteals receive chylomicrons.", "3": "Osmosis moves water."}'),
  ('BIO-1324', 'Human physiology', 'Large intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'Besides water and salts, the large intestine absorbs', '["amino acids from protein digestion", "fatty acids", "vitamin K", "bile pigments"]', 2, 'The large intestine absorbs water, salts and vitamin K into the blood.', '{"0": "Amino acids from digestion are absorbed in the small intestine.", "1": "Fatty acids are absorbed in the small intestine.", "3": "Bile pigments are lost in faeces."}'),
  ('BIO-1325', 'Human physiology', 'Large intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'The inner surface of the large intestine', '["has numerous villi", "is not convoluted and has no villi", "has microvilli and lacteals", "secretes pepsin"]', 1, 'The large intestine is not convoluted and its inner surface lacks villi.', '{"0": "Villi are in the small intestine.", "2": "Lacteals are in villi.", "3": "Pepsin is from the stomach."}'),
  ('BIO-1326', 'Human physiology', 'Large intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'The vermiform appendix in humans arises from', '["the transverse colon and absorbs water", "the rectum and stores faeces", "the duodenum and secretes enzymes", "the blind end of the caecum and is vestigial"]', 3, 'A finger-like vermiform appendix arises from the caecum''s blind end and has no digestive function, so it is vestigial.', '{"0": "The colon absorbs water.", "1": "The rectum stores faeces.", "2": "The duodenum receives secretions."}'),
  ('BIO-1327', 'Human physiology', 'Large intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'Faeces contain all of the following EXCEPT', '["bacteria", "cellulose fibres", "absorbed glucose", "bile pigments"]', 2, 'Faeces contain bacteria, plant fibres such as cellulose, undigested food, sloughed mucosal cells, bile pigments and water.', '{"0": "Bacteria make up a large part.", "1": "Indigestible fibres are present.", "3": "Bile pigments are present."}'),
  ('BIO-1328', 'Human physiology', 'Large intestine', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 211 (§9.1)', 'Bacteria such as E. coli in the colon produce', '["vitamin K, needed for blood clotting", "bile salts", "insulin", "pepsin"]', 0, 'Colon bacteria produce amino acids and vitamin K, which is needed for blood coagulation.', '{"1": "Bile is made by the liver.", "2": "Insulin comes from the pancreas.", "3": "Pepsin is a gastric enzyme."}'),
  ('BIO-1329', 'Human physiology', 'Egestion', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'The internal and external anal sphincters are made of', '["striated and smooth muscle respectively", "smooth muscle both", "smooth muscle and striated muscle respectively", "cartilage both"]', 2, 'The internal sphincter is smooth muscle and the outer one is striated muscle.', '{"0": "This reverses them.", "1": "The outer one is striated.", "3": "Sphincters are muscle."}'),
  ('BIO-1330', 'Human physiology', 'Egestion', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 211 (§9.1)', 'Infants cannot control defecation voluntarily because', '["their rectum cannot store faeces", "the higher brain centres for voluntary control are not yet developed", "they lack an internal anal sphincter", "their spinal cord is not developed"]', 1, 'In infants egestion is a spinal reflex; voluntary control of the external sphincter develops as the cerebral cortex matures.', '{"0": "The rectum does store faeces.", "2": "Both sphincters are present.", "3": "The reflex is mediated by the spinal cord."}'),
  ('BIO-1331', 'Human physiology', 'Accessory organs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 211 (§9.1)', 'The gallbladder', '["produces bile", "secretes insulin", "makes pancreatic juice", "stores and concentrates bile made by the liver"]', 3, 'The liver produces bile; the gallbladder stores and concentrates it and releases it when food enters the small intestine.', '{"0": "The liver produces bile.", "1": "The pancreas secretes insulin.", "2": "The pancreas makes pancreatic juice."}'),
  ('BIO-1332', 'Human physiology', 'Accessory organs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 211 (§9.1)', 'The endocrine portion of the pancreas secretes', '["insulin and glucagon", "trypsin and lipase", "bile", "secretin"]', 0, 'The ductless (endocrine) pancreas secretes insulin and glucagon into extracellular fluid; the exocrine part makes pancreatic juice.', '{"1": "These are exocrine enzymes.", "2": "Bile is from the liver.", "3": "Secretin is from the duodenal wall."}'),
  ('BIO-1333', 'Human physiology', 'Accessory organs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 211 (§9.1)', 'The pancreatic duct joins which duct before entering the duodenum?', '["The thoracic duct", "The common bile duct", "The cystic duct only", "The hepatic portal vein"]', 1, 'The pancreatic duct joins the common bile duct from the liver and enters the duodenum.', '{"0": "The thoracic duct carries lymph.", "2": "The cystic duct joins the hepatic duct to form the common bile duct.", "3": "A vein is not a duct."}'),
  ('BIO-1334', 'Human physiology', 'Hormonal control', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 212 (§9.1)', 'Secretin is released from the duodenal wall in response to', '["fats in chyme only", "proteins in the stomach", "the sight of food", "the acidity of chyme"]', 3, 'Acid chyme entering the duodenum stimulates release of secretin.', '{"0": "Partly digested proteins and fats stimulate CCK.", "1": "Proteins in the stomach stimulate gastrin.", "2": "The sight of food acts through the medulla."}'),
  ('BIO-1335', 'Human physiology', 'Hormonal control', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 212 (§9.1)', 'Cholecystokinin (CCK) is released from the duodenum in response to', '["low blood glucose", "distension of the oesophagus", "partially digested proteins and fats in chyme", "acid in the mouth"]', 2, 'Partially digested proteins and fats stimulate the duodenal wall to secrete CCK; both CCK and secretin stimulate pancreas and gallbladder.', '{"0": "Low glucose causes hunger contractions.", "1": "Not the trigger.", "3": "Not the trigger."}'),
  ('BIO-1336', 'Human physiology', 'Liver', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 212 (§9.1)', 'The liver removes harmful ammonia by converting it into', '["urea", "uric acid", "glycogen", "bile salts"]', 0, 'The liver converts amino acids and removes ammonia by turning it into urea, excreted in urine.', '{"1": "The book names urea.", "2": "Glycogen is stored glucose.", "3": "Bile salts are made from cholesterol, not ammonia."}'),
  ('BIO-1337', 'Human physiology', 'Liver', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 212 (§9.1)', 'Which group of vitamins does the book list as stored in the liver?', '["C and B1 only", "C, E and folic acid only", "A, D, B12 and K", "None, since vitamins are not stored"]', 2, 'The liver stores glucose as glycogen, vitamins A, D, B12 and K, minerals like iron and copper, and fats.', '{"0": "Not the vitamins listed.", "1": "Not the vitamins listed.", "3": "The liver stores several vitamins."}'),
  ('BIO-1338', 'Human physiology', 'Digestive disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 206 (§9.1)', 'Backflow of acidic chyme from the stomach into the oesophagus causes', '["jaundice", "heartburn (pyrosis)", "appendicitis", "hunger pangs"]', 1, 'Acid chyme flowing back into the oesophagus causes a painful burning sensation in the chest, called pyrosis or heartburn.', '{"0": "Jaundice is due to bile pigments in blood.", "2": "Appendicitis is inflammation of the appendix.", "3": "Hunger pangs are due to hunger contractions."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1339', 'Human physiology', 'Digestive disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 208 (§9.1)', 'Jaundice develops when', '["bile pigments are prevented from leaving the digestive tract and accumulate in blood", "acid flows back into the oesophagus", "the appendix becomes infected", "vitamin K is not absorbed"]', 0, 'If bile pigments cannot leave via the digestive tract, they accumulate in the blood, causing jaundice.', '{"1": "That causes heartburn.", "2": "That is appendicitis.", "3": "Not the cause given."}'),
  ('BIO-1340', 'Human physiology', 'Digestive disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 211 (§9.1)', 'Gallstones form when', '["bile salts are absorbed in the colon", "pancreatic juice becomes acidic", "chylomicrons block lacteals", "cholesterol secreted by the liver precipitates in the gallbladder"]', 3, 'Cholesterol secreted by the liver may precipitate in the gallbladder to form gallstones, which may block the bile duct.', '{"0": "Not the cause given.", "1": "Not the cause given.", "2": "Not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1341', 'Human physiology', 'Digestive disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Human Digestive System, p. 210 (§9.1)', 'The surgical removal of an inflamed appendix is called', '["cholecystectomy", "gastrectomy", "colostomy", "appendicectomy"]', 3, 'Inflammation of the appendix is appendicitis; its surgical removal is called appendicectomy.', '{"0": "That is removal of the gallbladder.", "1": "That is removal of the stomach.", "2": "That is an opening of the colon."}'),
  ('BIO-1342', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory surface', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 215 (Intro)', 'External respiration is best defined as', '["breakdown of glucose in mitochondria", "uptake of oxygen from and disposal of carbon dioxide to the environment at body-system level", "transport of oxygen inside cells", "production of ATP by glycolysis"]', 1, 'External respiration is the uptake of O2 from, and disposal of CO2 into, the environment at the body-system level, including breathing and capillary gas exchange.', '{"0": "That is cellular respiration.", "2": "That is not external respiration.", "3": "Glycolysis is part of cellular respiration."}'),
  ('BIO-1343', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory surface', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 216 (§10.1)', 'A respiratory surface is located internally mainly so that', '["it receives more light", "gases travel a greater distance", "its moist surface does not lose water to the atmosphere", "it stays dry"]', 2, 'An internal location prevents the moist respiratory surface from losing water to the air.', '{"0": "Light is irrelevant.", "1": "Surfaces are thin to shorten distance.", "3": "It must stay moist."}'),
  ('BIO-1344', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory surface', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 216 (§10.1)', 'Air reaches the respiratory surface through a branched tubular airway so that it', '["becomes saturated with water vapour", "loses all its oxygen", "becomes colder", "moves faster"]', 0, 'Passing through a branched airway saturates the air with water vapour before it reaches the surface.', '{"1": "Oxygen is kept for exchange.", "2": "Air is warmed, not cooled.", "3": "Not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-1345', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory surface', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 216 (§10.1)', 'Gas exchange between air and blood occurs by', '["active transport", "osmosis", "bulk flow through the alveolar wall", "diffusion"]', 3, 'Gaseous exchange at the respiratory surface occurs through diffusion.', '{"0": "No active transport of O2 or CO2 is described.", "1": "Osmosis moves water.", "2": "Gases diffuse across the wall."}'),
  ('BIO-1346', 'Human physiology', 'Upper respiratory tract', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 216 (§10.1)', 'The upper respiratory tract consists of the', '["trachea, bronchi and lungs", "nasal cavity, trachea and bronchi", "larynx, trachea and alveoli", "nasal cavity, pharynx and larynx"]', 3, 'The upper respiratory tract is the nasal cavity, pharynx and larynx; no gas exchange occurs there.', '{"0": "These are in the lower tract.", "1": "The trachea and bronchi are lower tract.", "2": "The trachea and alveoli are lower tract."}'),
  ('BIO-1347', 'Human physiology', 'Upper respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 216 (§10.1)', 'Inhaled air is warmed and humidified in the nasal cavity by', '["the vocal cords", "blood vessels lying close to its surface", "surfactant", "cartilage rings"]', 1, 'Air flowing through the nasal cavity is warmed and humidified by blood vessels close to its surface; hairs and mucus trap particles.', '{"0": "Vocal cords produce sound.", "2": "Surfactant is in alveoli.", "3": "Cartilage rings support the trachea."}'),
  ('BIO-1348', 'Human physiology', 'Upper respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 216 (§10.1)', 'Besides conducting air, the nasal cavity contains', '["alveoli for gas exchange", "taste buds only", "chemoreceptors for the sense of smell", "vocal cords"]', 2, 'The nasal cavity contains chemoreceptors for smell and contributes to taste.', '{"0": "Gas exchange is only in the lungs.", "1": "Taste buds are on the tongue.", "3": "Vocal cords are in the larynx."}'),
  ('BIO-1349', 'Human physiology', 'Upper respiratory tract', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 217 (§10.1)', 'The larynx is called the voice box because it contains', '["vocal cords", "the epiglottis only", "C-shaped cartilages", "alveoli"]', 0, 'The larynx contains the vocal cords, which vibrate as air flows over them to produce sound.', '{"1": "The epiglottis guards the glottis.", "2": "C-shaped cartilages are in the trachea.", "3": "Alveoli are in the lungs."}'),
  ('BIO-1350', 'Human physiology', 'Upper respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 217 (§10.1)', 'The opening of the larynx is called the', '["epiglottis", "glottis", "pharynx", "bronchus"]', 1, 'The epiglottis is a cartilaginous flap above the opening of the larynx, called the glottis.', '{"0": "The epiglottis is the flap covering it.", "2": "The pharynx is the shared passage.", "3": "Bronchi branch from the trachea."}'),
  ('BIO-1351', 'Human physiology', 'Upper respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 217 (§10.1)', 'If swallowed material starts to enter the larynx, it is usually expelled by', '["a strong cough reflex", "peristalsis", "sneezing through the mouth", "surfactant"]', 0, 'Material entering the larynx irritates it and stimulates a strong cough reflex that expels it.', '{"1": "Peristalsis is in the gut.", "2": "The book describes a cough reflex.", "3": "Surfactant prevents alveolar collapse."}'),
  ('BIO-1352', 'Human physiology', 'Lower respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'The trachea is kept open by', '["complete bony rings", "cartilage plates only", "alveolar surfactant", "C-shaped rings of cartilage"]', 3, 'The trachea has smooth muscle and C-shaped cartilage rings that give strength and keep the passage open.', '{"0": "The rings are cartilage, C-shaped.", "1": "Plates appear in secondary bronchi.", "2": "Surfactant acts in alveoli."}'),
  ('BIO-1353', 'Human physiology', 'Lower respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'Cilia lining the trachea move mucus and trapped particles', '["towards the alveoli", "into the oesophagus directly", "towards the pharynx", "into the blood"]', 2, 'Cilia propel trapped particles in mucus towards the pharynx.', '{"0": "That would carry dirt into the lungs.", "1": "They move it up to the pharynx.", "3": "Not into the blood."}'),
  ('BIO-1354', 'Human physiology', 'Lower respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'Which statement about the human trachea is correct?', '["It is the narrowest airway", "It is the widest airway, about 1 inch wide and 4–6 inches long", "It has no cartilage", "Gas exchange occurs in its wall"]', 1, 'The trachea is the widest passageway, about 1 inch wide and 4–6 inches long.', '{"0": "It is the widest.", "2": "It has C-shaped cartilage.", "3": "Gas exchange occurs only in alveoli."}'),
  ('BIO-1355', 'Human physiology', 'Lower respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'How many secondary bronchi does the left lung have?', '["Two", "Three", "One", "Five"]', 0, 'There are two secondary bronchi in the left lung and three in the right.', '{"1": "Three is the right lung.", "2": "Too few.", "3": "Too many."}'),
  ('BIO-1356', 'Human physiology', 'Lower respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'In secondary bronchi the C-shaped cartilages are replaced by', '["complete rings", "bone", "no support at all", "cartilage plates"]', 3, 'In secondary bronchi C-shaped cartilages are replaced with cartilage plates; bronchioles lack them.', '{"0": "Not described.", "1": "No bone in airways.", "2": "Bronchioles lack cartilage plates."}'),
  ('BIO-1357', 'Human physiology', 'Lower respiratory tract', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'Which airways lack cartilage plates?', '["Primary bronchi", "Secondary bronchi", "Bronchioles", "Tertiary bronchi"]', 2, 'Bronchioles do not have cartilage plates.', '{"0": "Primary bronchi have cartilage.", "1": "Secondary bronchi have plates.", "3": "Bronchioles arise from tertiary bronchi, which are bronchi."}'),
  ('BIO-1358', 'Human physiology', 'Lower respiratory tract', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'The correct order of air passages from the trachea is', '["bronchioles → primary bronchi → alveoli", "primary bronchi → bronchioles → secondary bronchi → alveoli", "primary bronchi → secondary bronchi → tertiary bronchi → bronchioles → alveolar ducts → alveoli", "alveolar ducts → bronchioles → alveoli"]', 2, 'Bronchi branch into secondary and tertiary bronchi, then bronchioles and terminal bronchioles, which end in alveolar ducts and alveoli.', '{"0": "Bronchi come before bronchioles.", "1": "Secondary bronchi come before bronchioles.", "3": "Bronchioles come before alveolar ducts."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1359', 'Human physiology', 'Lungs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 218 (§10.1)', 'The pleural fluid between the visceral and parietal pleura', '["carries out gas exchange", "stores oxygen", "produces surfactant", "allows the lungs to expand and contract freely"]', 3, 'Fluid in the pleural cavity allows the lungs to expand and contract freely during breathing.', '{"0": "Gas exchange is in alveoli.", "1": "Myoglobin stores oxygen in muscle.", "2": "Alveolar cells secrete surfactant."}'),
  ('BIO-1360', 'Human physiology', 'Lungs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 219 (§10.1)', 'The left lung is smaller with two lobes because', '["it leaves room for the heart", "it has no bronchus", "it receives less blood", "the diaphragm is higher on the left"]', 0, 'The right lung has three lobes; the smaller left lung with two lobes leaves room for the heart.', '{"1": "It has a primary bronchus.", "2": "Not the reason given.", "3": "Not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-1361', 'Human physiology', 'Lungs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 219 (§10.1)', 'Surfactant secreted by alveolar epithelial cells', '["digests trapped dust", "prevents alveoli from collapsing and sticking together", "carries oxygen in blood", "thickens the alveolar wall"]', 1, 'Surfactant reduces surface tension, preventing alveoli from collapsing and sticking together as air leaves.', '{"0": "Not its role.", "2": "Haemoglobin carries oxygen.", "3": "Thin walls favour diffusion."}'),
  ('BIO-1362', 'Human physiology', 'Lungs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 219 (§10.1)', 'The epithelium of an alveolus is about', '["1 mm thick", "10 micrometres thick", "1 cm thick", "0.1 micrometre thick"]', 3, 'An alveolus is made of moist epithelium only about 0.1 micrometre thick.', '{"0": "Far too thick.", "1": "Too thick.", "2": "Far too thick."}'),
  ('BIO-1363', 'Human physiology', 'Lungs', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 220 (§10.1)', 'An adult human pair of lungs contains approximately', '["480 thousand alveoli", "480 million alveoli", "4.8 billion alveoli", "48 million alveoli"]', 1, 'Lungs contain about 480 million alveoli (range 274–790 million) per pair in adults.', '{"0": "Far too few.", "2": "Too many.", "3": "Too few."}'),
  ('BIO-1364', 'Human physiology', 'Breathing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 220 (§10.1)', 'The two sets of muscles that ventilate the lungs are the', '["diaphragm and abdominal muscles only", "vocal cords and epiglottis", "diaphragm and intercostal muscles", "smooth muscles of bronchioles"]', 2, 'Breathing is produced by the diaphragm and the intercostal muscles between the ribs.', '{"0": "The book names intercostals.", "1": "These are laryngeal structures.", "3": "These do not ventilate the lungs."}'),
  ('BIO-1365', 'Human physiology', 'Breathing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 220 (§10.1)', 'During inspiration, the intercostal muscles', '["contract, raising the ribs and expanding the rib cage", "relax, lowering the ribs", "relax while the diaphragm rises", "do not take part"]', 0, 'In inspiration the intercostals contract, raising the ribs, while the diaphragm contracts and flattens.', '{"1": "Relaxation lowers the ribs in expiration.", "2": "That describes expiration.", "3": "They do take part."}'),
  ('BIO-1366', 'Human physiology', 'Breathing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 220 (§10.1)', 'The lungs expand with the thorax during inspiration because', '["the visceral and parietal pleura adhere to each other", "lung tissue contracts actively", "air is pumped in by the trachea", "surfactant pulls the lungs outward"]', 0, 'The lungs expand because the visceral and parietal pleural membranes stick together, so lungs follow the thorax.', '{"1": "Lungs do not draw air in themselves.", "2": "The trachea conducts air.", "3": "Not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-1367', 'Human physiology', 'Breathing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 220 (§10.1)', 'Breathing is harder at high altitudes because', '["oxygen percentage in air is zero", "air pressure is higher", "the diaphragm cannot contract", "atmospheric pressure is lower, so a larger thoracic expansion is needed"]', 3, 'At altitude atmospheric pressure is lower, so the thorax must expand more to make lung pressure lower than the outside.', '{"0": "Oxygen is present.", "1": "Pressure is lower, not higher.", "2": "The diaphragm works normally."}'),
  ('BIO-1368', 'Human physiology', 'Breathing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 220 (§10.1)', 'In birds, gas exchange is very efficient because', '["air flows in and out of the same sac", "air flows in one direction, opposite to blood flow", "birds have no lungs", "birds have only one air sac"]', 1, 'Birds have lungs and air sacs with one-way air flow opposite to blood flow, making exchange more efficient.', '{"0": "Flow is one-way.", "2": "Birds have lungs.", "3": "Birds have anterior and posterior air sacs."}'),
  ('BIO-1369', 'Human physiology', 'Breathing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 221 (§10.1)', 'Each breath is initiated by neurons in the respiratory centre of the', '["cerebrum", "cerebellum", "medulla oblongata", "spinal cord only"]', 2, 'Neurons in the medulla oblongata (brain stem) send impulses to the diaphragm and intercostals to cause inspiration.', '{"0": "Not the respiratory centre.", "1": "Coordinates movement.", "3": "The centre is in the brain stem."}'),
  ('BIO-1370', 'Human physiology', 'Breathing', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 220 (§10.1)', 'Expiration at rest occurs mainly because', '["the diaphragm contracts strongly", "the diaphragm and intercostals relax and the stretched lungs recoil", "the ribs rise", "the alveoli pump air out"]', 1, 'Relaxation of the diaphragm and intercostals relieves elastic tension; the lungs recoil and pressure rises above atmospheric.', '{"0": "Diaphragm contraction causes inspiration.", "2": "Ribs lower in expiration.", "3": "Alveoli do not pump."}'),
  ('BIO-1371', 'Human physiology', 'Oxygen transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 221 (§10.2)', 'Whole blood carries far more oxygen than plasma alone because', '["most oxygen is bound to haemoglobin in red blood cells", "plasma has extra oxygen pumps", "oxygen dissolves well in water", "white cells carry oxygen"]', 0, 'Plasma dissolves only a small amount of O2, but whole blood carries almost 200 mL O2 per litre because most O2 is bound to haemoglobin.', '{"1": "Not described.", "2": "Oxygen dissolves poorly.", "3": "Red cells carry it."}'),
  ('BIO-1372', 'Human physiology', 'Oxygen transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'At an alveolar oxygen partial pressure of about 105 mm Hg, haemoglobin is about', '["35% saturated", "50% saturated", "75% saturated", "97% saturated"]', 3, 'About 97% of haemoglobin in alveolar capillaries combines with oxygen to form oxyhaemoglobin.', '{"0": "35% is venous blood during exercise.", "1": "Not a figure given.", "2": "75% is venous blood at rest."}'),
  ('BIO-1373', 'Human physiology', 'Oxygen transport', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'At rest, venous blood (PO2 40 mm Hg) is 75% saturated. The percentage of oxyhaemoglobin that unloaded its oxygen in the tissues is', '["22%", "62%", "75%", "97%"]', 0, '97% − 75% = 22% of the oxyhaemoglobin gave up its oxygen; 75% remains as reserve.', '{"1": "62% is unloaded during exercise.", "2": "75% is what remains bound.", "3": "97% is arterial saturation."}'),
  ('BIO-1374', 'Human physiology', 'Oxygen transport', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'During exercise, venous PO2 falls to 20 mm Hg and saturation to 35%. The oxygen unloaded is', '["22%", "35%", "62%", "75%"]', 2, '97% − 35% = 62% unloaded during exercise, compared with 22% at rest.', '{"0": "22% is unloaded at rest.", "1": "35% is what remains bound.", "3": "75% is resting venous saturation."}'),
  ('BIO-1375', 'Human physiology', 'Oxygen transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'The reserve of oxyhaemoglobin in venous blood is enough to keep a person alive for about', '["one hour", "ten seconds", "four to five minutes if breathing stops", "one day"]', 2, 'The oxygen reserve maintains life for four to five minutes if breathing or the heart stops.', '{"0": "Too long.", "1": "Too short.", "3": "Far too long."}'),
  ('BIO-1376', 'Human physiology', 'Oxygen transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'The Bohr effect is the', '["increase in affinity when pH falls", "binding of CO to haemoglobin", "movement of chloride into red cells", "decrease in haemoglobin''s affinity for oxygen when blood pH falls"]', 3, 'CO2 from tissues lowers blood pH, reducing haemoglobin''s oxygen affinity so it releases oxygen more readily.', '{"0": "Affinity falls, not rises.", "1": "That is CO poisoning.", "2": "That is the chloride shift."}'),
  ('BIO-1377', 'Human physiology', 'Oxygen transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'During exercise, haemoglobin unloads more oxygen because', '["muscle produces more CO2 and heat", "blood pH rises", "body temperature falls", "haemoglobin''s affinity increases"]', 0, 'More CO2 (lower pH) and higher temperature reduce haemoglobin''s affinity, so more oxygen is released.', '{"1": "pH falls in active tissue.", "2": "Temperature rises.", "3": "Affinity decreases."}'),
  ('BIO-1378', 'Human physiology', 'CO2 transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'Carbon dioxide moves from tissues into blood because', '["it is actively pumped", "its partial pressure is lower in tissues", "haemoglobin pulls it in", "its partial pressure is higher in tissues than in blood"]', 3, 'PCO2 is higher in tissues than blood, so CO2 diffuses into blood; the reverse happens in the lungs.', '{"0": "Movement is by diffusion.", "1": "It is higher in tissues.", "2": "Not the cause."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1379', 'Human physiology', 'CO2 transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 222 (§10.2)', 'Inside red blood cells, CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid in the presence of', '["pepsin", "carbonic anhydrase", "rubisco", "trypsin"]', 1, 'Carbonic anhydrase in RBCs catalyses CO2 + H2O → H2CO3.', '{"0": "Pepsin is a stomach protease.", "2": "Rubisco fixes CO2 in plants.", "3": "Trypsin is a pancreatic protease."}'),
  ('BIO-1380', 'Human physiology', 'CO2 transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 223 (§10.2)', 'The exchange of a chloride ion for a bicarbonate ion across the red cell membrane is called the', '["Bohr effect", "Z-scheme", "chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon)", "Haldane reflex"]', 2, 'Bicarbonate leaves the RBC in exchange for chloride via a transporter; this is the chloride shift or Hamburger phenomenon.', '{"0": "The Bohr effect concerns pH and O2 affinity.", "1": "The Z-scheme is in photosynthesis.", "3": "Not described in the book."}'),
  ('BIO-1381', 'Human physiology', 'CO2 transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 223 (§10.2)', 'About what percentage of CO2 is carried bound to haemoglobin?', '["8%", "20%", "72%", "97%"]', 1, 'About 72% travels as bicarbonate, 20% bound to haemoglobin and 8% dissolved in plasma.', '{"0": "8% is dissolved.", "2": "72% is carried as bicarbonate.", "3": "97% is arterial O2 saturation."}'),
  ('BIO-1382', 'Human physiology', 'CO2 transport', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 223 (§10.2)', 'CO2 and O2 do not compete for binding to haemoglobin because', '["CO2 binds the globin protein while O2 binds the iron of haem", "both bind iron at different times", "CO2 binds only in plasma", "O2 binds the globin chains"]', 0, 'CO2 binds the protein portion of haemoglobin while O2 binds the iron ions, so they do not compete.', '{"1": "CO2 does not bind iron.", "2": "Some CO2 binds haemoglobin.", "3": "O2 binds iron."}'),
  ('BIO-1383', 'Human physiology', 'CO2 transport', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 223 (§10.2)', 'Carbon monoxide poisoning causes tissue hypoxia because CO', '["destroys red blood cells", "blocks the trachea", "binds haemoglobin with much higher affinity than oxygen", "dissolves in plasma as bicarbonate"]', 2, 'CO binds haemoglobin much more strongly than O2, reducing haemoglobin available for O2 transport.', '{"0": "Not the mechanism given.", "1": "It is inhaled through open airways.", "3": "Not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1384', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 224 (§10.3)', 'A haemoglobin molecule consists of', '["one polypeptide chain and one haem group", "four alpha chains and two haem groups", "two chains and two haem groups", "two alpha and two beta globin chains with four haem groups"]', 3, 'Haemoglobin has four globin chains (two alpha, two beta), each with a haem group.', '{"0": "That describes myoglobin.", "1": "There are two alpha and two beta chains, four haems.", "2": "There are four chains."}'),
  ('BIO-1385', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 224 (§10.3)', 'The alpha and beta chains of haemoglobin contain respectively', '["146 and 141 amino acids", "141 and 146 amino acids", "153 and 141 amino acids", "141 and 153 amino acids"]', 1, 'Alpha chains have 141 and beta chains 146 amino acids; myoglobin has 153.', '{"0": "Reversed.", "2": "153 belongs to myoglobin.", "3": "153 belongs to myoglobin."}'),
  ('BIO-1386', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 224 (§10.3)', 'The haem group of haemoglobin is an iron ion held in a', '["porphyrin ring", "pyrrole chain without a ring", "magnesium complex", "hydrocarbon tail"]', 0, 'A haem group consists of an iron ion held in a porphyrin ring.', '{"1": "Pyrroles form the porphyrin ring.", "2": "Magnesium is in chlorophyll.", "3": "The hydrocarbon tail belongs to chlorophyll."}'),
  ('BIO-1387', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 224 (§10.3)', 'Myoglobin serves as an oxygen store in muscle because it', '["carries four O2 molecules", "is found in red blood cells", "releases O2 at high partial pressure", "has a higher affinity for oxygen than haemoglobin"]', 3, 'Myoglobin binds O2 more tightly than haemoglobin and releases it only when PO2 falls below about 20 mm Hg.', '{"0": "It binds one O2.", "1": "It is in skeletal and cardiac muscle.", "2": "It releases O2 when PO2 is low."}'),
  ('BIO-1388', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory pigments', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 224 (§10.3)', 'Myoglobin releases its oxygen when the partial pressure of oxygen falls below about', '["20 mm Hg", "40 mm Hg", "80 mm Hg", "105 mm Hg"]', 0, 'Myoglobin releases oxygen when PO2 is below 20 mm Hg, supplying muscle when it needs it.', '{"1": "40 mm Hg is resting venous PO2.", "2": "Too high.", "3": "105 mm Hg is alveolar PO2."}'),
  ('BIO-1389', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory infections', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 225 (§10.4)', 'Sinusitis lasting longer than 12 weeks is described as', '["acute", "self-limiting", "chronic", "congenital"]', 2, 'Acute sinusitis lasts 7 to 10 days; chronic sinusitis lasts longer than 12 weeks.', '{"0": "Acute lasts 7–10 days.", "1": "Not the term for duration.", "3": "Not the term used."}'),
  ('BIO-1390', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory infections', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 225 (§10.4)', 'Most cases of sinusitis are caused by', '["bacteria", "viruses", "fungi", "protozoa"]', 1, 'Most cases are viral and resolve without antibiotics; some are bacterial and rarely fungal.', '{"0": "Bacteria cause some cases.", "2": "Fungi cause rare cases.", "3": "Not mentioned."}'),
  ('BIO-1391', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory infections', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 226 (§10.4)', 'The common cause of otitis media is', '["fluid accumulating in the Eustachian tube that cannot drain from the middle ear", "inflammation of the alveoli", "damage to vocal cords", "Mycobacterium in the lungs"]', 0, 'Undrained fluid in the Eustachian tube allows bacteria and viruses to grow in the middle ear.', '{"1": "That is pneumonia.", "2": "Not related.", "3": "That causes TB."}'),
  ('BIO-1392', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory infections', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 226 (§10.4)', 'In pneumonia, the alveoli of one or both lungs are', '["ruptured into large air spaces", "inflamed and filled with pus and fluid", "replaced by cartilage", "lined with extra surfactant"]', 1, 'Pneumonia inflames the alveoli and fills them with pus and fluid, making breathing painful.', '{"0": "Rupture into large spaces is emphysema.", "2": "Not described.", "3": "Not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1393', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory infections', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 227 (§10.4)', 'Which bacterium is named as a particular cause of pneumonia?', '["Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "Escherichia coli", "Vibrio cholerae", "Streptococcus pneumoniae"]', 3, 'Pneumonia is caused mainly by bacteria, particularly Streptococcus pneumoniae, or by viruses such as human rhinovirus.', '{"0": "This causes TB.", "1": "E. coli lives in the colon.", "2": "Not named."}'),
  ('BIO-1394', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory infections', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 227 (§10.4)', 'Damage to alveoli in advanced pulmonary tuberculosis can produce', '["extra alveoli", "a third lung", "cavities in lung tissue and respiratory failure", "thicker cartilage rings"]', 2, 'TB damages alveoli, forming small cavities, and advanced disease can cause respiratory failure.', '{"0": "Alveoli are destroyed.", "1": "Not possible.", "3": "Not described."}'),
  ('BIO-1395', 'Human physiology', 'Respiratory infections', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 227 (§10.4)', 'A typical symptom of pulmonary tuberculosis is', '["loss of the sense of smell", "intermittent fever, usually in the evening, with night sweats", "severe ear pain", "pain worsening on bending over"]', 1, 'TB causes cough with blood, evening fever, night sweats, weight loss and weakness.', '{"0": "Loss of smell is a sinusitis symptom.", "2": "Ear pain is otitis media.", "3": "This is a sinusitis headache."}'),
  ('BIO-1396', 'Human physiology', 'COPD', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 228 (§10.4)', 'A genetic cause of COPD and emphysema even in non-smokers is deficiency of', '["alpha-1 antitrypsin", "haemoglobin", "surfactant", "carbonic anhydrase"]', 0, 'A small percentage develop COPD or emphysema from genetic alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency.', '{"1": "Not the cause named.", "2": "Not the cause named.", "3": "Not the cause named."}'),
  ('BIO-1397', 'Human physiology', 'COPD', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 228 (§10.4)', 'Chronic bronchitis involves', '["rupture of alveolar walls", "infection of the middle ear", "inflammation and narrowing of the bronchial tubes with increased mucus", "pus-filled alveoli"]', 2, 'Chronic bronchitis, a type of COPD, inflames and narrows bronchial tubes and increases mucus production.', '{"0": "That is emphysema.", "1": "That is otitis media.", "3": "That is pneumonia."}'),
  ('BIO-1398', 'Human physiology', 'COPD', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 228 (§10.4)', 'The primary cause of emphysema is', '["viral infection", "fluid in the Eustachian tube", "low altitude", "smoking"]', 3, 'Smoking is the primary cause; air pollution, dust, fumes and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency can also cause it.', '{"0": "Not the primary cause.", "1": "That causes otitis media.", "2": "Not a cause."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-1399', 'Human physiology', 'COPD', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Human Respiratory System, p. 229 (§10.4)', 'The most important step in managing emphysema is', '["taking antibiotics for nine months", "nasal surgery", "quitting smoking", "removing the appendix"]', 2, 'Quitting smoking is the most important step; bronchodilators, steroids and oxygen also help.', '{"0": "Long antibiotic courses treat TB.", "1": "Nasal surgery is for chronic sinusitis.", "3": "Unrelated."}'),
  ('BIO-1400', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 232 (§11.1)', 'The heart is usually felt on the left side of the chest because', '["the heart lies entirely in the left thorax", "the right lung pushes it", "the left side of the heart is larger and stronger", "the aorta is on the left"]', 2, 'The heart is in the middle of the chest, but its left side is larger and stronger because it pumps to all body parts.', '{"0": "It lies in the middle, between the lungs.", "1": "Not the reason given.", "3": "Not the reason given."}'),
  ('BIO-1401', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 232 (§11.1)', 'The fibrous pericardium', '["lines the heart chambers", "protects the heart against shocks and prevents its excessive dilation", "produces pericardial fluid", "conducts impulses to the ventricles"]', 1, 'The outer fibrous pericardium of strong connective tissue protects the heart and prevents excessive dilation.', '{"0": "The endocardium lines the chambers.", "2": "The fluid is in the pericardial cavity of the serous layer.", "3": "Purkinje fibres conduct impulses."}'),
  ('BIO-1402', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 232 (§11.1)', 'The visceral pericardium is also called the', '["epicardium", "endocardium", "myocardium", "fibrous pericardium"]', 0, 'The inner visceral layer of serous pericardium, closely attached to the heart, is the epicardium.', '{"1": "The endocardium lines the chambers.", "2": "The myocardium is cardiac muscle.", "3": "The fibrous layer is outermost."}'),
  ('BIO-1403', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 232 (§11.1)', 'The pericardial cavity lies between the', '["fibrous and parietal pericardium", "myocardium and endocardium", "two atria", "parietal and visceral pericardium and holds up to 50 mL of fluid"]', 3, 'The space between parietal and visceral pericardium holds up to 50 mL of lubricating pericardial fluid.', '{"0": "The parietal layer lies directly beneath the fibrous layer.", "1": "These are heart wall layers.", "2": "Atria are chambers."}'),
  ('BIO-1404', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'easy', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 232 (§11.1)', 'The thickest layer of the heart wall is the', '["epicardium", "endocardium", "pericardium", "myocardium"]', 3, 'Beneath the epicardium is the thickest layer, the myocardium of cardiac muscle.', '{"0": "The epicardium is the outer lining.", "1": "The endocardium is one cell thick.", "2": "The pericardium is the sac."}'),
  ('BIO-1405', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 233 (§11.1)', 'Which septum is much thicker?', '["The interventricular septum", "The interatrial septum", "Both are equally thin", "Neither, since the heart has no septa"]', 0, 'The interventricular septum separating the ventricles is much thicker than the interatrial septum.', '{"1": "The interatrial septum is thinner.", "2": "They differ in thickness.", "3": "The heart has septa."}'),
  ('BIO-1406', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 233 (§11.1)', 'The tricuspid valve lies between the', '["left atrium and left ventricle", "right atrium and right ventricle", "right ventricle and pulmonary trunk", "left ventricle and aorta"]', 1, 'The tricuspid valve (three cusps) is between the right atrium and right ventricle.', '{"0": "The bicuspid valve is on the left.", "2": "The pulmonary semilunar valve is there.", "3": "The aortic valve is there."}'),
  ('BIO-1407', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 233 (§11.1)', 'The semilunar valves close when', '["the ventricles contract", "the atria contract", "the ventricles relax", "the SA node fires"]', 2, 'When ventricles relax, pulmonary and aortic valves close, preventing backflow from the arteries.', '{"0": "During contraction they open.", "1": "Atrial systole opens AV valves.", "3": "Not the trigger."}'),
  ('BIO-1408', 'Human physiology', 'Heart structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 234 (§11.1)', 'The wall of the left ventricle is about three times thicker than the right because it', '["receives blood from the lungs only", "holds more blood", "pumps only to the lungs", "pumps blood to the whole body"]', 3, 'The left ventricle pushes blood all over the body, so its wall is about three times thicker.', '{"0": "Receiving is the left atrium''s job.", "1": "Not the reason given.", "2": "The right ventricle pumps to the lungs."}'),
  ('BIO-1409', 'Human physiology', 'Blood flow through heart', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 234 (§11.1)', 'Deoxygenated blood from the body enters the right atrium through', '["the pulmonary veins", "the aorta", "the superior and inferior venae cavae", "the coronary arteries"]', 2, 'The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood through the superior and inferior venae cavae.', '{"0": "Pulmonary veins enter the left atrium.", "1": "The aorta leaves the left ventricle.", "3": "Coronary arteries supply the heart wall."}'),
  ('BIO-1410', 'Human physiology', 'Blood flow through heart', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 234 (§11.1)', 'The correct path of blood from the right ventricle back to the left atrium is', '["aortic valve → aorta → lungs → venae cavae", "pulmonary valve → pulmonary trunk → pulmonary arteries → lungs → pulmonary veins", "tricuspid valve → pulmonary veins → lungs → pulmonary arteries", "pulmonary veins → lungs → pulmonary trunk"]', 1, 'Right ventricular blood passes the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk and arteries to the lungs, returning by pulmonary veins to the left atrium.', '{"0": "The aorta carries blood from the left ventricle.", "2": "Arteries carry blood to the lungs, veins away.", "3": "The order is reversed."}'),
  ('BIO-1411', 'Human physiology', 'Cardiac cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 235 (§11.1)', 'In one heartbeat, the durations of diastole, atrial systole and ventricular systole are about', '["0.4 s, 0.1 s and 0.3 s", "0.1 s, 0.4 s and 0.3 s", "0.3 s, 0.1 s and 0.4 s", "0.4 s, 0.3 s and 0.1 s"]', 0, 'Diastole lasts about 0.4 s, atrial systole about 0.1 s and ventricular systole about 0.3 s.', '{"1": "Diastole is the longest phase.", "2": "Ventricular systole is 0.3 s.", "3": "Atrial systole is the shortest."}'),
  ('BIO-1412', 'Human physiology', 'Cardiac cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 235 (§11.1)', 'During ventricular systole,', '["the AV valves open and the semilunar valves close", "all valves are open", "the AV valves close and the semilunar valves open", "all valves are closed throughout"]', 2, 'When ventricles contract, tricuspid and bicuspid valves close while pulmonary and aortic valves open.', '{"0": "This happens in diastole.", "1": "Not during systole.", "3": "Semilunar valves open to eject blood."}'),
  ('BIO-1413', 'Human physiology', 'Cardiac cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 235 (§11.1)', 'Over a lifetime, the human heart beats about', '["2.5 billion times", "2.5 million times", "250 million times", "25 billion times"]', 0, 'The heart beats about 2.5 billion times in one''s life without stopping.', '{"1": "Far too few.", "2": "Too few.", "3": "Too many."}'),
  ('BIO-1414', 'Human physiology', 'Cardiac cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 235 (§11.1)', 'The ''dubb'' sound of the heartbeat is produced by', '["closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves", "contraction of the atria", "opening of the AV valves", "closure of the pulmonary and aortic semilunar valves"]', 3, 'When ventricular systole ends, the semilunar valves close and the ''dubb'' sound is made.', '{"0": "That makes the ''lubb'' sound.", "1": "Atrial contraction is not the sound described.", "2": "Opening is silent."}'),
  ('BIO-1415', 'Human physiology', 'Cardiac cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 236 (§11.1)', 'A heart murmur is', '["the normal lubb-dubb sound", "a hissing sound caused by turbulence when valves do not close fully or open narrowly", "a block in the SA node", "rupture of a blood vessel"]', 1, 'Turbulence from leaky or narrowed valves is heard as a hiss called a murmur.', '{"0": "That is normal.", "2": "That affects rhythm, not valves.", "3": "That is haemorrhage."}'),
  ('BIO-1416', 'Human physiology', 'Control of heartbeat', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 236 (§11.1)', 'The SA node is located in the', '["lower part of the interatrial septum", "interventricular septum", "wall of the left ventricle", "upper wall of the right atrium"]', 3, 'The SA node (pacemaker), a small cluster of cardiac muscle cells, is in the upper wall of the right atrium.', '{"0": "That is the AV node.", "1": "That is the bundle of His.", "2": "Purkinje fibres are in ventricular walls."}'),
  ('BIO-1417', 'Human physiology', 'Control of heartbeat', 'moderate', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 236 (§11.1)', 'The AV node lies in the', '["upper wall of the right atrium", "apex of the left ventricle", "lower portion of the interatrial septum", "aortic arch"]', 2, 'The AV node, also a cluster of cardiac muscle cells, lies at the lower interatrial septum.', '{"0": "That is the SA node.", "1": "Not its location.", "3": "Baroreceptors are in the aortic arch."}'),
  ('BIO-1418', 'Human physiology', 'Control of heartbeat', 'hard', 'Biology 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Human Circulatory System, p. 236 (§11.1)', 'The order of conduction of the cardiac impulse is', '["AV node → SA node → Purkinje fibres → bundle of His", "SA node → AV node → bundle of His → Purkinje fibres", "SA node → Purkinje fibres → AV node → bundle of His", "bundle of His → SA node → AV node → Purkinje fibres"]', 1, 'Impulses go from the SA node to the AV node, then the AV bundle (bundle of His) in the septum, and its branches end in Purkinje fibres.', '{"0": "The SA node starts the beat.", "2": "The AV node comes before the ventricles.", "3": "The SA node starts the beat."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

